.TH KARIDNS.CONF 5 "2026-11-14" "KariDNS" "KariDNS Manual"
.\" Generated from docs/karidns.md by man/md2man.pl (make man): edit the Markdown manual, not this page.
.SH NAME
karidns.conf \- the configuration file of karidns(8)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.RS 2
.nf
karidns [\-v | \-\-version | \-V] [\-f] [\-d debug_level] [\-p port | \-p pid_file] [\-P pid_file] [\-c config_file | config_file]
.fi
.RE
.SH CONFIGURATION OVERVIEW
The configuration file uses the \fBnamed.conf\fR\-style block syntax. A complete
reference of every statement follows in CONFIGURATION REFERENCE.
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
options {
    port 53;
    bind\-address { 0.0.0.0; ::; };
    user "named";
    group "named";
    pid\-file "/var/run/karidns/karidns.pid"; // "none" to disable
    udp\-recvbuf\-size 4M;
    udp\-sndbuf\-size 4M;
\&
    rate\-limit {
        responses\-per\-second 50;
        nodata\-per\-second 50;
        nxdomains\-per\-second 20;
        errors\-per\-second 10;
        window 15;
        slip 2;
        exempt\-clients { 127.0.0.1/32; 192.168.0.0/16; ::1/128; };
    };
};
\&
logging {
    channel queries_log {
        file "/var/log/named/queries.log" versions 3 size 10M;
        print\-time yes;
        print\-category yes;
        print\-severity yes;
    };
    category queries { queries_log; };
};
\&
control\-channel {
    socket "/var/run/karidns/control.sock";
    algorithm "hmac\-sha256";
    secret "UkVQTEFDRS1NRTpvcGVuc3NsLXJhbmQtYmFzZTY0LTMy"; // placeholder: replace with `openssl rand \-base64 32`
};
\&
key "transfer\-key" {
    algorithm "hmac\-sha256";
    secret "UkVQTEFDRS1NRTpvcGVuc3NsLXJhbmQtYmFzZTY0LTMy"; // placeholder: replace with `openssl rand \-base64 32`
};
\&
zone "example.com" {
    type master;
    file "/usr/local/etc/namedb/master/example.com.zone";
    file\-format bind;   # "bind" (default) or "tinydns"; see TINYDNS ZONE FORMAT below
    allow\-transfer { 192.168.1.100; };
    also\-notify { 192.168.1.100 port 53; };
    notify\-source "192.168.1.1";
};
.fi
.RE
.SH CONFIGURATION REFERENCE
.SS General syntax
.IP \(bu 2
Statements end with \fB;\fR, and blocks are written as \fBname { ... };\fR (the
\fB;\fR after the closing brace is required).
.IP \(bu 2
Comments: \fB# ...\fR, \fB// ...\fR and \fB/* ... */\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Values may be quoted (\fB"..."\fR) or bare words. A single token is limited to
4096 bytes (longer tokens are truncated with a warning). The configuration file
(and each included file) may be at most 256 MiB.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBinclude "file";\fR may appear anywhere and inserts the file in
place. Relative paths are resolved against \fBoptions { directory }\fR when it
is set (BIND 9.20), otherwise against the directory of the file that contains
the \fBinclude\fR. Includes can be nested up to 16 levels deep; circular
includes are rejected.
.IP \(bu 2
Boolean values accept \fByes\fR / \fBtrue\fR / \fB1\fR and \fBno\fR /
\fBfalse\fR / \fB0\fR (case-insensitive), in every statement, as BIND does. Any
other value is a configuration error.
.IP \(bu 2
An unknown statement inside \fBzone { }\fR or \fBview { }\fR is a configuration
error (reload and startup fail, \fBkaricheck conf\fR shows it): it used to be
skipped silently, so a typo such as \fBalow\-transfer\fR left a zone open or
closed. Options that BIND 9 documents for those statements but KariDNS does not
implement (\fBallow\-query\fR, \fBinline\-signing\fR, \fBdnssec\-policy\fR,
\fBauto\-dnssec\fR, \fBkey\-directory\fR, \fBmax\-journal\-size\fR,
\fBzone\-statistics\fR, \fBrecursion\fR, ...) are skipped with a warning. An
unknown key in \fBcontrol\-channel { }\fR is a configuration error as well. So
is an unknown statement in \fBoptions { }\fR and in \fBkey { }\fR, with the file
and line (\fB[ERROR] karidns.conf:12: options: unknown
option 'bind\-adress' ...\fR): a misspelt \fBbind\-address\fR, \fBuser\fR,
\fBpid\-file\fR, \fBsecret\fR or \fBalgorithm\fR used to have no effect. The
options that BIND 9.18 or 9.20 documents for \fBoptions { }\fR but KariDNS does
not implement (\fBrecursion\fR, \fBforwarders\fR, ...; the list is the union of
the two ARMs plus the options of older BIND versions) are skipped with a warning
(\fB[WARNING] karidns.conf:3: options: 'recursion' is a BIND option that KariDNS
does not implement; it is ignored\fR). \fBkey { }\fR takes \fBalgorithm\fR and
\fBsecret\fR, each once, and both are required (BIND does too): a key that lacks
one, or has any other statement, is an error. Unknown statements in
\fBlogging\fR are still skipped without a message. An unknown top-level
statement (BIND refuses it; a BIND statement that KariDNS does not implement is
skipped) is skipped up to its \fB;\fR or closing \fB}\fR with a warning that
names it and the line (\fB[WARNING] karidns.conf:7: configuration: unknown
statement 'zonee' is skipped ...\fR), so a misspelt \fBzone\fR does not go
unnoticed. The lexer refuses what it used to cope with silently: a \fB/*
comment\fR that is never closed (it swallowed the rest of the file), a quoted
string that is never closed, and a token longer than 4096 bytes (it was cut and
used with a warning) are syntax errors, and so is an \fBinclude\fR path that is
longer than \fBPATH_MAX\fR (a cut path opened another file). An address-list
element with host bits below its prefix length (\fB10.0.0.1/8\fR) is accepted,
as in BIND (the network is matched), with a warning that names the file and line
(\fBkaricheck conf\fR shows it too). Out of memory while the configuration is
read ends the process (\fBexit(1)\fR), at start-up and on a reload alike: the
parser has no way back from a failed allocation, and a half-read configuration
is never applied.
.IP \(bu 2
Zone names are normalized to their fully qualified form (a trailing \fB.\fR is
added), so \fB"example.com"\fR and \fB"example.com."\fR are the same zone. A
class after the zone or view name is accepted as in BIND 9.20 (\fBzone
"example.com" IN { ... };\fR, \fBview "v" IN { ... };\fR): \fBIN\fR in any
letter case is ignored, any other class (\fBCH\fR, \fBHS\fR,
\fBCLASSnn\fR, ...) is a configuration error with the file and line
(\fBzone 'example.com.': class 'CH' is not supported (only IN)\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
Relative file paths (zone \fBfile\fR, log \fBfile\fR, \fBpid\-file\fR, the
\fBcontrol\-channel\fR and \fBdnstap\fR sockets, \fBdatabase\-file\fR,
\fBconninfo\-file\fR, \fBinclude\fR, \fB$INCLUDE\fR) are resolved against
\fBoptions { directory "<dir>"; }\fR when it is written (BIND 9.20: "all
relative paths use this directory as a base"; see the option below), otherwise
against the working directory \fBkaridns\fR was started from, including paths
that contain \fB../\fR. \fBWorking directory.\fR As BIND does, \fBkaridns\fR
changes into \fBoptions { directory }\fR once, when it starts (a missing or
unreadable directory is a start-up error), and \fBdaemonize()\fR does not change
directory (it used to \fBchdir("/")\fR): the daemon keeps the directory it was
started in, or \fBdirectory\fR, so that a relative name means the same before
and after it daemonizes. This is what the UNIX socket paths of
\fBcontrol\-channel\fR and \fBdnstap\fR rely on: they are used as written, not
made absolute (a UNIX socket path holds only 103 bytes, \fBsun_path\fR), so a
relative \fBsocket "karidns.sock";\fR is created in, and connected from, that
directory; a path longer than 103 bytes is a start-up error with a message
(before the daemon forks, so it reaches the terminal). The cost is the one BIND
has: that directory's file system stays busy (it cannot be unmounted while
\fBkaridns\fR runs), and a core dump is written there. A reload never changes
directory. The paths handed to something that does not share the working
directory are made absolute at start-up (against \fBdirectory\fR, else the
starting directory): \fBpid\-file\fR (also \fB\-P\fR), and \fBloader\-path\fR,
\fBdatabase\-file\fR and \fBconninfo\-file\fR of \fBoptions { database }\fR
(\fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR runs as \fBloader\-user\fR and is started from \fB/\fR;
so are the \fBtype program\fR plugins). Exceptions: \fBprogram\fR stays
absolute-only, and a path given on the command line (\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-P\fR) is
relative to the directory \fBkaridns\fR was started from, even when \fBoptions {
directory }\fR is set. The server opens the directories of zone and log files
when it starts, before it enters the Capsicum sandbox; a zone or log file that a
reload adds in a directory the server has not used before cannot be opened
unless it lies below \fBdirectory\fR or below the directory of a zone file that
was loaded at start-up (those trees are reached through the directory file
descriptors opened then, with \fBopenat()\fR) (the zone answers SERVFAIL and
\fBkarictl reload\fR / \fBreconfig\fR report it) until the next restart.
.IP \(bu 2
Unknown top-level blocks are skipped silently (up to the next \fB;\fR at the
same block level), so a misspelled statement name has no effect; \fBoptions\fR,
\fBkey\fR, \fBzone\fR, \fBview\fR, \fBcontrol\-channel\fR, \fBlogging\fR and
\fBcontrols\fR are strict, see above. Use \fBkaricheck conf\fR to validate a
configuration.
.IP \(bu 2
Settings that are fixed when the server starts (\fBport\fR, \fBbind\-address\fR,
\fBlisten\-on\fR, \fBlisten\-on\-v6\fR, \fBuser\fR, \fBgroup\fR,
\fBpid\-file\fR, \fBudp\-recvbuf\-size\fR, \fBudp\-sndbuf\-size\fR,
\fBtcp\-window\fR, the \fBcontrol\-channel\fR socket path, \fBdnstap\fR, a log
file in a directory that was not open at start-up (see \fBlogging\fR), and the
interface snapshot that \fBlocalhost\fR / \fBlocalnets\fR in address match lists
are evaluated against) need a restart; a reload (\fBSIGHUP\fR, \fBkarictl
reload\fR / \fBreconfig\fR) applies everything else. When a reload changes one
of them, the server logs a warning that names it, and \fBkarictl\fR shows it as
\fBOK (restart needed for: ...)\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
A reload that cannot be applied (the file cannot be read, a configuration error,
a log file that cannot be opened) keeps the running configuration. A reload
whose zone files fail to load applies the configuration and serves SERVFAIL for
those zones; the failed zones are logged. If readers of the previous
configuration are still active after 10 seconds, the reload is postponed and
retried every second until it runs. \fBSIGHUP\fR signals that arrive while a
reload is running are merged into one reload.
.SS Top-level statements
.TS
lbw(35) lbw(36)
lw(35) lw(36).
\&Statement	\&Description
_
\&\fBoptions { ... };\fR	\&Server-wide options (below).
\&\fBzone "<name>" { ... };\fR	T{
A zone. Top-level zones and \fBview\fR blocks cannot be mixed in one
configuration.
T}
\&\fBview "<name>" { ... };\fR	T{
A view containing \fBmatch\-clients\fR and \fBzone\fR blocks.
T}
\&\fBkey "<name>" { ... };\fR	T{
A TSIG key, visible in every view (a \fBkey\fR inside a \fBview\fR is visible in
that view only; see \fBkey\fR).
T}
\&\fBserver <prefix> { ... };\fR	T{
Settings for the remote servers an address or prefix covers: the TSIG key,
source addresses and other options of the requests to them (BIND \fBserver\fR
statement; see \fBserver\fR). Also allowed inside \fBview\fR.
T}
T{
\fBremote\-servers "<name>" [ port <n> ] { ... };\fR
T}	T{
A named list of servers for \fBalso\-notify\fR (also written \fBprimaries\fR or
\fBmasters\fR). See \fBalso\-notify\fR and \fBremote\-servers\fR below.
T}
\&\fBcontrol\-channel { ... };\fR	T{
Enables the \fBkarictl(8)\fR control socket.
T}
\&\fBlogging { ... };\fR	T{
Log channels and categories (BIND syntax; see \fBlogging\fR).
T}
\&\fBcontrols { ... };\fR	T{
BIND's rndc channel: read, checked and ignored (see \fBcontrols\fR).
T}
\&\fBstatistics\-channels { ... };\fR	T{
BIND's HTTP statistics server: not implemented, skipped with a warning.
T}
\&\fBdnstap { ... };\fR	T{
dnstap output (same block as inside \fBoptions\fR).
T}
\&\fBacl "<name>" { ... };\fR	T{
A named address match list (see Address match lists).
T}
\&\fBinclude "<file>";\fR	\&Includes another file.
.TE
.PP
Duplicate zones (in the same view or at top level), duplicate views, duplicate
keys and duplicate \fBacl\fR names (names compared case-insensitively) are
rejected. Zone names are compared as DNS names, so \fBexample.com\fR and
\fBexample.com.\fR are the same zone.
.SS options { ... }
.TP 8
\fBport <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB53\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Listen port (UDP and TCP), 1\[u2013]65535. A value that is
not a number in that range is a configuration error with the file and line. The
port of a \fBlisten\-on\fR / \fBlisten\-on\-v6\fR statement that has no
\fBport\fR of its own (see "Listening addresses"). Overridden by \fB\-p
<port>\fR, which replaces this port and the ports written in \fBlisten\-on\fR /
\fBlisten\-on\-v6\fR too. It is also the default port of the \fBprimaries\fR /
\fBmasters\fR and \fBalso\-notify\fR elements that have none, of the
\fBforwarders\fR of a forward zone (an element, or a \fBforwarders port <n>
{ ... }\fR list, with none), of the \fBremote\-servers\fR lists, of the NOTIFY
sent to the NS hosts of a zone and of the primary of a catalog member (BIND
\fBnamed_config_getport()\fR; see \fBalso\-notify\fR and \fBremote\-servers\fR);
\fB\-p\fR does not change that default.
.TP 8
\fBdirectory "<path>";\fR
\fIDefault:\fR none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR BIND 9.20 ARM: "This sets the working directory of the
server"; the ARM recommends an absolute path. Every relative path of the
configuration is relative to it: zone \fBfile\fR, \fBpid\-file\fR, log channel
\fBfile\fR, the \fBcontrol\-channel\fR and \fBdnstap\fR sockets,
\fBdatabase\-file\fR and \fBconninfo\-file\fR, \fBinclude\fR and the relative
\fB$INCLUDE\fR lines of zone files. Without it, relative paths are relative to
the directory \fBkaridns\fR was started in (and a relative \fBinclude\fR to the
file that contains it). \fBkaridns\fR changes into the directory once at
start-up, as BIND does when it reads the statement, and keeps it when it
daemonizes (see "Relative file paths" in General syntax); a relative
\fBsocket\fR of \fBcontrol\-channel\fR or \fBdnstap\fR is therefore looked for
there. It may be written anywhere in \fBoptions\fR (and, for an \fBinclude\fR,
it must be in the main configuration file: a \fBdirectory\fR that only an
included file contains, after a relative \fBinclude\fR was already read, is
refused). The directory is opened once, when the server starts, and the server
refuses to start without it; \fBa reload keeps using the directory the server
was started with\fR: a different or removed \fBdirectory\fR in the file is a
warning, \fBkarictl\fR answers \fBOK (restart needed for: directory)\fR, and
relative paths keep resolving against the old one until the next
restart. \fBkaricheck conf\fR checks that it exists and is a directory.
.TP 8
\fBbind\-address { <addr>; ... };\fR or \fBbind\-address <addr>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR all addresses (\fB0.0.0.0\fR and \fB::\fR)
.br
\fIDescription:\fR KariDNS's own statement: addresses to listen on (IPv4 and
IPv6), each at \fBport\fR. An element that is not an IPv4 or IPv6 address is a
configuration error with the file and line (it used to bind nothing,
silently). Combined with \fBlisten\-on\fR / \fBlisten\-on\-v6\fR: the union (see
"Listening addresses").
.TP 8
\fBlisten\-on [ port <n> ] { <address match list>; ... };\fR and \fBlisten\-on\-v6 [ port <n> ] { ... };\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB0.0.0.0\fR and \fB::\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR BIND 9.20 syntax, may be written several times: the IPv4 /
IPv6 addresses of this host that the list allows, at the port of the
statement. See "Listening addresses". \fBtls\fR, \fBhttp\fR and \fBproxy\fR are
configuration errors (this server has no DNS-over-TLS, DNS-over-HTTPS or PROXYv2
listener).
.TP 8
\fBblackhole { <address match list>; ... };\fR
\fIDefault:\fR none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR BIND 9.20 syntax: requests from an address the list matches
(first match decides; a non-negated match drops) are dropped without any
response, over UDP and TCP (a TCP connection is closed at once). It is read for
every request, so a reload adds, changes or removes it without a restart. See
"Listening addresses".
.TP 8
\fBuser "<name>";\fR
\fIDefault:\fR none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR User to drop to after binding sockets. Required when started
as root. A name that resolves to uid 0 (\fBroot\fR, \fBtoor\fR) is refused by
\fBkaricheck conf\fR and at start (a reload cannot change it: it takes effect
after a restart, and the Backend does not resolve names).
.TP 8
\fBgroup "<name>";\fR
\fIDefault:\fR the user's primary group
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Group to drop to. The group that is used (this one, else the
user's primary group) must not be gid 0: a root group, also under another name,
is refused like root.
.TP 8
\fBpid\-file "<path>";\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB/var/run/karidns/karidns.pid\fR when daemonized, none in
foreground
.br
\fIDescription:\fR PID lock file; \fB"none"\fR disables it. Overridden by
\fB\-P\fR / \fB\-p <path>\fR. A relative name (also from \fB\-P\fR) is made
absolute once at start-up, against \fBdirectory\fR when it is set, else the
directory \fBkaridns\fR was started in; a name that is longer than 1023 bytes
once absolute is a start-up error, never a truncated path. BIND's \fBnamed\fR
writes a relative pid file relative to its working directory,
i.e. \fBdirectory\fR.
.TP 8
\fBudp\-recvbuf\-size <size>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB4M\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR \fBSO_RCVBUF\fR of the UDP sockets. Accepts a
\fBK\fR/\fBM\fR/\fBG\fR suffix, up to 2147483647 octets; \fB0\fR keeps the
default and a non-zero value is held to at least 4096 (as BIND does for
\fBudp\-receive\-buffer\fR). A value that is not a size (\fBjunk\fR, \fB\-8k\fR,
\fB4G\fR) is a configuration error with the file and line. The kernel caps it at
\fBkern.ipc.maxsockbuf\fR.
.TP 8
\fBudp\-sndbuf\-size <size>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB4M\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR \fBSO_SNDBUF\fR of the UDP sockets (same syntax).
.TP 8
\fBtcp\-mss <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR not set (OS default)
.br
\fIDescription:\fR 536\[u2013]65495. See TRANSPORT TUNING.
.TP 8
\fBtcp\-window <size>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR not set (OS default)
.br
\fIDescription:\fR 4K\[u2013]64M, \fBK\fR/\fBM\fR suffix allowed. See TRANSPORT
TUNING.
.TP 8
\fBudp\-bufsize <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB1232\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR 512\[u2013]4096. Maximum UDP response size and the payload
size advertised in the response OPT. See TRANSPORT TUNING.
.TP 8
\fBtcp\-connection\-reuse yes|no;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBno\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Keep TCP connections open for further queries (RFC 7766
\[u00A7]6.2.1). With \fBno\fR, the connection is closed after the first
response. With \fByes\fR, pipelined queries are read and answered in the order
they arrive (not concurrently, RFC 7766 \[u00A7]6.2.1.1 is a SHOULD), up to 16
per turn of the event loop. Any TCP connection is closed 60 s after it was
opened at the latest.
.TP 8
\fBtcp\-initial\-timeout <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB10000ms\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Time a new TCP connection may take to deliver its first
complete query. \fBThe unit is BIND's: 100 ms\fR (\fBtcp\-initial\-timeout
100;\fR is 10 s; BIND raises a value below 25 (2.5 s) with a
warning). Milliseconds are written with the suffix \fBms\fR (\fB10000ms\fR;
\fB0ms\fR is the default). A number without a suffix above 1200 (BIND's maximum,
120 s) is a configuration error that names the suffix: it is almost certainly an
old KariDNS value in milliseconds, which BIND would silently lower to 120 s. See
"BIND option values".
.TP 8
\fBtcp\-idle\-timeout <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB10000ms\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Time after a complete query in which the next one must have
arrived completely; same units and limits as \fBtcp\-initial\-timeout\fR (BIND
minimum 1 = 100 ms). It is not extended while only part of a message has arrived
(RFC 7766 \[u00A7]6.2.3), so a client that sends a message a few bytes at a time
is disconnected. Also the value advertised in edns-tcp-keepalive (RFC 7828)
unless \fBtcp\-advertised\-timeout\fR is written.
.TP 8
\fBtcp\-advertised\-timeout <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR the idle timeout
.br
\fIDescription:\fR The idle timeout the edns-tcp-keepalive option (RFC 7828
\[u00A7]3.3.2) tells a client, in units of 100 ms (BIND; at most 65535, a larger
value is lowered with a warning; \fB0\fR is allowed); \fBms\fR suffix as above.
.TP 8
\fBtcp\-keepalive\-timeout <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR n/a
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Accepted (units of 100 ms, \fBms\fR suffix, minimum 1,
maximum 65535 as in BIND) with a note that it has no effect: every connection
uses \fBtcp\-idle\-timeout\fR.
.TP 8
\fBminimal\-responses yes|no|no\-auth|no\-auth\-recursive;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBno\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR BIND 9.20 values. \fByes\fR: no authority NS records and no
additional section (an NS question still gets its glue, as in
BIND). \fBno\-auth\fR: no authority NS records, the additional section
stays. \fBno\-auth\-recursive\fR: \fBno\-auth\fR for queries with the RD bit
set, \fBno\fR for the others. Negative answers keep their SOA.
.TP 8
\fBin\-view\-settings owner|view;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBowner\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Also in \fBview\fR and in a zone that has \fBin\-view\fR:
whose ACLs apply to the requests that come through a view for a zone it shares
with an earlier view. See Zones shared between views.
.TP 8
\fBminimal\-any yes|no;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBno\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR RFC 8482: answer \fBQTYPE=ANY\fR with a synthesized \fBHINFO
"RFC8482" ""\fR record instead of all RRsets. When the query has DO=1 and the
name has RRSIG records, a single RRset is returned instead (RFC 8482
\[u00A7]4.2).
.TP 8
\fBminimal\-any\-ttl <seconds>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB86400\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR TTL of the synthesized RFC 8482 \fBHINFO\fR record. 0 to
2147483647 (RFC 2181 section 8); a larger value is a configuration error.
.TP 8
\fBalias\-resolver { <addr> [port <n>]; ... };\fR
\fIDefault:\fR none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR The resolvers that expand the ALIAS records of every zone
(PowerDNS \fBexpand\-alias\fR); 1 to 8 IP addresses. Without it ALIAS is stored
and transferred and A / AAAA at an ALIAS name are NODATA. Can be overridden per
zone. See POWERDNS ALIAS AND LUA RECORDS.
.TP 8
\fBadditional\-from\-auth yes|in\-domain|no;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fByes\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Whether additional-section data (glue, MX/SRV targets) is
taken from the server's authoritative data. \fBin\-domain\fR (alias
\fBin\-zone\fR) limits it to names inside the zone of the
answer. Additional-section addresses ignore ECS: only records without an ECS tag
(\fB$ECS\-SUBNET default\fR / \fBnone\fR) are given there, whatever the client
subnet, and they do not change the ECS SCOPE of the response (RFC 7871
\[u00A7]7.4: "Addresses in the Additional section SHOULD therefore ignore ECS
data"; \[u00A7]7.3.1: a resolver does not tie them to a network). Location tags
and tinydns locations, which follow the client's own address, still apply. Glue
taken from another zone of the view is chosen with that zone's own location
tags, tinydns locations and SOA visibility, as if the client had asked that
zone. Any other value is a configuration error. Can be overridden per zone. When
a referral does not fit the message, TC=1 is set only if glue for a name server
inside the delegated zone is left out (RFC 9471 \[u00A7]3.1); glue of other
(sibling) name servers that does not fit is left out without TC (RFC 9471
\[u00A7]3.2, RFC 2181 \[u00A7]9).
.TP 8
\fBsend\-extended\-errors yes|no;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fByes\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Add Extended DNS Errors (EDE, RFC 8914) to responses of EDNS
queries.
.TP 8
\fBixfr\-history <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB4\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR 0\[u2013]32. How many past versions of a zone are kept as
IXFR differences (RFC 1995 \[u00A7]4). A secondary that is at most this many
versions behind gets an incremental transfer; a secondary that is further
behind, or any secondary when the value is \fB0\fR, gets the whole zone in the
AXFR-style IXFR answer that RFC 1995 \[u00A7]4 allows when an incremental answer
is not available (a client that is already up to date still gets a single SOA,
\[u00A7]2). The history is kept in memory only and is allocated when the first
difference is stored, so a zone that never changes after loading costs
nothing. \fB0\fR also skips computing differences. A reload that lowers the
value drops the oldest differences at once; a higher value takes effect with the
next stored difference. A value outside 0\[u2013]32 is a configuration
error. Can be overridden per zone.
.TP 8
\fBmax\-zone\-records <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB10000000\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR 2..4294967295. The most RRs one inbound AXFR/IXFR of a
secondary zone may bring (the size of the zone being built, after an IXFR's
deletions); a transfer that goes over is refused as a whole and the zone keeps
its last version (the log says \fBmax\-zone\-records\fR). Same name, range and
default as the \fBdatabase\fR block. Can be overridden per zone.
.TP 8
\fBmax\-zone\-bytes <size>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB1G\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR 65535..64G (K, M, G suffixes). The most message octets one
inbound AXFR/IXFR of a secondary zone may bring; over it the transfer is
refused. Can be overridden per zone. Independently of both limits, a transfer
signed with TSIG that has more than 99 messages in a row without a TSIG is
refused (RFC 8945 section 5.3.1: a primary signs at least every 100th message),
and so is an IXFR whose difference sequences do not start from the secondary's
serial and chain to the current one (RFC 1995 section 4; karidns then asks for
an AXFR), and a transfer that carries a record of another class than IN (RFC
5936 section 2.2.1). A zone may carry at most 4096 Extended AXFR
\fBLOC_TAGDEF\fR and 4096 \fBECS_TAGDEF\fR records and 65536 CIDRs in them.
.TP 8
\fBalso\-notify [ port <n> ] { <addr> [port <n>] [key <name>]; <list>; ... };\fR
\fIDefault:\fR none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Servers that receive NOTIFY in addition to the apex NS hosts,
for every zone (and catalog member) that has no \fBalso\-notify\fR of its own or
of its view. Same grammar and meaning as in \fBzone\fR; see \fBalso\-notify\fR
and \fBremote\-servers\fR below.
.TP 8
\fBnotify yes|no|explicit|primary\-only|master\-only;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fByes\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Which NOTIFY messages are sent; same meaning as in \fBzone\fR
(BIND 9.20 ARM "notify"). Can be overridden per view and per zone.
.TP 8
\fBnotify\-retries <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB2\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR 0\[u2013]10. How many times a NOTIFY that got no answer is
sent again over UDP (RFC 1996 \[u00A7]3.6); after the last one the NOTIFY is
made once over TCP, see NOTIFY over TCP. The default is BIND 9.20's
\fBudpretries\fR of a NOTIFY (\fBnotify_send_toaddr()\fR). Can be overridden per
zone.
.TP 8
\fBnotify\-retry\-interval <seconds>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB5\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR 1\[u2013]3600. Time from sending a NOTIFY to its first
retransmission, and the wait for an answer after the last one. The default is
BIND 9.20's \fBudptimeout\fR of a NOTIFY. Can be overridden per zone.
.TP 8
\fBnotify\-retry\-backoff fixed|exponential;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBfixed\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR \fBfixed\fR keeps \fBnotify\-retry\-interval\fR between all
retransmissions; \fBexponential\fR doubles it after each one (capped at 3600
s). The defaults (2 retransmissions, 5 s, \fBfixed\fR) are what BIND 9.20 does
when nothing is configured: \fBnotify_send_toaddr()\fR passes \fBudptimeout\fR 5
and \fBudpretries\fR 2 to \fBdns_request_create()\fR, which sends the datagram
at 0, 5 and 10 s and gives up at 15 s, and \fBnotify_done()\fR then makes the
NOTIFY over TCP. A NOTIFY to a silent peer therefore reaches TCP after about 15
s (the earlier defaults of 60 s and 5 retransmissions took about 6
minutes). \fBnotify\-retry\-interval 3; notify\-retry\-backoff exponential;\fR
retransmits after 3, 6, 12, 24 and 48 s. Can be overridden per zone.
.TP 8
\fBnotify\-tcp\-timeout <seconds>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB15\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR 1\[u2013]300. The time one attempt of a NOTIFY over TCP may
take, the connection and the answer together (NOTIFY over TCP). BIND 9.20 has no
such option: its TCP request has a fixed timeout of 15 s
(\fBnotify_send_toaddr()\fR), which is the default here. A peer that does not
answer holds one thread for at most this long per attempt (\fBtcp\-only\fR makes
two attempts). Can be overridden per zone.
.TP 8
\fBallow\-query { <acl>; ... };\fR
\fIDefault:\fR any host
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Who may ask the zones ordinary questions (BIND 9.20 ARM
"allow-query"). A client that is not allowed gets REFUSED with Extended DNS
Error 18 (Prohibited) over UDP and TCP. The default of every zone; a \fBview\fR
or \fBzone\fR statement replaces it. A list is read as described in Address
match lists.
.TP 8
\fBallow\-query\-on { <acl>; ... };\fR
\fIDefault:\fR any local address
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Which local addresses accept ordinary questions: the address
the query was sent to (BIND 9.20 ARM "allow-query-on"). A query must be allowed
by \fBallow\-query\fR \fBand\fR \fBallow\-query\-on\fR. Inherited like
\fBallow\-query\fR.
.TP 8
\fBallow\-transfer { <acl>; ... };\fR
\fIDefault:\fR none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Who may transfer the zones (AXFR/IXFR over TCP). BIND 9.20
ARM: "If allow-transfer is not specified, then the default is none". The default
of every zone; a \fBview\fR or \fBzone\fR statement replaces it. \fBport\fR and
\fBtransport\fR in front of the braces (BIND 9.20) are configuration errors.
.TP 8
\fBallow\-update { <acl>; ... };\fR
\fIDefault:\fR none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Who may send Dynamic Updates (RFC 2136); the default of every
primary zone, replaced by a \fBview\fR or \fBzone\fR statement. BIND advises
writing it per zone: a default at this level opens every primary zone to those
clients. The plain list of a database zone (its settings text) has no \fBkey\fR
or \fB!key\fR elements: a key is named by its bare name, and a negated key
cannot be written there (an entry that begins with \fB!\fR and is not an address
is ignored).
.TP 8
\fBallow\-notify { <acl>; ... };\fR
\fIDefault:\fR the zone's primaries only
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Who may send NOTIFY to the secondary zones, in addition to
their primaries (BIND 9.20 ARM "allow-notify"); a \fBkey\fR element is the TSIG
key the NOTIFY is signed with. It applies to secondary zones only.
.TP 8
\fBserve\-stale yes|no;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fByes\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR When a secondary zone has expired (SOA EXPIRE passed since
the last successful transfer, or earlier when the server it transfers from
returned a shorter EDNS EXPIRE value, RFC 7314), keep answering from the stale
data (with EDE 3, Stale Answer). With \fBno\fR such queries get SERVFAIL with
EDE 24 (Invalid Data, RFC 8914 \[u00A7]4.25).
.TP 8
\fBnsid "<string>";\fR
\fIDefault:\fR not set
.br
\fIDescription:\fR NSID (RFC 5001) value returned to queries that request it, at
most 128 octets (a longer one is a configuration error: it would not fit a
512-octet response). \fBserver\-id\fR (below) sets the same value.
.TP 8
\fBversion ( "<text>" | none );\fR \fBhostname ( "<text>" | none );\fR \fBserver\-id ( "<text>" | none | hostname );\fR
\fIDefault:\fR the KariDNS version, the host name, none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR The answers to \fBversion.bind\fR, \fBhostname.bind\fR and
\fBid.server\fR TXT queries in class CH (RFC 4892), as BIND's built-in CHAOS
zones give them. \fBnone\fR leaves the name without a TXT record (NOERROR with
no answer, the SOA in the authority section); a quoted \fB"none"\fR is the text
none. \fBserver\-id\fR is also the NSID of EDNS queries, so an \fBnsid\fR with
another value is an error. See "BIND option values".
.TP 8
\fBrequire\-server\-cookie yes|no;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBno\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR A UDP query that carries a client cookie but no valid server
cookie gets BADCOOKIE (with a server cookie to repeat the query with) instead of
an answer (RFC 7873 \[u00A7]5.2.3; BIND 9.20). A query without a cookie option,
and TCP queries, are answered as usual. Can be overridden per view.
.TP 8
\fBmax\-udp\-size <n>;\fR \fBedns\-udp\-size <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB1232\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR \fBmax\-udp\-size\fR is \fBudp\-bufsize\fR under BIND's name:
512..4096, a value outside is lowered or raised with a warning (BIND does it
silently); with \fBudp\-bufsize\fR written too, the two must agree. Can be
overridden per view. \fBedns\-udp\-size\fR is the size of the queries a resolver
sends; it is read and has no effect.
.TP 8
\fBudp\-receive\-buffer <n>;\fR \fBudp\-send\-buffer <n>;\fR \fBtcp\-receive\-buffer <n>;\fR \fBtcp\-send\-buffer <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR see below
.br
\fIDescription:\fR BIND names. \fBudp\-receive\-buffer\fR /
\fBudp\-send\-buffer\fR are \fBudp\-recvbuf\-size\fR / \fBudp\-sndbuf\-size\fR
(both names with different values is an error; the four names are read by one
parser and a bad value is an error under each; BIND raises a non-zero value
below 4096 to 4096; \fB0\fR keeps KariDNS's
\fB4M\fR). \fBtcp\-receive\-buffer\fR / \fBtcp\-send\-buffer\fR set the two
directions of \fBtcp\-window\fR separately; a direction that is written replaces
\fBtcp\-window\fR for it.
.TP 8
\fBmax\-records <n>;\fR \fBmax\-records\-per\-type <n>;\fR \fBmax\-types\-per\-name <n>;\fR \fBmax\-zone\-ttl <ttl>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB0\fR (none)
.br
\fIDescription:\fR BIND 9.20 limits, in \fBoptions\fR, \fBview\fR and \fBzone\fR
(the zone's value wins, then the view's, then options'). \fBmax\-records\fR: RRs
in the zone; \fBmax\-records\-per\-type\fR: RRs in one RRset;
\fBmax\-types\-per\-name\fR: RR types at one name; \fBmax\-zone\-ttl\fR: the
largest TTL of a primary zone (\fBunlimited\fR or 0 = no limit; seconds or a TTL
with \fBs m h d w\fR; an ISO 8601 duration is refused). A zone file, a transfer
or a database zone that breaks one is not loaded (the version in service stays,
the log names the owner), and \fBkaricheck zones\fR reports it as an error. A
dynamic update that would break one is refused with SERVFAIL; an update's TTL
above \fBmax\-zone\-ttl\fR is lowered to it. \fBThe defaults are no limit\fR;
BIND's own defaults are 0 / 100 / 100 / unlimited.
.TP 8
\fBserial\-update\-method increment|unixtime|date;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBincrement\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR How a dynamic update that changes a zone sets the SOA serial
(BIND \fBdns_update_soaserial()\fR): \fBincrement\fR adds 1; \fBunixtime\fR uses
the clock; \fBdate\fR uses \fBYYYYMMDD00\fR of the local date. A value that is
not ahead of the current serial (RFC 1982) falls back to \fBincrement\fR. In
\fBoptions\fR, \fBview\fR and \fBzone\fR.
.TP 8
\fBrollback\-window <time>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB1h\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR KariDNS. How long the server keeps the version that a
zone-file reload or an install from the database replaces, so that \fBkarictl
rollback\fR can put it back (see "Rollback" below). A number of seconds or a
time with the units \fBs m h d w\fR (\fB90m\fR, \fB1d\fR); \fB0\fR keeps
nothing. In \fBoptions\fR, \fBview\fR and \fBzone\fR (the zone's value wins,
then the view's, then options'); a database zone follows the view's or options'
value. A change applies from the next reload or install on: a version that is
kept already ends at the time it was given.
.TP 8
\fBmasterfile\-format text|raw;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBtext\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR \fBtext\fR is accepted (zone files are read as text);
\fBraw\fR is an error: a raw file read as text would fail in a confusing
way. \fBmasterfile\-style\fR is a BIND option that KariDNS does not implement (a
warning).
.TP 8
\fBcheck\-names\fR, \fBcheck\-integrity\fR, \fBcheck\-mx\fR, \fBcheck\-mx\-cname\fR, \fBcheck\-srv\-cname\fR, \fBcheck\-dup\-records\fR, \fBcheck\-wildcard\fR, \fBcheck\-sibling\fR, \fBcheck\-spf\fR, \fBcheck\-svcb\fR
\fIDefault:\fR see below
.br
\fIDescription:\fR BIND's zone checks. They set the severity (\fBfail\fR,
\fBwarn\fR, \fBignore\fR; booleans: \fByes\fR = fail, \fBno\fR = ignore) of the
findings of \fBkaricheck zone\fR, \fBkaricheck zones\fR and \fBkaricheck db\fR;
the server does not run these checks itself.
.TP 8
\fBcatalog\-zones { zone "<name>" [default\-primaries { ... }] [zone\-directory "<dir>"] [in\-memory <bool>] [min\-update\-interval <ttl>]; ... };\fR
\fIDefault:\fR none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR BIND 9.20 syntax, in \fBoptions\fR or \fBview\fR: the zone of
that name is a catalog zone (as \fBcatalog\-zone yes;\fR in the zone). The other
parts are read and have no effect (members use the primaries of the catalog zone
and are always in memory).
.TP 8
\fBcookie\-secret "<32 hex digits>";\fR
\fIDefault:\fR random per process
.br
\fIDescription:\fR 128-bit SipHash-2-4 server cookie secret (RFC 7873 / RFC
9018). Up to 4 entries: the first creates cookies, all of them are accepted
(secret rollover); a cookie made with an entry other than the first is answered
with a new cookie from the first, so that clients move to the new secret before
the old one is removed. Use the same secret on all servers of an anycast set.
.TP 8
\fBcookie\-algorithm siphash24;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBsiphash24\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR The only supported algorithm (RFC 9018); any other value is
an error.
.TP 8
\fBrfc10029\-mqtype yes|no;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBno\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Enables multiple QTYPEs in one query (RFC 10029).
.TP 8
\fBmax\-mqtypes <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB4\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Maximum number of additional QTYPEs processed per query
(0\[u2013]16; \fB0\fR means the default 4, a larger number is taken as 16, a
negative number or one with other characters is a configuration error).
.TP 8
\fBecs\-enable yes|no;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBno\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Enables EDNS Client Subnet (RFC 7871) processing, used by
\fB$ECS\-SUBNET\fR steering. Global only.
.TP 8
\fBecs\-trusted\-resolvers { <addr/cidr>; ... };\fR
\fIDefault:\fR none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Resolvers whose ECS option is trusted. Can be overridden per
zone.
.TP 8
\fBecs\-tags { tag "<name>" { <cidr>; ... }; ... };\fR
\fIDefault:\fR none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR ECS tag definitions used by \fB$ECS\-SUBNET\fR (see TAG
BLOCKS).
.TP 8
\fBlocation\-tags { tag "<name>" { <cidr>; ... }; ... };\fR
\fIDefault:\fR none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Location tag definitions used by \fB$LOCATION\fR.
.TP 8
\fBallow\-program\-zones yes|no;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBno\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Must be \fByes\fR for \fBtype program\fR zones to be loaded.
.TP 8
\fBresponse\-cache\-size <size>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB32M\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Size of the response cache shared by all worker threads
(allocated once at startup; suffixes \fBK\fR and \fBM\fR, at most \fB1024M\fR;
an invalid value is a configuration error). Answers are added when they are
first asked for: NOERROR authoritative answers of plain queries (no DO bit, ECS,
MQTYPE or other options that change the answer) that fit in 512 octets. Every
reload, transfer, UPDATE or glue change of a zone, and every configuration
reload, gives the zone a new key, so a stale answer is never returned. An answer
whose Additional section holds glue taken from another zone of the view
(additional-from-auth) is not cached, because the other zone can change without
changing the key. Each cached answer takes about 160 octets (the answer itself
plus a 32-octet index entry), so \fB32M\fR holds about 200,000 answers. \fB0\fR
disables the cache. Changing it needs a restart.
.TP 8
\fBwire\-cache\-max\-records <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB0\fR (no limit)
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Answers of zones with more records than this are not put into
the response cache. Answers of tinydns zones and of zones using location/ECS
steering are never cached.
.TP 8
\fBquerylog <bool>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR not set
.br
\fIDescription:\fR BIND 9.20: switches the query log on or off (see
\fBlogging\fR). Not set: on when \fBcategory queries\fR lists a channel.
.TP 8
\fBquery\-log\-max\-qps <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB5000\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Maximum number of query log lines per second, shared by all
worker threads (\fB0\fR means 5000). Queries over the limit are not logged.
.TP 8
\fBquery\-log\-buffer\-size <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB32768\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Entries in each worker's query log ring buffer; a power of
two between 1024 and 1048576. When a ring is 80 % full, query logging is
suspended to protect query processing. Since 0.7 each worker also has an
\fBevent ring\fR of its own for the event and debug messages of its thread (see
"Event messages" under LOGGING), of an eighth of this size and at least 1024
entries (4096 by default), so that they never take room from the query log. An
entry is 424 bytes: by default a worker's two rings take 13.9 MB + 1.7 MB (0.6:
13.9 MB for the one ring). Read at start-up only.
.TP 8
\fBrate\-limit { ... };\fR
\fIDefault:\fR not set (no RRL)
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Response Rate Limiting (below).
.TP 8
\fBalerts { ... };\fR
\fIDefault:\fR not set
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Threshold alerts written to syslog (below; KariDNS only).
.TP 8
\fBdnstap { ... };\fR
\fIDefault:\fR not set
.br
\fIDescription:\fR dnstap output (below).
.SS BIND option values (milestone: option value compatibility)
Where a BIND 9.20.4 option has a KariDNS equivalent, the BIND value is read. The
sources are the BIND files named below (\fBlib/isccfg/namedconf.c\fR for the
grammar, \fBlib/isccfg/check.c\fR for the checks, \fBbin/named/server.c\fR and
\fBzoneconf.c\fR for what the server does).
.PP
\fBTCP timeouts.\fR BIND's unit for \fBtcp\-initial\-timeout\fR,
\fBtcp\-idle\-timeout\fR, \fBtcp\-keepalive\-timeout\fR and
\fBtcp\-advertised\-timeout\fR is 100 ms; KariDNS used milliseconds under the
same names. Plain numbers now mean BIND's unit, and the old meaning is written
with a suffix:
.PP
.TS
lb lbw(46)
l lw(46).
\&In the file	\&Means
_
\&\fBtcp\-idle\-timeout 300;\fR	\&30 s (BIND)
\&\fBtcp\-idle\-timeout 10000ms;\fR	\&10 s (the old KariDNS value)
\&\fBtcp\-idle\-timeout 10000;\fR	T{
\fBerror\fR: 10000 is above BIND's maximum of 1200 (120 s); the message says
\fBwrite 10000ms\fR if milliseconds were meant
T}
\&\fBtcp\-initial\-timeout 10;\fR	T{
raised to 25 (2.5 s) with a warning, as BIND does
T}
.TE
.PP
A configuration that has a number in milliseconds between 1 and 1200 (for
example \fBtcp\-idle\-timeout 1000;\fR) is read as BIND would read it (100 s)
without an error: check the TCP timeouts of a configuration from an earlier
version. \fBkaricheck conf\fR shows the effective warnings. The default stays 10
s (BIND's is 30 s).
.PP
\fB\fBversion.bind\fB, \fBhostname.bind\fB, \fBid.server\fB.\fR \fBdag
version.bind TXT \-c CH @server\fR (RFC 4892). The zones are built in, class CH,
authoritative: the TXT has TTL 0; the SOA is \fB<zone> hostmaster.<zone> 0 28800
7200 604800 86400\fR with TTL 86400 and there is an NS at the apex (BIND
\fBbin/named/builtin.c\fR); a name below the apex is NXDOMAIN, other types at
the apex are NODATA. \fBauthors.bind\fR is not served; any other question in
class CH (a name that is not one of these zones, whatever the IN zones hold) is
REFUSED with AA=0 and EDE 20, as BIND answers a CH question for which it has no
zone. \fBallow\-query\fR of \fBoptions\fR (and of the view the client matches)
applies; a CH zone of the same name in the configuration is served
instead. \fBThe defaults follow BIND: \fBversion.bind\fB answers with the
KariDNS version and \fBhostname.bind\fB with the host name.\fR Write \fBversion
none; hostname none;\fR to say nothing. The host name is read when the
configuration is read (the Backend never asks for it).
.PP
\fB\fBminimal\-responses\fB.\fR As in BIND, an NS question is always answered
with its glue, whatever the setting.
.PP
\fBThe \fBcheck\-*\fB options\fR (\fBkaricheck\fR, \fBkaridns\-dbloader
\-\-check\fR through \fB\-\-zone\-checks\fR). Written values replace the
severity a finding has without them; without any \fBcheck\-*\fR the findings are
the ones KariDNS always reported.
.PP
.TS
lbw(21) lbw(21) lbw(26)
lw(21) lw(21) lw(26).
\&Option	\&Finding	\&Without the option
_
\&\fBcheck\-integrity\fR	T{
an in-bailiwick NS target without A/AAAA glue (error), an MX / SRV target in the
zone without an address (warning)
T}	T{
as written; \fBno\fR turns both off
T}
\&\fBcheck\-mx\-cname\fR	T{
an MX target that is a CNAME
T}	T{
error (BIND's default is warn); an NS target that is a CNAME is always an error
T}
\&\fBcheck\-srv\-cname\fR	T{
an SRV target that is a CNAME
T}	\&warning
\&\fBcheck\-wildcard\fR	T{
an owner name with a \fB*\fR label that is not the first
T}	T{
no finding; \fByes\fR / \fBno\fR: a warning
T}
\&\fBcheck\-dup\-records\fR	T{
the same RR twice in an RRset
T}	\&no finding
\&\fBcheck\-mx\fR	T{
an MX whose target is an IP address
T}	\&no finding
\&\fBcheck\-names\fR	T{
host names: owners of A, AAAA and MX, targets of NS, MX, SRV and the SOA MNAME
made of letters, digits and hyphens (a leading \fB*\fR label in an owner)
T}	T{
no finding. In \fBoptions\fR / \fBview\fR: \fBcheck\-names
primary|secondary|response <mode>;\fR; in \fBzone\fR: \fBcheck\-names <mode>;\fR
T}
T{
\fBcheck\-sibling\fR, \fBcheck\-spf\fR, \fBcheck\-svcb\fR
T}	\&not implemented	T{
read, with a note that they have no effect
T}
.TE
.PP
\fB\fBupdate\-policy\fB\fR (primary zones; \fBgrant\fR / \fBdeny\fR \fB<key>\fR
\fB<match type>\fR \fB[<name>]\fR \fB<types>;\fR). The identity is the TSIG key
that signed the UPDATE (\fB*.suffix\fR matches any key below the suffix). Match
types: \fBname\fR, \fBsubdomain\fR, \fBzonesub\fR, \fBwildcard\fR, \fBself\fR,
\fBselfsub\fR, \fBselfwild\fR; the first rule that matches decides; with no type
list a rule covers every type except NS, SOA and RRSIG; \fBANY\fR covers
all. The UPDATE must be signed; one change that no rule grants makes the whole
UPDATE REFUSED before anything is applied; a delete of every RRset of a name
checks each type found there. \fBkrb5\-*\fR, \fBms\-*\fR, \fBtcp\-self\fR,
\fB6to4\-self\fR and \fBexternal\fR need Kerberos, the transport address or a
helper: such a \fBgrant\fR is dropped with a warning (it can only refuse more
than BIND), such a \fBdeny\fR is an error. \fBupdate\-policy local;\fR and type
counts such as \fBA(3)\fR are errors. \fBallow\-update\fR in the same zone is an
error (BIND \fBcheck.c\fR).
.PP
\fBdnstap in BIND's grammar.\fR \fBdnstap { auth; };\fR \fBdnstap { auth query;
};\fR with \fBdnstap\-output unix "<socket>";\fR, \fBdnstap\-identity ( "<text>"
| none | hostname );\fR, \fBdnstap\-version ( "<text>" | none );\fR and
\fBfstrm\-set\-*\fR (read, no effect). The first word of the block tells the
grammars apart; the KariDNS statements (\fBsocket\fR,
\fBidentity\fR, ...) cannot be mixed with the list. \fBauth\fR and \fBall\fR
select AUTH_QUERY and AUTH_RESPONSE; \fBclient\fR, \fBresolver\fR,
\fBforwarder\fR and \fBupdate\fR are accepted with a note. \fBdnstap\-output
file\fR is an error (the Backend would open a file; use a UNIX socket). BIND
requires \fBdnstap\-output\fR with \fBdnstap\fR, and so does this form.
.PP
\fBWhat stays different from BIND.\fR \fBmax\-records\-per\-type\fR and
\fBmax\-types\-per\-name\fR default to no limit here (BIND: 100; a value above
100 is applied as written, with a warning that it is above BIND's default);
\fBmax\-zone\-ttl\fR accepts seconds and TTL units, not ISO 8601;
\fBmasterfile\-format raw\fR is refused; \fBauthors.bind\fR is not served;
\fBedns\-udp\-size\fR has no effect; the \fBdnstap\-output\fR file mode and
\fBupdate\-policy local\fR are not available.
.SS Listening addresses: listen-on, listen-on-v6, bind-address, blackhole
\fBlisten\-on [ port <n> ] { <address match list>; };\fR and
\fBlisten\-on\-v6 ...\fR follow BIND 9.20 (ARM "Interfaces"): the list is
matched against the addresses of the interfaces of this host, and the server
listens (UDP and TCP) on each IPv4 address (\fBlisten\-on\fR) or IPv6 address
(\fBlisten\-on\-v6\fR) the list allows. The list is an ordinary address match
list (CIDR prefixes, \fB!\fR, \fBany\fR, \fBnone\fR, \fBlocalhost\fR,
\fBlocalnets\fR, acl names, nested lists; first match decides, see "Address
match lists"); a \fBkey\fR element is an error. A statement without \fBport\fR
uses \fBoptions { port }\fR (53 by default, wherever in \fBoptions\fR it is
written). The statements may be repeated, e.g. \fBlisten\-on { 192.0.2.1; };
listen\-on port 5353 { 198.51.100.1; };\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBany\fB and \fBnone\fB.\fR A list that is just \fBany\fR (also through an
acl block or a nested list) listens on the wildcard address (\fB0.0.0.0\fR or
\fB::\fR), as BIND does for \fBlisten\-on\-v6 { any; }\fR; BIND binds each IPv4
interface address separately for \fBlisten\-on { any; }\fR, this server uses the
wildcard there too, so an address added to the host later is served as well. Any
other list, e.g. \fB{ !192.0.2.1; any; }\fR, listens on each interface address
it allows, one socket each. \fBlisten\-on\-v6 { none; }\fR opens no IPv6 socket;
\fBlisten\-on { none; }\fR no IPv4 socket.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBInterface addresses are those of the start-up snapshot\fR (the same table
\fBlocalhost\fR / \fBlocalnets\fR use). An address in the list that no interface
has gets nothing (BIND does not listen on it either); \fBkaridns\fR and
\fBkaricheck conf\fR print a warning with the file and line (\fB[WARNING]
karidns.conf:3: options listen\-on: '192.0.2.99' matches no address of an
interface of this host: nothing is listened on for it\fR). An IPv6 address in
\fBlisten\-on\fR (or IPv4 in \fBlisten\-on\-v6\fR) is ignored with a warning, as
in BIND. IPv6 link-local interface addresses are not listened on (the snapshot
carries no scope id); use \fBany\fR or a global address.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDefaults.\fR Neither \fBlisten\-on\fR / \fBlisten\-on\-v6\fR nor
\fBbind\-address\fR: \fB0.0.0.0\fR and \fB::\fR, as before. With a
\fBlisten\-on\fR or \fBlisten\-on\-v6\fR statement, a family that is not written
anywhere (no statement, no \fBbind\-address\fR element of that family) listens
on its wildcard address, which is BIND's default ("all IPv6 interfaces"): write
\fBlisten\-on\-v6 { none; };\fR to stay off IPv6. With only \fBbind\-address\fR,
exactly the addresses listed, as before.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBUnion with \fBbind\-address\fB.\fR The addresses of all \fBlisten\-on\fR,
\fBlisten\-on\-v6\fR and \fBbind\-address\fR statements are added together (a
\fBbind\-address\fR element is at \fBoptions { port }\fR). The same address and
port twice is one socket. \fBThe same address with two different ports is a
configuration error\fR naming both places (\fBoptions listen\-on: address
192.0.2.1 is listened on at port 53 (karidns.conf:2) and at port 5353\fR): this
server gives an address one port. A wildcard address makes the specific
addresses of the same family and port redundant (\fBlisten\-on { any; };
bind\-address { 127.0.0.1; };\fR is just \fB0.0.0.0\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLimit.\fR At most 64 addresses (UDP sockets of the Frontend, notify-source
pre-binds included); more is a configuration error. \fBany\fR is one address
however many interfaces there are.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB\-p <port>\fB\fR replaces \fBoptions { port }\fR and the port of every
\fBlisten\-on\fR / \fBlisten\-on\-v6\fR statement (the command line overrides
the file, as it did when the file had one port).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNothing selected.\fR If the statements select no address (\fBlisten\-on {
none; }; listen\-on\-v6 { none; };\fR, or lists that match no interface),
\fBkaricheck conf\fR warns and \fBkaridns\fR refuses to start.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBReload.\fR The sockets are opened once, at start-up, by the Frontend (and the
workers, for TCP); a reload that changes the set of addresses or ports logs a
warning and \fBkarictl\fR shows \fBOK (restart needed for: listen\-on)\fR; the
old sockets stay and no new one is opened. The list is evaluated against the
start-up interface snapshot, so a reload does not notice new addresses either.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBkaricheck conf\fR prints the result (\fB[INFO] Listening (UDP and TCP) on:
127.0.0.1:53 [::1]:53\fR) and reports the errors above with the file and line.
.PP
\fBblackhole { <list>; };\fR (ARM "blackhole", default \fBnone\fR): a request
from an address for which the first matching element of the list is not negated
is dropped before it is read, logged or looked up in the response cache; a TCP
connection from such an address is accepted and closed at once. UDP packets from
it that are NOTIFY answers are dropped too. As the ARM warns, \fB!address;\fR
alone only exempts \fBaddress\fR and drops nothing (nothing else matches);
\fBblackhole { !192.0.2.1; any; };\fR drops everyone except 192.0.2.1. Only
requests received are affected: this server does not apply the list to the
NOTIFY messages and SOA queries it sends or the transfers it starts. A \fBkey\fR
element is an error.
.SS rate-limit { ... } (in options or zone)
A \fBrate\-limit\fR block in a zone replaces the server-wide block for that
zone. \fB0\fR means no limit for that class. Only UDP responses are limited.
.PP
Responses are counted like BIND (\fBlib/dns/rrl.c\fR): clients are grouped by
address prefix (\fBipv4\-prefix\-length\fR, \fBipv6\-prefix\-length\fR), and
within a client prefix each kind of response has its own budget per name:
.PP
.TS
lbw(35) lbw(36)
lw(35) lw(36).
\&Response	\&Counted per
_
\&NOERROR with data	T{
QNAME + QTYPE + QCLASS. Answers made from a wildcard share one budget per zone
(\fB*.<zone>\fR).
T}
\&NODATA	\&QNAME + QCLASS (wildcard: \fB*.<zone>\fR)
\&NXDOMAIN	T{
the zone (random names under one zone share one budget)
T}
\&Referral (delegation)	\&the delegation point + QCLASS
T{
Errors (SERVFAIL, REFUSED, FORMERR, ...)
T}	\&the client prefix only
\&\fBall\-per\-second\fR	T{
the client prefix only, all responses
T}
.TE
.PP
Differences from BIND: the table has a fixed size of 131072 entries
(\fBmin\-table\-size\fR / \fBmax\-table\-size\fR are accepted and ignored); when
the four slots a key can use are taken by other keys, the one used least
recently is taken over (BIND reuses the oldest entry of its table);
\fBqps\-scale\fR is accepted and ignored; \fBnxdomains\-per\-second\fR and
\fBerrors\-per\-second\fR default to \fB0\fR (no limit) instead of
\fBresponses\-per\-second\fR; responses that carry a valid server cookie are
limited like any other.
.TP 8
\fBresponses\-per\-second <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB0\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Limit for positive (NOERROR with data) responses.
.TP 8
\fBnodata\-per\-second <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR value of \fBresponses\-per\-second\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Limit for NODATA responses.
.TP 8
\fBnxdomains\-per\-second <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB0\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Limit for NXDOMAIN responses.
.TP 8
\fBreferrals\-per\-second <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR value of \fBresponses\-per\-second\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Limit for referrals (delegations to a child zone).
.TP 8
\fBerrors\-per\-second <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB0\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Limit for error responses.
.TP 8
\fBall\-per\-second <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB0\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Limit for all responses to one client prefix, whatever the
name. When both limits apply, this one decides.
.TP 8
\fBipv4\-prefix\-length <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB24\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR 0\[u2013]32. IPv4 clients in the same prefix share their
budgets.
.TP 8
\fBipv6\-prefix\-length <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB56\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR 0\[u2013]128. IPv6 clients in the same prefix share their
budgets.
.TP 8
\fBqps\-scale\fR, \fBmin\-table\-size\fR, \fBmax\-table\-size\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \[u2014]
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Accepted for BIND compatibility; no effect (warning).
.TP 8
\fBwindow <seconds>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB15\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Accounting window (maximum 3600). As in BIND 9: the budget of
a key starts at its per-second rate and never holds more than one second of
responses; every response is counted, also a limited one, so the balance goes
down to \-window \[u00D7] rate and a client that keeps sending stays limited
until it has been quiet for up to the window; a key unused for longer than the
window starts again at the rate.
.TP 8
\fBslip <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB2\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Every \fIn\fR\-th limited UDP response is sent truncated
(TC=1) instead of being dropped; \fB0\fR drops all.
.TP 8
\fBlog\-only yes|no;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBno\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Only log what would be limited; nothing is dropped, and the
dropped / slipped counters do not count it.
.TP 8
\fBearly\-drop yes|no;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBno\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR For \fBtype program\fR zones: drop UDP queries before they
are passed to the program when the client's budget for a positive answer to this
QNAME/QTYPE (or its \fBall\-per\-second\fR budget) is already exhausted.
.TP 8
\fBexempt\-clients { <addr/cidr>; ... };\fR
\fIDefault:\fR none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Clients that are never limited.
.PP
Negative or non-numeric values are ignored with a warning; unknown keys are
ignored with a warning.
.SS alerts { ... } (in options or zone; KariDNS only, 0.6.1)
Threshold alerts. The control thread reads the counters every \fBinterval\fR
seconds, works out what happened since the last check, and writes \fBone\fR line
to syslog (category \fBgeneral\fR, \fB[Control]\fR) when a value goes above its
limit and \fBone\fR line when it is back at or below it. While a value stays
above the limit nothing more is written. The alerts read counters only: the
server-wide ones (one add per response in the thread's own cache line, summed by
the control thread) and the zone counters of the zones that have an \fBalerts\fR
block. No alerts are checked unless an \fBalerts { }\fR block with at least one
limit is written. A check walks no zone table: the server's own check reads the
server-wide counters, and each zone that has an \fBalerts\fR block (kept in a
list that is built when the configuration is read) costs one lookup.
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
options {
    alerts {
        interval 60;                  // seconds between checks, 10..3600 (options only)
        qps\-above 200000;             // queries per second over the interval
        servfail\-ratio\-above 5;       // percent of the responses of the interval, 0..100
        rrl\-drops\-above 1000;         // responses dropped by rate\-limit in the interval
        transfer\-failures\-above 0;    // secondary refreshes that failed at every primary, in the interval
        reload\-failures\-above 0;      // reloads that failed or left zones unloaded, in the interval (options only)
    };
};
zone "example.test" {
    type primary;
    file "example.test.zone";
    alerts {                          // this zone alone; a name written here replaces the options value for the zone
        qps\-above 5000;
        servfail\-ratio\-above 1;
    };
};
.fi
.RE
.TP 8
\fBinterval <seconds>;\fR
\fINotes:\fR Default 60. \fBoptions\fR only. A change takes effect with the next
configuration; each reload starts the comparison again (the first check after a
start or a reload only takes the starting values).
.TP 8
\fBqps\-above <n>;\fR
\fICompared with:\fR queries / seconds of the interval
.br
\fINotes:\fR Server: all queries (answered for a zone or not). Zone: the queries
counted for that zone.
.TP 8
\fBservfail\-ratio\-above <percent>;\fR
\fICompared with:\fR SERVFAIL responses / all responses of the interval, in
percent
.br
\fINotes:\fR Fractions are allowed (\fB0.5\fR). Checked only when at least 100
responses were sent in the interval; with fewer the ratio is taken as 0.
.TP 8
\fBrrl\-drops\-above <n>;\fR
\fICompared with:\fR responses dropped by \fBrate\-limit\fR in the interval
.br
\fINotes:\fR Server: the server's total (includes drops for queries no zone
answered). Zone: the zone's count.
.TP 8
\fBtransfer\-failures\-above <n>;\fR
\fICompared with:\fR secondary refreshes in the interval that failed at every
primary asked
.br
\fINotes:\fR An answer "up to date" or "primary is behind" is not a
failure. Server: all zones.
.TP 8
\fBreload\-failures\-above <n>;\fR
\fICompared with:\fR reloads (\fBSIGHUP\fR, \fBkarictl reload\fR,
\fBreconfig\fR) that failed or loaded with zone errors
.br
\fINotes:\fR \fBoptions\fR only. Not counted: a reload postponed because readers
of the old configuration remained.
.PP
An alert is raised when the value is \fBabove\fR the limit
(\fBtransfer\-failures\-above 0\fR raises at the first failure). In the
\fBoptions\fR block the limits are the server's. In a \fBzone\fR block they
apply to that zone; a name not written there falls back to the \fBoptions\fR
value of the same name, so \fBoptions { alerts { qps\-above 100000; } }\fR with
\fBzone { alerts { servfail\-ratio\-above 1; } }\fR checks the zone for
both. Only zones with an \fBalerts\fR block (and a limit after the fallback) are
checked individually, up to 4096 of them; a database zone cannot carry an
\fBalerts\fR block (its settings text does not accept the statement). A counter
that goes back (a zone that was replaced by a reload starts at 0 again) counts
as new, so an interval right after a reload under-counts rather than being
negative.
.PP
Log lines (the limit is shown as written):
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
[Control] alert: qps\-above on server: 250031.4 queries/s above 200000
[Control] alert: servfail\-ratio\-above on zone example.test (view default): 7.20% above 1
[Control] alert cleared: qps\-above on server: 1211.0 queries/s, limit 200000
.fi
.RE
.PP
If the limit is removed from the configuration, or the zone leaves it, while an
alert is raised, one \fBalert cleared: ... no longer checked ...\fR line is
written. \fBkarictl stats\fR shows how many alerts are raised
(\fBkaridns_alerts_active\fR). \fBkaricheck conf\fR reports an unknown statement
(\fBconf.alerts.option\fR), a bad value or an \fBinterval\fR outside 10..3600
(\fBconf.alerts.range\fR) and a block with no limit (\fBconf.alerts.empty\fR).
.SS dnstap { ... } (top level or in options)
.TP 8
\fBsocket "<path>";\fR (alias \fBsocket\-path\fR)
\fIDefault:\fR none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR UNIX socket of the Frame Streams collector
(e.g. \fBfstrm_capture\fR). The first connection is made at startup. When the
collector goes away (or was not running at startup), the server reconnects
through the connect broker, first after 1 s and then with the interval doubling
up to 60 s; events produced while disconnected are dropped. The reconnection
runs as the \fBuser\fR karidns runs as, so that user must be allowed to connect
to the socket.
.TP 8
\fBidentity "<string>";\fR
\fIDefault:\fR none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR dnstap \fBidentity\fR field.
.TP 8
\fBversion "<string>";\fR
\fIDefault:\fR none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR dnstap \fBversion\fR field.
.TP 8
\fBqueue\-size <n>;\fR (alias \fBqueue_size\fR)
\fIDefault:\fR \fB4096\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Entries in each worker's dnstap ring buffer, and in the one
ring of the other threads (0\[u2013]1048576; values below 64 use the default;
rounded up to a power of two). See "Loss" below.
.TP 8
\fBrequire\-connect yes|no;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBno\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Abort startup when the collector cannot be reached (otherwise
startup continues and the server keeps trying to connect).
.TP 8
\fBlog\-queries yes|no;\fR (alias \fBauth\-query\fR)
\fIDefault:\fR see below
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Emit \fBAUTH_QUERY\fR messages.
.TP 8
\fBlog\-responses yes|no;\fR (alias \fBauth\-response\fR)
\fIDefault:\fR see below
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Emit \fBAUTH_RESPONSE\fR messages.
.PP
When neither \fBlog\-queries\fR nor \fBlog\-responses\fR is given, both queries
and responses are logged. When one of them is given, only the types set to
\fByes\fR are logged (the other one defaults to \fBno\fR). The message types are
taken over on reload; a changed \fBsocket\fR path needs a restart.
.PP
\fBAUTH_QUERY\fR and \fBAUTH_RESPONSE\fR messages carry \fBquery_zone\fR (the
zone that contains the QNAME in the client's view, looked up when the message is
sent). At shutdown the queued messages are sent and the Frame Streams session is
ended with STOP / FINISH.
.PP
\fBTransport.\fR Frame Streams <https://farsightsec.github.io/fstrm/> over a
UNIX stream socket, bidirectional: the consumer listens on \fBsocket\fR,
\fBkaridns\fR connects and sends READY with the content type
\fBprotobuf:dnstap.Dnstap\fR; an ACCEPT that lists content types must list this
one (otherwise \fBkaridns\fR logs it and does not use the connection), then
START and the data frames; at shutdown STOP, and \fBkaridns\fR waits up to 2 s
for FINISH. The run \fBuser\fR must be allowed to connect (write permission on
the socket file): a consumer that creates its socket again at every start needs
the mode set again after each start. Without the collector at start-up,
\fBkaridns\fR starts anyway and retries (1 s, doubling up to 60 s; the retries
go through the connect broker, which connects to this path only);
\fBrequire\-connect yes\fR makes start-up fail instead. The path is fixed when
the server starts.
.PP
\fBWhat a frame contains.\fR One \fBDnstap\fR message (type \fBMESSAGE\fR) per
DNS message; \fBkaridns\fR sends \fBAUTH_QUERY\fR and \fBAUTH_RESPONSE\fR only
(\fBlog\-queries\fR, \fBlog\-responses\fR). Top level: \fBidentity\fR and
\fBversion\fR when configured (absent otherwise). \fBMessage\fR: \fBtype\fR;
\fBsocket_family\fR (INET / INET6); \fBsocket_protocol\fR (UDP or TCP);
\fBquery_address\fR and \fBquery_port\fR (the client); \fBresponse_address\fR
(the server's address the query came to, when known) and \fBresponse_port\fR
(when not 0); \fBquery_zone\fR (the wire-format name of the zone that contains
the QNAME, chosen in the client's view by the sender thread when the frame is
sent, so a reload in between gives the new configuration; absent when no zone
contains the name, e.g. REFUSED, or the message has no question); for
\fBAUTH_QUERY\fR \fBquery_time_sec\fR / \fBquery_time_nsec\fR and
\fBquery_message\fR, for \fBAUTH_RESPONSE\fR \fBresponse_time_sec\fR /
\fBresponse_time_nsec\fR and \fBresponse_message\fR. An \fBAUTH_RESPONSE\fR
carries the response only (no \fBquery_message\fR, no query time): pair it with
its query by client address, port and DNS ID, or read the question in the
response. The times are the wall clock (\fBCLOCK_REALTIME\fR) when the message
was recorded: the query when it was parsed, the response when it was built. Not
filled: \fBpolicy\fR, \fBhttp_protocol\fR, \fBextra\fR. A zone transfer (AXFR,
IXFR) is one \fBAUTH_RESPONSE\fR per DNS message of the transfer. A message
recorded by a worker thread (UDP and TCP queries and responses) is cut at 4096
octets, without a mark (the DNS message ends early); \fBdnstap truncated\fR in
\fBkarictl status\fR (\fBkaridns_dnstap_truncated_total\fR) counts them. The
other threads (transfers, the responses of the asynchronous I/O threads) send up
to 65535 octets.
.PP
\fBLoss.\fR Each worker thread queues its messages in its own ring of
\fBqueue\-size\fR slots, the other threads in one shared ring of the same size;
one sender thread writes them to the socket. Messages are lost, and counted,
when:
.IP \(bu 2
a ring is full: the consumer reads more slowly than the server answers, or a
burst is longer than the ring (\fBdnstap dropped (queue full)\fR,
\fBkaridns_dnstap_dropped_total{reason="queue_full"}\fR). The sender writes
\fB[dnstap] N messages dropped, the queue was full (the collector reads too
slowly, or queue\-size is too small); M since the start\fR (category
\fBdnstap\fR, warning), at most once every 10 s while it goes on;
.IP \(bu 2
no consumer is connected: a socket that has not taken data for 2 s (\fB[dnstap]
write failed, reconnecting: collector too slow (no room in the socket for 2
s)\fR) or that failed is closed, the queued messages are discarded, and nothing
is queued until the next connection (\fBdnstap dropped (not connected)\fR,
\fBreason="not_connected"\fR). The reconnect line says how many: \fB[dnstap]
reconnected to <path>; N messages were lost while not connected\fR.
.PP
Not counted: the frames already written into the socket when the consumer went
away (the kernel buffer; which of them it read is not known), and what is still
queued when the server stops without the consumer answering. \fBdnstap
connected\fR (\fBkaridns_dnstap_connected\fR) is the link state, \fBdnstap
reconnects\fR (\fBkaridns_dnstap_reconnects_total\fR) the connections made by
the retry. A worker slot takes 4656 bytes and a slot of the shared ring 66104
bytes (\fBsizeof\fR, FreeBSD 15.1 amd64): with the default 4096 slots, 19 MB per
worker and 271 MB for the shared ring, reserved at start-up and resident as the
slots are used (the worker rings are reserved also without \fBdnstap\fR). A ring
rides out a pause of the consumer of \fBqueue\-size\fR / (messages per second of
one worker): at 50,000 queries a second, both types logged and 8 workers (about
12,500 messages a second per worker), 4096 slots last about 0.3 s. Raise
\fBqueue\-size\fR for longer pauses; a consumer that is slower on average loses
messages whatever the size.
.PP
\fBCounting.\fR With \fBlog\-queries\fR and \fBlog\-responses\fR both on (the
default) every query is two frames. A consumer that counts frames for rates or
top lists counts each query twice: count one message type, or send responses
only (\fBlog\-queries no;\fR, which also halves the frames). A query that is not
answered (a rate-limit drop) has an \fBAUTH_QUERY\fR and no \fBAUTH_RESPONSE\fR.
.PP
\fBPrivacy.\fR Frames carry the full client address and port and the whole DNS
message (the query name). Nothing is truncated or anonymised by \fBkaridns\fR:
that is the consumer's job (the client prefixes of \fBkaridns\-tapstat \-4\fR /
\fB\-6\fR are an example).
.PP
\fBConsumers.\fR \fBkaridns\-tapstat\fR(8) aggregates the stream by zone, name,
type, rcode and client prefix. Any Frame Streams receiver that reads
\fBprotobuf:dnstap.Dnstap\fR over a UNIX socket works; observed on 2026-11-13:
DNS-collector 3.3.7 on FreeBSD 15.1 took the stream without changes and fed
Prometheus and Grafana; its Prometheus output had no per-zone metric (it decodes
\fBquery_zone\fR but does not export it) and counted queries and responses
together.
.PP
The block is also read in BIND's grammar, \fBdnstap { auth; };\fR (with
\fBdnstap\-output unix "<socket>";\fR in \fBoptions\fR); see "BIND option
values". A socket path that does not fit \fBsockaddr_un.sun_path\fR (103 bytes
here) is a configuration error (\fBkaricheck conf\fR shows it); it used to be
cut, which connected another socket.
.SS ecs-tags / location-tags
.RS 2
.nf
ecs\-tags {
    tag "eu\-tier" { 198.51.100.0/24; 2001:db8:ee::/48; };
    tag "us\-tier" { 203.0.113.0/24; };
};
.fi
.RE
.PP
The same syntax is used for \fBlocation\-tags\fR. The blocks can be placed in
\fBoptions\fR and in \fBzone\fR. For each zone, tags defined inside the zone
file (\fB$ECS\-SUBNET\-TAG\fR / \fB$LOCATION\-TAG\fR) take precedence, then the
\fBzone\fR block, then \fBoptions\fR. Tags are checked in the order they are
defined and the first tag with a matching CIDR wins. ECS tags steer the Answer
section only: Additional-section addresses and the NS / DS RRsets of the
Authority section are chosen as for a query without ECS (RFC 7871 \[u00A7]7.3.1,
\[u00A7]7.4); location tags apply everywhere. See the Client Geolocation &
Subnet Steering Guide.
.SS logging { ... }
The BIND 9.20 \fBlogging\fR statement (ARM "logging Block Grammar"), with the
same meaning: \fBchannels\fR say where a line goes and how it looks,
\fBcategories\fR say which messages go to which channels.
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
logging {
    channel <name> {
        ( file "<path>" [ versions ( <n> | unlimited ) ] [ size <size> ] [ suffix ( increment | timestamp ) ]
        | syslog [ <facility> ]
        | stderr
        | null );
        severity ( critical | error | warning | notice | info | dynamic | debug [ <n> ] );
        print\-time ( yes | no | local | iso8601 | iso8601\-utc );
        print\-category <yes|no>;
        print\-severity <yes|no>;
        buffered <yes|no>;
        max\-qps <n>;                       // KariDNS: the limit of the query log lines per second through this channel
    };
    category <name> { <channel>; ... };
};
.fi
.RE
.PP
Every channel has exactly one destination (\fBfile\fR, \fBsyslog\fR,
\fBstderr\fR or \fBnull\fR); \fBfile\fR takes its options in any order, each
once. \fBseverity\fR defaults to \fBinfo\fR. A line is \fB<time> <category>:
<severity>: <text>\fR, each part when the channel asks for it (\fBprint\-time\fR
\fByes\fR or \fBlocal\fR: \fB28\-Oct\-2026 12:34:56.789\fR; \fBiso8601\fR:
\fB2026\-10\-28T12:34:56.789\fR in local time without a zone, as BIND writes it;
\fBiso8601\-utc\fR: \fB2026\-10\-28T03:34:56.789Z\fR). Control characters in a
message (text that came from the network, such as a key name) are written as
\fB?\fR, so a message cannot start a line of its own. A statement that is not
\fBchannel\fR or \fBcategory\fR, an option a channel does not have, a value that
is not valid, a channel with no or two destinations, a category name that BIND
9.20 does not have and a channel name that is not defined (or built in) are
configuration errors with the file and line, in \fBkaridns\fR (start and reload)
and in \fBkaricheck conf\fR.
.PP
\fBBuilt-in channels\fR (usable in a \fBcategory\fR without being defined, as in
BIND): \fBdefault_syslog\fR (syslog, facility \fBdaemon\fR, \fBinfo\fR),
\fBdefault_stderr\fR (standard error, \fBinfo\fR, with the time),
\fBdefault_debug\fR and \fBnull\fR. A channel of the file with the name of a
built-in channel replaces it (a warning says so). A channel defined twice: the
later definition is used (a warning). A syslog line is also printed on standard
error (\fBopenlog(3)\fR with \fBLOG_PERROR\fR: \fBKariDNS <pid> \- \-
<text>\fR), which is how a foreground server shows its messages; with
\fBKARIDNS_NOSYSLOG=1\fR (ENVIRONMENT, a test hook) it goes there only.
.PP
\fB\fBdefault_debug\fB and \fBkaridns.run\fB.\fR As BIND's (\fBfile "named.run";
severity dynamic;\fR with the time, in the working directory),
\fBdefault_debug\fR is the file \fB\fBkaridns.run\fB in \fBoptions { directory
}\fB\fR, \fBseverity dynamic\fR with the time (\fBprint\-time yes\fR, no
category, no severity), and it writes \fBonly while the debug level is above
0\fR (BIND's \fBISC_LOG_DEBUGONLY\fR): nothing at level 0, not even an error,
and from level 1 on every message up to the level (errors, warnings, notices,
info and the debug lines). Differences from BIND:
.IP \(bu 2
BIND creates \fBnamed.run\fR when it first writes to it. The sandboxed process
cannot create a file after the start, so KariDNS opens \fBkaridns.run\fR
(creating it empty, appending when it is there) \fBat every start\fR, before the
sandbox, like the other log files, and hands it to \fBuser\fR / \fBgroup\fR;
\fBkarictl trace\fR then works without a \fBlogging\fR statement. Unlike the
other log files it is opened without keeping a descriptor of its directory:
\fBoptions { directory }\fR holds the configuration, the zone files, the
loader's state and SQLite files, and the sandboxed process holds no writable
descriptor of it. A reload keeps the descriptor of the start (the file is never
rolled, and its path changes only with \fBdirectory\fR, which needs a restart);
a reload that needs \fBkaridns.run\fR when the start did not open it (no
category wrote to \fBdefault_debug\fR then) leaves \fBdefault_debug\fR writing
nothing, with the warning below, until a restart. It is opened only when some
category writes to \fBdefault_debug\fR: a category without channels while there
is no \fBcategory default\fR (so with no \fBlogging\fR statement at all), or a
category that names it; when every category has channels without it (a
\fBcategory default\fR that does not name it, say), or a channel of the file is
named \fBdefault_debug\fR, \fBkaridns.run\fR is left alone. It is never rolled
(no \fBversions\fR / \fBsize\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
Without \fBoptions { directory }\fR, \fBdefault_debug\fR writes nothing and no
\fBkaridns.run\fR is made (a server started from \fB/\fR by rc(8) would
otherwise write \fB/karidns.run\fR). Define a channel with \fBseverity
dynamic\fR to see debug output there.
.IP \(bu 2
A \fBkaridns.run\fR that cannot be opened (a directory of that name, a symbolic
link, a file refused for the reasons under \fBDirectories and files\fR, or, at a
reload, one the start did not open) is not an error, as in BIND: \fB[Logging]
The debug file '...' (built\-in channel default_debug) is not used: <reason>;
the debug output goes only to the channels of the logging statement\fR
(warning), \fBdefault_debug\fR writes nothing until a restart can open it, and
the start or the reload goes on.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBkaricheck conf\fR reads the configuration and creates nothing.
.IP \(bu 2
The supervisor, the routers and the connect broker write to \fBkaridns.run\fR
through the descriptor the start opened, never by its name (so they never create
it, nor reach another file of \fBdirectory\fR): when it was removed by hand, the
lines go nowhere until a restart makes it again. \fBkarictl trace\fR and
\fBnotrace\fR change the level of every process of the server (0.7; see
\fBFiles\fR below).
.PP
\fBCategories.\fR The names are those of BIND 9.20.4 (\fBdefault\fR,
\fBgeneral\fR, \fBconfig\fR, \fBxfer\-in\fR, \fBxfer\-out\fR, \fBnotify\fR,
\fBupdate\fR, \fBupdate\-security\fR, \fBsecurity\fR, \fBnetwork\fR,
\fBqueries\fR, \fBresponses\fR, \fBquery\-errors\fR, \fBclient\fR,
\fBdispatch\fR, \fBdnssec\fR, \fBlame\-servers\fR, \fBrate\-limit\fR,
\fBzoneload\fR, \fBdatabase\fR, \fBdnstap\fR, \fBnsid\fR, \fBrpz\fR,
\fBrpz\-passthru\fR, \fBcname\fR, \fBspill\fR, \fBserve\-stale\fR,
\fBedns\-disabled\fR, \fBresolver\fR, \fBtrust\-anchor\-telemetry\fR,
\fBsslkeylog\fR, \fBunmatched\fR). A category with no \fBcategory\fR statement
(or only an empty list) writes to the channels of \fBcategory default\fR; with
no \fBdefault\fR category either, to \fBdefault_syslog\fR and
\fBdefault_debug\fR (BIND ARM: \fBcategory default { default_syslog;
default_debug; };\fR): that is what a configuration without \fBlogging\fR
does. At debug level 0 this is what KariDNS always did (syslog, facility
\fBdaemon\fR); since 0.7 \fBdefault_debug\fR adds \fBkaridns.run\fR above level
0. Several statements for one category add their channels (BIND appends), a
category may list up to 8 channels, and a line goes to every channel whose
severity admits it.
.PP
The messages of the server carry a \fB[Prefix]\fR (the part of the program they
come from); the prefix selects the category:
.TP 8
\fBconfig\fR
Reading and reloading the configuration (\fB[Config]\fR).
.TP 8
\fBxfer\-in\fR
Inbound AXFR / IXFR and the SOA checks of a secondary zone (\fB[AXFR]\fR,
\fB[IXFR]\fR, \fB[XFR]\fR).
.TP 8
\fBxfer\-out\fR
Outbound transfers: start and end with the statistics, falls back from IXFR to
AXFR, the IXFR over UDP, refused and bad requests, quota, aborted transfers, and
the older messages (a record that does not fit a message, settings that cannot
be sent, Extended AXFR that is refused); see Event messages below.
.TP 8
\fBquery\-errors\fR
Why a query was answered with SERVFAIL, FORMERR, REFUSED, NOTIMP or NOTAUTH (see
Event messages below).
.TP 8
\fBnotify\fR
NOTIFY sent, answered, retried, failed (\fB[Notify]\fR).
.TP 8
\fBupdate\fR
Dynamic updates applied or aborted (\fB[Update]\fR), and why an UPDATE for a
zone of the server was not applied (\fBupdating zone 'z/IN': update
failed: ... (RCODE)\fR, see Event messages below).
.TP 8
\fBupdate\-security\fR
Who may update: \fBsigner "k" approved|denied\fR, \fBupdate 'z/IN' denied\fR,
\fBupdate forwarding 'z/IN' denied\fR (see Event messages below).
.TP 8
\fBsecurity\fR
TSIG errors (\fB[TSIG]\fR), DNS cookie problems (\fB[Cookie]\fR).
.TP 8
\fBdnssec\fR
A signed zone that cannot give the NSEC or NSEC3 proof a negative, wildcard or
referral answer needs (RFC 4035 3.1.3, 3.1.4; RFC 5155 7.2): \fB[DNSSEC]
zone 'example.test': no NSEC3 that matches the closest encloser (RFC 5155 7.2.2)
for '...': the answer goes out without that proof ...\fR, at warning, at most
once a minute per zone (0.7). The answer itself is unchanged. Reported: with
NSEC, the NSEC that covers the name and the one that covers the wildcard (name
error), the NSEC of the name or the one that covers an empty non-terminal (no
data), the NSEC that proves no closer match of a wildcard answer and of every
wildcard CNAME on the way, the NSEC of the wildcard owner (wildcard no data),
the NSEC of an insecure delegation (referral); with NSEC3, the NSEC3 that
matches the closest provable encloser, the one that covers the next closer name
and the one that covers the wildcard (name error), the NSEC3 of the name (no
data; at an empty non-terminal that only insecure delegations in an Opt-Out span
make, which has no NSEC3 (RFC 5155 7.1), RFC 5155 7.2.3 cannot be met literally
and the answer carries the closest provable encloser proof of 7.2.1 instead, as
BIND 9.20 does: its NSEC3 and the Opt-Out cover of the next closer name are
reported when missing), the closest provable encloser proof of a DS query at,
and a referral to, an insecure delegation that has no NSEC3 of its own (also
when the cover of the next closer name lacks the Opt-Out flag), the NSEC3 that
covers the next closer name of a wildcard answer and of every wildcard CNAME,
and the closest encloser proof and the NSEC3 of the wildcard (wildcard no
data). Not reported: a wildcard that exists at the closest provable encloser of
a name error (it cannot be covered).
.TP 8
\fBnetwork\fR
Socket options and buffer sizes (\fB[Network]\fR).
.TP 8
\fBzoneload\fR
Loading zone files and building zone images (\fB[Zone]\fR, \fB[ZoneLoader]\fR,
\fB[Image]\fR, \fB[tinydns]\fR).
.TP 8
\fBdatabase\fR
The database zones (\fB[DB]\fR), and the messages of \fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR
(read from its standard error by the supervisor, see karidns-dbloader(8)
MESSAGES).
.TP 8
\fBrate\-limit\fR
Response rate limiting (\fB[RRL]\fR).
.TP 8
\fBdnstap\fR
The dnstap output (\fB[dnstap]\fR).
.TP 8
\fBqueries\fR
The query log, one line per query.
.TP 8
\fBresponses\fR
The response log (KariDNS; BIND 9.20 has a category of that name too).
.TP 8
\fBgeneral\fR
Everything else: start-up and shut-down, the processes (\fB[Manager]\fR,
\fB[Backend]\fR, \fB[Frontend]\fR, \fB[Broker]\fR, \fB[IPC]\fR), \fBkarictl\fR
commands (\fB[Control]\fR), catalog zones, ALIAS, forwarding, program zones, an
expired zone, the log files themselves (\fB[Logging]\fR).
.TP 8
\fBclient\fR
The debug 3 line of each query, NOTIFY and UPDATE (0.7, see \fBDebug
messages\fR).
.PP
\fBSeverities and the debug level.\fR Severities are those of BIND: critical,
error, warning, notice, info, \fBdynamic\fR and \fBdebug [<n>]\fR. The \fBdebug
level in force\fR (0\[u2013]99, 0 at the start) is set by \fBkaridns \-d
<level>\fR and changed with \fBkarictl trace\fR (one more, up to 99), \fBkarictl
trace <level>\fR and \fBkarictl notrace\fR (0), as \fBnamed \-d\fR and \fBrndc
trace\fR / \fBnotrace\fR; each change is logged as \fB[Control] debug level is
now N\fR (category \fBgeneral\fR, notice) and \fBkarictl status\fR shows
\fBdebug level: N\fR. The channels follow BIND's \fBisc_log_doit()\fR:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBseverity dynamic\fR passes the messages up to the level in force: critical to
info at level 0, plus \fBdebug 1\fR .. \fBdebug N\fR at level N.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBseverity debug <n>\fR is a fixed level: it passes up to \fBdebug n\fR
whatever the level in force (at level 0 too, as in BIND: a channel of the file
has no "debug only" flag), and \fBseverity info\fR (the default) never passes a
debug message.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdefault_debug\fR (\fBkaridns.run\fR, above) is \fBdynamic\fR and writes
nothing at level 0.
.PP
Changing the level opens no file (a channel must exist at the start, or come
with a reload under the rules of \fBFiles\fR) and costs nothing on the query
path: the event messages below test one atomic value, recomputed when the level
changes, before they build any text, and a debug message that no channel passes
is not formatted. The event messages use \fBdebug 1\fR .. \fBdebug 4\fR where
BIND does. The two messages that were \fBdebug\fR in earlier versions (a NOTIFY
that was answered, a zone file left alone by \fBreconfig\fR because its mtime is
unchanged) are \fBinfo\fR now, which is what \fBdefault_syslog\fR passes. What
the debug levels show is under \fBDebug messages\fR below.
.PP
\fBDebug messages (0.7).\fR Raising the level explains what the server
decided. The levels follow BIND's use of them:
.PP
.TS
lb lbw(66)
l lw(66).
\&Level	\&What it shows
_
\&1	T{
One line per decision about a zone, a reload, a peer or a process: why a zone
file is read again or left alone, which primary is asked and what came of it,
why a NOTIFY was taken or refused, which rule allowed or refused a transfer or
an UPDATE, what became of each zone of a database batch, why a process waits
before it is started again.
T}
\&2	T{
The steps of a longer operation: a reload, a zone snapshot published, old zone
versions freed, the steps of an incoming transfer and of an UPDATE, the fetches
of database zones (asked, held back, done), a zone taken out of memory, the
destinations of a NOTIFY, socket buffer sizes.
T}
\&3	T{
One line per message: every query, NOTIFY and UPDATE with the view and the zone
it was routed to and its RCODE (\fBclient\fR), every TSIG check and DNS cookie
decision (\fBsecurity\fR), every message of a transfer (\fBxfer\-in\fR,
\fBxfer\-out\fR), every response RRL dropped or slipped (\fBrate\-limit\fR),
every control command (\fBgeneral\fR), TCP connections accepted or closed at the
limit (\fBnetwork\fR).
T}
\&10	T{
One line per record: each record of an incoming transfer (\fBxfer\-in\fR) or of
the loader's stream (\fBdatabase\fR) as it is taken.
T}
.TE
.TP 8
\fBgeneral\fR
1: the zone entries a reload makes (new zones, type changes), per view the zones
kept, new, carried-over catalog members and database zones; catalog members that
leave and the count of a catalog change; the processes the manager started; a
plugin's restart backoff and a stopped plugin reaped; an ALIAS target that
failed and its next try. 2: the steps of a reload (parsed, published with its
generation, zones rebuilt with the time and the failures), a zone snapshot
published, the freeing of retired zone versions, a scan of the zones for ALIAS
records, an ALIAS refresh and the next one. 3: a control command as it was taken
apart and what the key's permissions said.
.TP 8
\fBconfig\fR
1: an \fBinclude\fR read (its path and size, never its text). 2: \fBoptions {
directory }\fR and where \fBdefault_debug\fR writes.
.TP 8
\fBzoneload\fR
1: why a primary zone's file is read again (a full reload, a changed mtime or
size), and each load: format, records, serial before and after, time taken,
whether a NOTIFY follows. 2: each \fB$INCLUDE\fR read.
.TP 8
\fBxfer\-in\fR
1: the primary asked (its place in the list, IXFR or AXFR and why, our serial,
the key's name, the Extended AXFR version), the SOA query of \fBrequest\-ixfr
no\fR and its serial comparison, an up-to-date primary, the summary of a refresh
(asked, transferred from, up to date, behind, failed, deferred by quota). 2: a
transfer complete (messages, octets, records, serial) before it is installed. 3:
each message (octets, answer RRs, TSIG verified or unsigned). 10: each record.
.TP 8
\fBxfer\-out\fR
1: the \fBallow\-transfer\fR decision and the rule that made it (an element of
the list, the zone's \fBtsig\-key\fR, \fBtransfer\-acl\-match\fR, a database
zone's ACL and \fBacl\-ceiling\fR) with the key's name; what the answer will be
(the client's and our serial, IXFR, the SOA alone, AXFR, the differences used,
the format). 3: each message sent.
.TP 8
\fBnotify\fR
1: a received NOTIFY accepted or refused and why (from a primary of the zone or
of its catalog zone, \fBallow\-notify\fR, no zone); a NOTIFY not sent (\fBnotify
no\fR, \fBprimary\-only\fR for a secondary); the destinations of a NOTIFY. 2:
each NOTIFY sent (address, UDP or TCP, the key's name).
.TP 8
\fBupdate\fR
1: the rule that decides an UPDATE (\fBallow\-update\fR, \fBupdate\-policy\fR, a
secondary). 2: the result of the prerequisites (RFC 2136 \[u00A7]3.2.5) and of
the update section, the serial published.
.TP 8
\fBsecurity\fR
1: a failed control channel authentication. 3: each TSIG check (key name,
algorithm, view, result, Time Signed, Fudge and the difference to our clock),
each server cookie taken or not (length, age, the number of the
\fBcookie\-secret\fR that made it), a control connection authenticated.
.TP 8
\fBdatabase\fR
1: each zone of a batch (installed, version taken with the data out of memory,
hidden, unchanged, rejected), how long the loader ran and how long the next
start waits. 2: each fetch (asked, done; a request that waits for a place, a
token or room, 0.7: it is not logged per zone, see \fBqueued=\fR of
\fBfetchq:\fR; one that is refused because the wait queue is full, or a
background request that is not made), a zone taken out of memory by
\fBmemory\-limit\fR, and the loader's own lines
(\fBkaridns\-dbloader(8)\fR). 10: each record of the loader's stream.
.TP 8
\fBnetwork\fR
1: each UDP listener of a Frontend router. 2: the socket buffer sizes the system
gave. 3: a TCP connection accepted, or closed at once because 1000 are open.
.TP 8
\fBrate\-limit\fR
3: each response RRL dropped or slipped (\fB[RRL] response class N:
dropped|slipped\fR).
.TP 8
\fBclient\fR
3: each query, NOTIFY and UPDATE: its name and type, whether it was signed, the
view and the zone it was routed to, and the RCODE of the answer.
.PP
A debug message never contains a TSIG secret or MAC, the control channel's
secret or the HMAC of its challenge, a \fBcookie\-secret\fR or a whole cookie, a
database password or the text of a conninfo file, or a plugin's
\fBprogram\-args\fR: only names (of keys, zones, views, files) and
numbers. \fBtests/run_log_secrets_test.sh\fR runs the server at \fB\-d 99\fR
with canary values in all of these and fails when one appears in any channel,
\fBkaridns.run\fR or syslog. At level 0 the query path pays one atomic load for
the \fBclient\fR line of a query and nothing else for the debug messages (the
TSIG and cookie lines are looked at only for a signed request or one with a
server cookie).
.PP
Measured for 0.7 (1,000,000 zones from SQLite3 without \fBmemory\-limit\fR, a
server with 8 virtual CPUs, dnsperf on another host, uniform names, 20 seconds a
step): at 200,000 queries a second 100.00% of the queries are answered at every
level (at most 29 of 4,000,000 lost), with an average latency of 0.6 ms at level
0 (0.6.2: 0.6 ms), 0.6 ms at \fB\-d 1\fR, 0.7 ms at \fB\-d 3\fR and 0.8 ms at
\fB\-d 10\fR. At 400,000 queries a second, which is the limit of the sending
host, 99.87% to 99.92% are answered at level 0 (0.6.2: 99.87% to 99.90%), 99.89%
at \fB\-d 1\fR, 99.78% at \fB\-d 3\fR and 99.83% at \fB\-d 10\fR; the average
latency is 7.2 to 7.8 ms throughout. Level 3 adds a \fBclient\fR line for a
query, which the debug budget of the category holds to 1000 lines a second.
.PP
The other categories are accepted (a configuration written for BIND works) but
this authoritative server has nothing to write to them: \fBdispatch\fR,
\fBlame\-servers\fR, \fBnsid\fR, \fBrpz\fR, \fBrpz\-passthru\fR, \fBcname\fR,
\fBspill\fR, \fBserve\-stale\fR, \fBedns\-disabled\fR, \fBresolver\fR,
\fBtrust\-anchor\-telemetry\fR, \fBsslkeylog\fR, \fBunmatched\fR.
.PP
\fBEvent messages (\fBupdate\-security\fB, \fBupdate\fB, \fBxfer\-out\fB,
\fBquery\-errors\fB).\fR These categories carry messages about what clients ask
of the server, in the form BIND writes them (\fBlib/ns/update.c\fR,
\fBxfrout.c\fR, \fBquery.c\fR): "client <address>#<port>: <text>". They arise on
the query path, so they do not write anything there: a worker thread of the
Backend puts a fixed-size record into its own \fBevent ring\fR (0.7: a ring
apart from the one of the query log, \fBcategory queries\fR, so that these
records never take room from the query log; before 0.7 both shared one ring),
and the query logger thread formats it and writes it to the channels of the
category; a message from any other thread (a transfer thread, the asynchronous
resolver threads) is formatted and written at once. Consequences:
.IP \(bu 2
A category costs nothing while no channel passes the message: whether some
non-null channel of the category (or of \fBdefault\fR, or \fBdefault_syslog\fR
and \fBdefault_debug\fR when there is none) admits the level is decided when the
configuration is applied and when the debug level changes, and the sites test it
with one atomic load before they build a text. With no \fBlogging\fR statement
the messages go to \fBdefault_syslog\fR at \fBinfo\fR, as BIND's do (and to
\fBkaridns.run\fR above level 0); a message at a debug level (BIND logs the
reasons of \fBquery\-errors\fR at \fBdebug 1\fR and \fBdebug 3\fR) is written to
a channel with \fBseverity debug <n>\fR where n is at least the level of the
message, to a \fBseverity dynamic\fR channel while the debug level in force is
at least the level of the message, and (with \fBoptions { directory }\fR) to
\fBkaridns.run\fR likewise.
.IP \(bu 2
Every message can be caused by a client, so each category has a rate limit:
\fBupdate\-security\fR 10 per second, \fBupdate\fR 20, \fBquery\-errors\fR 20,
\fBxfer\-out\fR 50, \fBsecurity\fR (the refused transfers) 20, every other
category 20. The messages over the limit are not written; when the second is
over one line reports them, \fB<N> message(s) suppressed by the rate limit of
<L> per second\fR, at the level of the last one dropped. (BIND logs every one of
them.)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTwo budgets per category\fR (0.7): the lines at \fBcritical\fR .. \fBinfo\fR
and the debug lines (\fBdebug 1\fR and up) are counted apart, each with the
limit of the category, and each budget reports its own suppressed lines (a
report of debug lines is written at a debug level, one of the others at
\fBinfo\fR .. \fBcritical\fR). A channel with \fBseverity debug <n>\fR receives
the debug lines at debug level 0 as well, so without the split the debug lines
of 0.7 (in \fBxfer\-out\fR one or two per transfer request) would take the place
of the notices and errors an operator watches; now a debug line never does. A
category whose lines are all of one kind (\fBquery\-errors\fR without the query
log: all debug) is limited exactly as in 0.6; one with both kinds may write the
limit of each.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBWhile the debug level in force is above 0\fR every category may write \fB1000
lines a second\fR (0.7) in each of its two budgets, so that a debug session at
level 3 sees one line per message; the lines over it are still counted and
reported the same way, and at \fBkarictl notrace\fR the limits above are
back. The number is held to what a worker's event ring and the query logger
thread can carry: an event ring has 1024 records at the least (an eighth of
\fBquery\-log\-buffer\-size\fR, 4096 by default) and is emptied every 1 ms while
there is work and every 10 ms when idle; 1000 lines a second in each of a dozen
busy categories is some 120 records per 10 ms and some 12000 lines a second for
the logger thread. A channel with \fBseverity debug <n>\fR and a \fBsize\fR
limit (0.6 configurations have them) now also receives the debug lines of 0.7 at
debug level 0, so it reaches its \fBsize\fR sooner: with \fBversions\fR it rolls
over sooner, without \fBversions\fR it stops taking lines sooner (the notices
and errors after that point are not written to it either); give such a channel a
lower \fBdebug <n>\fR, a larger \fBsize\fR or \fBversions\fR if that matters.
.IP \(bu 2
The event ring is for these records only (0.7, K-11): a debug flood fills at
most the event ring, never the query log's, so it can never bring the query log
to the 80% at which it stops itself. Inside the event ring a debug record (level
1 and up) may take at most half of the slots, so that the other half is always
there for the lines at \fBinfo\fR and above; a record that finds no room is
dropped and counted (a worker never waits for the log), and the query logger
thread reports the count (\fB[QueryLog] Dropped N event log messages ...\fR,
category \fBgeneral\fR) every five seconds, like the dropped queries. The logger
thread empties, worker by worker, the event ring and then the query log ring,
each up to what was in it when it got there: neither waits for the other for
more than one pass, and a line at \fBwarning\fR waits at most behind the debug
records already in its ring (half of it at most), never behind query log
records. The other threads (transfer, asynchronous resolver and control threads)
write their messages at once and have no ring; the response log and dnstap have
rings of their own as before.
.IP \(bu 2
Names that came from the network are written in their presentation form: a byte
outside \fB0x21..0x7e\fR as \fB\eDDD\fR, a control character that is left
becomes \fB?\fR; a line is at most 255 bytes of text.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBupdate\-security\fR: \fBsigner "k" approved|denied\fR for a signed request
(\fBinfo\fR), then for a denial \fBupdate 'z/IN' denied\fR (\fBerror\fR when the
zone has an update ACL -- \fBallow\-update\fR, \fBupdate\-acl\-match\fR,
\fBupdate\-policy\fR -- and \fBinfo\fR when it has none), as BIND's
\fBcheckupdateacl()\fR. A secondary zone gives \fBupdate forwarding 'z/IN'
denied: this server does not forward updates to the primary\fR (BIND forwards or
refuses by \fBallow\-update\-forwarding\fR; KariDNS never
forwards). \fBsigner ... approved\fR is logged before the update is applied. A
request whose TSIG does not verify is answered NOTAUTH before this point and
logged by the \fBsecurity\fR category.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBxfer\-out\fR (BIND \fBlib/ns/xfrout.c\fR; the client is \fBclient
<address>#<port>\fR, the text starts \fBtransfer of 'zone/IN': \fR):
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAXFR started[: TSIG <key>] (serial N)\fR, \fBIXFR started[: TSIG <key>]
(serial A \-> B)\fR, \fBAXFR\-style IXFR started ...\fR (an IXFR answered with
the whole zone), \fBExtended AXFR started ...\fR (the KariDNS extension), and
when the last message has been sent \fB<mnemonic> ended: <n> messages, <n>
records, <n> bytes, <s>.<ms> secs (<n> bytes/sec) (serial N)\fR, all
\fBinfo\fR. These are written by the transfer thread (a transfer is not on the
query path).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBIXFR version not in journal, falling back to AXFR\fR (\fBinfo\fR; also when
the history has a gap), \fBIXFR falling back to AXFR: <reason>\fR (the zone has
data only an Extended AXFR can carry, records that depend on the client, or
differences with several or restricted SOA records), and at \fBdebug 4\fR
\fBIXFR delta response disabled due to 'provide\-ixfr no;' being set, sending an
AXFR\-style IXFR\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
A client that is up to date: \fBIXFR poll up to date (serial N)\fR at \fBdebug
1\fR (BIND does too; also when the zone is out of memory and the answer comes
from the summary of the directory).
.IP \(bu 2
The IXFR over UDP (RFC 1995 4.2): \fBIXFR over UDP: serial A is behind B,
answered with the SOA and TC=1 (the client retries over TCP)\fR (\fBinfo\fR; the
transfer itself follows over TCP and is logged as above).
.IP \(bu 2
Refusals (every line names the class of the request, \fBIN\fR or e.g. \fBCH\fR
for a refused transfer of another class): \fBzone transfer 'zone/AXFR/IN'
denied\fR (\fBerror\fR, over TCP and UDP, \fBIXFR\fR for an IXFR) goes to
category \fBsecurity\fR, as in BIND, which logs it through the ACL check; the
others stay in \fBxfer\-out\fR: \fBbad zone transfer request: non\-authoritative
zone (REFUSED)\fR, \fB... TSIG verification failed (NOTAUTH)\fR, \fB... the TSIG
record cannot be parsed (FORMERR)\fR, \fB... unsupported EDNS version
(BADVERS)\fR, \fB... attempted AXFR over UDP (FORMERR)\fR, \fB... malformed
request, <reason> (FORMERR)\fR (\fBinfo\fR; the reason is one of: \fBOPCODE is
not QUERY\fR, \fBRCODE is not NOERROR\fR, \fBQDCOUNT is not 1\fR, \fBmalformed
question\fR, \fBthe question is not AXFR or IXFR\fR, \fBANCOUNT is not 0\fR,
\fBunexpected NSCOUNT\fR, \fBthe IXFR Authority record is not a SOA\fR,
\fBmalformed or unexpected Additional record\fR, \fBtrailing octets after the
last record\fR, \fBrequest too long\fR, \fBQR is set\fR, \fBshorter than a DNS
header\fR; an IXFR request without the client's SOA is \fBunexpected NSCOUNT\fR
or \fBthe IXFR Authority record is not a SOA\fR), \fBAXFR request denied: quota
reached (transfers\-out N)\fR (\fBwarning\fR, as BIND), \fBrequest denied:
cannot start a thread for the transfer\fR / \fBout of memory\fR (\fBerror\fR),
\fBrequest denied: the zone has no data yet (SERVFAIL, Not Ready)\fR and
\fBrequest failed: the zone data could not be read in time (SERVFAIL, Not
Ready)\fR (\fBwarning\fR; the zone of a database is out of memory and could not
be read), \fBrequest denied: the zone is not served to this client (REFUSED)\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
A transfer that cannot go on: \fB<mnemonic> aborted: <reason> (<n> messages, <n>
records, <n> bytes were sent)\fR (\fBerror\fR): the client closed the connection
or a limit of the transfer (\fBmax\-transfer\-time\-out\fR,
\fBmax\-transfer\-idle\-out\fR) was reached, a message could not be signed, a
record is too large.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fBquery\-errors\fR (BIND \fBlib/ns/query.c\fR \fBlog_queryerror()\fR): \fBquery
failed (<RCODE>) for <name>/<class>/<type>: <reason>\fR. The reason is the text
of the Extended DNS Error the answer carries (\fBQuery refused due to access
control\fR, \fBThis server is not authoritative for the queried zone\fR, \fBZone
expired (SOA EXPIRE exceeded)\fR, \fBZone not ready (empty)\fR, \fBInvalid
TSIG\fR, \fBThis opcode is not supported by this server\fR ...; the text is used
also when \fBsend\-extended\-errors\fR is off and the client does not see it),
or what the server found wrong with a malformed query (\fBthe question count is
not valid for this opcode (RFC 9619)\fR, \fBa query without a question and
without a COOKIE option (RFC 9619, RFC 7873 5.4)\fR, \fBthe question name is
compressed (RFC 1035 4.1.4)\fR, \fBthe question is cut short (no QTYPE and
QCLASS)\fR, \fBmalformed COOKIE option (RFC 7873 5.2.2)\fR, \fBmalformed EDNS
Client Subnet option (RFC 7871 6)\fR, \fBinvalid MQTYPE\-Query option ...\fR,
\fBthe OPT record cannot be used ...\fR, \fBthe TSIG record cannot be
parsed\fR). A query whose question cannot be read is \fB\-/\-/\-\fR. SERVFAIL is
logged at \fBdebug 1\fR, the other results at \fBdebug 3\fR (BIND's levels), and
everything at \fBinfo\fR while the query log is on (BIND:
\fBNS_SERVER_LOGQUERIES\fR), so an operator who wants them writes a channel with
\fBseverity debug 3;\fR (or turns \fBquerylog yes\fR on). UPDATE, NOTIFY and the
transfers are not query errors (their own categories); an answer that was
dropped (RRL, a message too short to have a header) has no line. UDP, TCP, TLS
and DoH go through the same code.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBupdate\fR (besides the \fB[Update]\fR lines): \fBupdate failed: <zone>: not
authoritative for update zone (NOTAUTH)\fR, \fBupdate failed: zone section is
not of type SOA (FORMERR)\fR, and for the zone's own UPDATEs \fBupdating
zone 'z/IN': update failed: <reason> (RCODE)\fR with the reasons \fBrejected by
secure update (REFUSED): <name> <type>\fR (the first change that
\fBupdate\-policy\fR does not grant), \fBtoo many records in the update
message\fR, \fBprerequisite not satisfied
(NXRRSET|YXRRSET|YXDOMAIN|NXDOMAIN)\fR, \fBname outside the zone (NOTZONE)\fR
and \fBmalformed update message (FORMERR)\fR; all at \fBinfo\fR, as BIND's
\fBupdate_log()\fR.
.PP
Examples (client \fB192.0.2.7\fR or \fB2001:db8::7\fR, zone \fBexample.test\fR):
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
28\-Oct\-2026 12:34:56.789 update\-security: info: client 192.0.2.7#40321: signer "updkey" approved
28\-Oct\-2026 12:34:57.001 update\-security: error: client 192.0.2.7#40322: update 'example.test/IN' denied
28\-Oct\-2026 12:34:57.002 update\-security: info: client 192.0.2.9#51022: update forwarding 'sec.example.test/IN' denied: this server does not forward updates to the primary
28\-Oct\-2026 12:34:58.004 update: info: client 192.0.2.7#40330: updating zone 'example.test/IN': update failed: rejected by secure update (REFUSED): www.example.test TXT
28\-Oct\-2026 12:34:58.120 update: info: client 192.0.2.7#40331: update failed: other.example.test: not authoritative for update zone (NOTAUTH)
28\-Oct\-2026 12:35:00.000 update\-security: error: 540 message(s) suppressed by the rate limit of 10 per second
28\-Oct\-2026 12:40:01.100 xfer\-out: info: client 192.0.2.7#40501: transfer of 'example.test/IN': AXFR started: TSIG xfer\-key (serial 2026102801)
28\-Oct\-2026 12:40:01.350 xfer\-out: info: client 192.0.2.7#40501: transfer of 'example.test/IN': AXFR ended: 3 messages, 21000 records, 1312000 bytes, 0.249 secs (5269076 bytes/sec) (serial 2026102801)
28\-Oct\-2026 12:41:00.020 xfer\-out: info: client 2001:db8::7#51311: transfer of 'example.test/IN': IXFR version not in journal, falling back to AXFR
28\-Oct\-2026 12:41:00.021 xfer\-out: info: client 2001:db8::7#51311: transfer of 'example.test/IN': AXFR\-style IXFR started (serial 2026102802)
28\-Oct\-2026 12:42:10.500 security: error: client 192.0.2.99#33000: zone transfer 'example.test/AXFR/IN' denied
28\-Oct\-2026 12:42:11.000 xfer\-out: warning: client 192.0.2.7#40510: transfer of 'example.test/IN': AXFR request denied: quota reached (transfers\-out 10)
28\-Oct\-2026 12:43:00.000 xfer\-out: debug 1: client 192.0.2.7#40520: transfer of 'example.test/IN': IXFR poll up to date (serial 2026102802)
28\-Oct\-2026 12:44:00.100 query\-errors: debug 1: client 192.0.2.50#35010: query failed (SERVFAIL) for www.example.test/IN/A: Zone expired (SOA EXPIRE exceeded)
28\-Oct\-2026 12:44:01.200 query\-errors: debug 3: client 192.0.2.50#35011: query failed (REFUSED) for www.other.test/IN/A: This server is not authoritative for the queried zone
28\-Oct\-2026 12:44:02.300 query\-errors: debug 3: client 192.0.2.50#35012: query failed (FORMERR) for \-/\-/\-: the question count is not valid for this opcode (RFC 9619)
.fi
.RE
.PP
A channel that shows these, with the debug levels of \fBquery\-errors\fR:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
logging {
    channel qerr { file "/var/log/karidns/query\-errors.log" versions 3 size 5m; severity debug 3; print\-time yes; print\-category yes; print\-severity yes; };
    channel xfer { file "/var/log/karidns/xfer.log" versions 10 size 10m; severity info; print\-time yes; print\-category yes; print\-severity yes; };
    category query\-errors { qerr; };
    category xfer\-out     { xfer; };
};
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBQuery log and response log.\fR \fBoptions { querylog yes; }\fR switches the
query log on and \fBquerylog no;\fR off; the lines go to the channels of
\fBcategory queries\fR (to the \fBdefault\fR category's when there are none, so
\fBquerylog yes;\fR alone logs the queries to syslog). With \fBquerylog\fR not
written, the query log is on when \fBcategory queries\fR lists a channel (BIND
leaves it off until \fBrndc querylog\fR; KariDNS has always logged queries as
soon as the category was configured). The response log is on when \fBcategory
responses\fR lists a channel. A line is \fBclient <address>#<port> (<name>):
query: <name> <class> <type> <flags>\fR (\fBresponse: ... \-> <rcode>\fR for
responses). The rate limit of a channel is its own \fBmax\-qps\fR (when written
and above 0), else \fBquery\-log\-max\-qps\fR; every channel of \fBcategory
queries\fR is held to its own limit (the workers admit what the most generous
channel allows and the query logger drops the lines over a channel's limit).
.PP
\fBFiles.\fR The log files are opened when the server starts, before it drops
its privileges and enters the Capsicum sandbox; the sandboxed process then
writes to the descriptors it holds and rolls the files through the directory
descriptors it holds, so a log directory is fixed by the start.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBversions <n>\fR: when the file reaches \fBsize\fR,
\fB<path>.0\fR ... \fB<path>.<n\-1>\fR are shifted up and the file becomes
\fB<path>.0\fR; \fBversions unlimited\fR keeps every old file; \fBversions 0\fR
empties the file instead (BIND removes it). With \fBsuffix timestamp\fR the old
file is renamed \fB<path>.YYYYMMDD\fR (\fB.1\fR, \fB.2\fR, ... for a second roll
on the same day; the file is also rolled when the day changes, which BIND does
not do), and \fBversions <n>\fR keeps the \fBn\fR newest. Other files in the
directory are never removed.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsize\fR without \fBversions\fR (BIND \fBISC_LOG_ROLLNEVER\fR): when the next
line does not fit, \fBno more lines are written to the file\fR until it is
removed or shorter than \fBsize\fR; the file is looked at again about once a
second. (Earlier KariDNS versions truncated the file; write \fBversions 0\fR for
that. A warning is given when the configuration is read.) \fBsize\fR unlimited /
absent: the file grows.
.IP \(bu 2
A file never exceeds \fBsize\fR (BIND lets the last line pass it).
.IP \(bu 2
Messages written before the channels exist (the first lines of a start: the
version, the warnings and errors found while the configuration is read) go to
syslog, like BIND's messages before its \fBlogging\fR statement is applied; a
reload reports what it finds in the configuration through the channels in use.
.IP \(bu 2
The supervisor (the manager, with the supervisors of the plugins and of
\fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR), the UDP routers and the connect broker are separate
processes: they open the file by name for each line and never roll it, so their
lines follow a roll done by the sandboxed process. They never create a file
either (0.7): a log file that was removed by hand is made again only by the
sandboxed process, as the run user, at its next roll or reload, and the lines of
these processes are lost until then (before 0.7 the supervisor, which runs as
root, made it again, root-owned, and the sandboxed process could then neither
append to it nor roll it). The channels they are handed come from the sandboxed
process, which must be assumed hostile, so they are held to the configuration of
the start (0.7): a file only in a directory of the start's file channels (one
the sandboxed process can write to itself), only a regular file that is not a
symbolic link, with one link, that belongs to the run user (never to root,
although the supervisor runs as root; when the server has no \fBuser\fR and runs
as one user throughout, that user's), and \fBkaridns.run\fR only through the
descriptor of the start; a \fBsyslog\fR facility only one a channel of the start
used (any other is \fBdaemon\fR); and their messages at \fBwarning\fR and worse
always also reach the channels of the start for their category (unless a channel
in use has just written the line to the same file, facility or standard error),
so that no logging configuration handed over silences the supervisor's warnings
and errors.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThe other processes follow the sandboxed one (0.7).\fR After a \fBkarictl
trace\fR / \fBnotrace\fR and after a reload that applies a \fBlogging\fR
statement, the sandboxed process hands the debug level and the channels to the
supervisor, the routers and the broker over the descriptors they share with it
since the start (nothing is opened for it), and the level to
\fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR; they write to the new channels from then on. Before 0.7
these processes kept the channels and the level of the start. The supervisor
keeps the level for the processes it starts later: a loader started again (after
a crash, say) comes up with the level in force (\fBkaridns\-dbloader
\-\-debug\fR). A configuration whose channels do not fit the record they travel
in (more than 32 KB, or a file path longer than 1023 bytes) is refused for them
with a warning (\fB[Logging] The logging configuration does not fit ...\fR):
they follow the level but keep the channels of the start. Their debug lines are
the same kind as the sandboxed process's (\fB[Logging] Manager: debug level
N\fR, \fB[Frontend N] ...\fR, \fB[Broker] ...\fR, \fB[Plugin] ...\fR,
\fB[Manager] Database loader ...\fR, and the loader's \fB[DB] ...\fR lines in
category \fBdatabase\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBReload.\fR A reload builds the channels again. A file in a directory that was
already open when the server started can be opened; a file in a directory the
sandbox cannot reach (a new directory) cannot, and \fBkarictl reload\fR /
\fBSIGHUP\fR then keeps the logging in use and reports \fBrestart needed for:
logging (a log file in a directory not opened at start\-up)\fR; everything else
of the reload is applied. A file that cannot be opened for another reason
rejects the reload (the running configuration is kept); the built-in
\fBkaridns.run\fR never does (a warning, see \fBdefault_debug\fR above).
.IP \(bu 2
Not honoured, accepted with a warning: \fBbuffered yes\fR (every line is written
at once); a \fBsyslog\fR facility name that does not exist (BIND silently uses
\fBdaemon\fR; so does KariDNS).
.PP
\fBDirectories and files (root start).\fR karidns creates the directory of a log
file when it is missing and hands over only a directory it created itself to
\fBuser\fR/\fBgroup\fR; an existing directory keeps its owner and mode (as BIND
does): prepare it so that the run user can write to it (and, for a rotation,
create files in it). A start that is refused after the log files were opened (a
port that cannot be bound, an unsafe pid file or control socket directory,
another instance, a log file that cannot be opened) hands the files and
directories it made to \fBuser\fR / \fBgroup\fR before it ends, as a start that
goes on would (0.7; earlier versions left them root-owned, and the run user
could not roll the files in such a directory); a file or directory that was
there before keeps its owner. Such a start also removes the control socket it
bound and the pid file it locked; a socket that another server bound at that
name meanwhile stays, and so does everything of a running instance (0.7; earlier
versions left the socket behind when the start was refused after the bind, for
example by an allow-group owner or mode that could not be set, root-owned when
its owner could not be set, and a fork or \fBsetsid()\fR that failed while
karidns became a daemon exited without the hand-off of the log files and left
the pid file; now that is \fB[Startup] Refusing to start: cannot run as a
daemon: <reason>\fR on stderr, with exit status 1 when the first fork fails,
while after a later failure the process that was started has already exited with
0). The directory of a log file cannot be the directory of the pid file or of
the control socket when karidns makes it for the log (it would go to the run
user, and the next start would refuse the pid file or control socket directory
as not root-owned) or when it is already not root-owned or writable by group or
others: the run user writes to a log directory, and must not be able to replace
the pid file or the socket. Such a start is refused with \fB[Startup] Refusing
to start: the pid file directory '...' is also the directory of the log
file '...'; ...\fR (or \fBthe control socket directory\fR), before anything is
bound; put the log files in a directory of their own. A root-owned directory
that was there and holds both is used as before (the run user cannot roll or
make log files in it). Nor can a log directory that karidns makes be above the
pid file or control socket directory (its parent or further up, as the
directories are, whatever symlinks or \fB..\fR the configured paths go through):
the run user would get it and could rename the root-owned directory in it away
and put one of its own in its place. Such a start is refused with \fB[Startup]
Refusing to start: the directory '...' of the log file '...', made by this
start, contains the pid file directory '...'; ...\fR (or \fBthe control socket
directory\fR), before that directory is made; the log files need a directory of
their own that is not above it (0.7; earlier versions started). A log directory
that was there before keeps working above them as it did. A log file is accepted
only when it is a regular file with one link whose owner is root, the user
karidns runs as, or the run user; a hard link, another type of file or a file of
a third user makes the start (or the reload) fail with \fBRefusing to use log
file\fR, so that a planted link cannot make karidns append to, or hand over,
another file.
.PP
Example: transfers and NOTIFY to a rotated file with the time in UTC, TSIG
errors to syslog \fBauth\fR, the query log to a file, everything else to syslog:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
logging {
    channel xfers    { file "/var/log/named/xfer.log" versions 5 size 10m; severity info; print\-time iso8601\-utc; print\-category yes; print\-severity yes; };
    channel security { syslog auth; severity notice; };
    channel qlog     { file "/var/log/named/queries.log" versions 3 size 50m; print\-time yes; };
    category xfer\-in  { xfers; };
    category xfer\-out { xfers; };
    category notify   { xfers; };
    category security { security; };
    category queries  { qlog; };
};
options { querylog yes; };
.fi
.RE
.SS controls { ... }
BIND's \fBrndc\fR control channel (\fBcontrols { inet 127.0.0.1 allow {
localhost; } keys { rndc\-key; }; };\fR). KariDNS is controlled by \fBkarictl\fR
over \fBcontrol\-channel\fR, whose protocol is not rndc's, so a \fBcontrols\fR
statement is \fBread, checked and ignored\fR: each \fBinet\fR entry gets a
warning (\fBinet 127.0.0.1 port 953: rndc is not supported ... accepted and has
no effect\fR); \fBcontrols { };\fR is silent. The grammar is BIND's: \fBinet (
<address> | * ) [ port ( <n> | * ) ] allow { <address match list> } [ keys {
<name>; ... } ] [ read\-only <bool> ];\fR, in that order; the \fBallow\fR list
must name defined acls, every key must be a defined top-level \fBkey\fR, and two
entries with the same address and port are an error (BIND
\fBcheck_controls()\fR). \fBunix "<path>" ...;\fR is an error, as in BIND 9.20
("unix control ... not supported"). When both \fBcontrols\fR and
\fBcontrol\-channel\fR are written, only \fBcontrol\-channel\fR has an effect.
.SS statistics-channels { ... }
BIND's HTTP statistics server. KariDNS has none: the statement is accepted and
skipped with a warning.
.SS key \(dq<name>\(dq { ... }
.TP 8
\fBalgorithm "<name>";\fR
\fBhmac\-md5\fR (also \fBhmac\-md5.sig\-alg.reg.int\fR), \fBhmac\-sha1\fR,
\fBhmac\-sha224\fR, \fBhmac\-sha256\fR, \fBhmac\-sha384\fR,
\fBhmac\-sha512\fR. MD5 and SHA-1 are accepted with a deprecation warning (RFC
8945). When omitted, \fBhmac\-sha256\fR is used.
.TP 8
\fBsecret "<base64>";\fR
Shared secret. Invalid base64 is an error (white space around the text is
ignored). The placeholder of \fBkaridns.conf.sample\fR (\fBUkVQTEFD...\fR) is an
error when the key is used by an ACL (\fBallow\-transfer\fR,
\fBallow\-update\fR, \fBallow\-query\fR, \fBallow\-notify\fR,
\fBmatch\-clients\fR, an \fBacl { }\fR, \fBacl\-ceiling\fR), by an
\fBupdate\-policy\fR rule or by \fBcontrol\-channel { keys }\fR, at start-up, on
reload and in \fBkaricheck conf\fR (\fBconf.tsig.secret\-placeholder\-used\fR);
a key nobody uses only gets a warning (\fBconf.tsig.secret\-placeholder\fR). The
name of a key is at most 255 bytes.
.PP
Keys are referenced by \fBallow\-transfer { key "<name>"; }\fR,
\fBallow\-update\fR, \fBtsig\-key\fR, the \fBkey\fR of a \fBprimaries\fR /
\fBalso\-notify\fR element and the \fBkeys\fR of a \fBserver\fR statement. Key
names are domain names: references match regardless of case and of a trailing
dot (\fBkey "K1"\fR is referenced by \fBkey "k1."\fR). A zone that references an
undefined key in \fBtsig\-key\fR or \fBallow\-transfer\fR is an
error. \fBkarictl tsig\-keygen\fR prints a new key block.
.PP
TSIG processing (RFC 8945 \[u00A7]5.2, \[u00A7]5.3) is the same for every opcode
(QUERY, NOTIFY, UPDATE) and for AXFR/IXFR. The server first checks the request's
TSIG against the key that the request names (key name and algorithm); only then
does it apply \fBallow\-transfer\fR, \fBallow\-update\fR or
\fBmasters\fR/\fBtsig\-key\fR:
.PP
.TS
lbw(35) lbw(36)
lw(35) lw(36).
\&Request	\&Response
_
T{
Several TSIG records, a TSIG that is not the last record, or a MAC size outside
RFC 8945 \[u00A7]5.2.2.1
T}	\&FORMERR, no TSIG
\&Unknown key name or algorithm	T{
NOTAUTH, TSIG error BADKEY, unsigned (MAC size 0) with the request's key name
T}
\&MAC does not verify	\&NOTAUTH, TSIG error BADSIG, unsigned
\&Time Signed outside the fudge	T{
NOTAUTH, TSIG error BADTIME, signed with the request's key; Time Signed and
Fudge are the client's, Other Data is the server time
T}
T{
MAC truncated (shorter than the full hash length)
T}	T{
NOTAUTH, TSIG error BADTRUNC, signed. KariDNS has no setting for truncated MACs,
like BIND without \fBdigest\-bits\fR
T}
T{
Valid TSIG, but not allowed by the zone's access control
T}	T{
REFUSED, signed with the request's key
T}
\&Valid TSIG	T{
The normal response, signed with the request's key
T}
\&No TSIG	T{
The normal response or REFUSED, unsigned
T}
.TE
.PP
A signed response that would not fit the client's UDP size with its TSIG is
truncated (TC=1). A NOTIFY is accepted from a \fBmasters\fR address and, when
that primary has a \fBkey\fR (or else the zone has \fBtsig\-key\fR), only when
it is signed with that key. \fBallow\-update\fR is evaluated in BIND's order: a
\fBkey\fR element matches a request signed with that key (see "Address match
lists"). A truncated RRL \fBslip\fR response to a signed request is signed with
the request's key as well, as in BIND 9.20 (signed requests are not exempt from
rate limiting).
.PP
\fBThe shape of an AXFR / IXFR request (RFC 5936 \[u00A7]2.1, RFC 1995
\[u00A7]3).\fR A request that is not shaped like a transfer request gets FORMERR
and no transfer (RFC 5936 \[u00A7]2.2.1 note e), over TCP, and for an IXFR over
UDP as well. A transfer request has OPCODE QUERY and RCODE 0 (a NOTIFY or UPDATE
message with the question of a transfer is not one); exactly one question,
complete and not compressed; ANCOUNT 0; NSCOUNT 0 for an AXFR, and for an IXFR
NSCOUNT 1 with the client's SOA (RFC 1995 \[u00A7]3); an Additional section with
at most one OPT record (owner the root, the options filling the RDATA exactly,
RFC 6891 \[u00A7]6.1.1-\[u00A7]6.1.2) and at most one TSIG or SIG(0) record,
which is the last record (\[u00A7]2.1.5: ARCOUNT 0, 1 or 2); no octets after the
last counted record; and at most 4096 octets. The check comes after the TSIG (a
request whose TSIG fails is NOTAUTH or FORMERR as in the table above, and the
FORMERR for a malformed request with a good TSIG is signed with the request's
key), after the EDNS version and after \fBallow\-transfer\fR (BIND checks the
transfer ACL before it reads the IXFR SOA, too), and before the transfer is
started or a zone is read. So only a client that may transfer the zone sees the
FORMERR; everybody else, and every request for a zone that is not served, gets
the REFUSED it got before, and the FORMERR tells nothing about a zone. (The
exception is a question section that does not parse, below: BIND answers it
FORMERR before it looks at anything, and so does KariDNS, for every client and
without OPT.) The answer carries no record and AA=0, and its question section is
the one BIND 9.20 gives (see "The question section of a FORMERR" below). A
database zone that is out of memory is not read for a malformed request. The
reason is in the log of category \fBxfer\-out\fR (\fBbad zone transfer request:
malformed request, <reason> (FORMERR)\fR).
.PP
\fBThe class of a transfer request (RFC 5936 \[u00A7]2.1.2, RFC 1995
\[u00A7]3).\fR QCLASS is the class of the zone requested, and every zone KariDNS
serves is of class IN. An AXFR or IXFR of any other class (CH, ANY,
NONE, ...) is a request for a zone this server is not authoritative for: REFUSED
with AA=0 and EDE 20 (Not Authoritative, RFC 8914 \[u00A7]4.21), as for a zone
that does not exist, before \fBallow\-transfer\fR and the shape check, over TCP
and for an IXFR over UDP, and a database zone that is out of memory is not read
for it. (EDE 18 Prohibited, \[u00A7]4.19, is for a client that
\fBallow\-transfer\fR refuses; a client that no view matches gets EDE 18 too, as
a query does.) It is logged with the class of the request: \fBtransfer
of 'zone/CH': bad zone transfer request: non\-authoritative zone (REFUSED)\fR
(\fBCLASSnnn\fR for a class without a mnemonic, RFC 3597 \[u00A7]5). A transfer
question for a \fBforward\fR zone is a request for a zone this server is not
authoritative for: REFUSED with AA=0 and EDE 20, before the shape check, logged
as \fBbad zone transfer request: non\-authoritative zone (REFUSED)\fR (it is not
relayed to the forwarders; BIND answers it as a non-authoritative zone, see
below); one for a \fBprogram\fR zone goes to the plugin after the same checks of
the class and of the shape of the request (REFUSED / FORMERR, logged as
above). BIND looks the zone up in the views of the request's class and answers
such a request "non-authoritative zone" with NOTAUTH (RFC 5936 \[u00A7]2.2.1
note e: SHOULD NotAuth); KariDNS answers every non-authoritative transfer
REFUSED (R-06).
.PP
\fBThe class and type of a NOTIFY or an UPDATE.\fR A NOTIFY or an UPDATE of a
class other than IN (CH, HS, ...; ANY and NONE are a FORMERR, below) is answered
NOTIMP with the question and the OPT, before the zone, the ACLs or the keys are
looked at, as named 9.20 does (it has no view of that class); also for a zone
that is out of memory. A NOTIFY whose question is not an SOA (RFC 1996
\[u00A7]4.5, \[u00A7]4.7: QTYPE=SOA) is a FORMERR with the question, the OPT and
EDE 0, and does not start a refresh of a secondary. An UPDATE whose zone section
is not an SOA stays a FORMERR (RFC 2136 \[u00A7]3.1.1).
.PP
\fBRefusals of a transfer for a reason of the server.\fR A transfer request that
the client may make but that the server cannot serve now is REFUSED with AA=0
and Extended DNS Error 0 (Other, RFC 8914 \[u00A7]4.1), not 18 (Prohibited,
which is for the ACL and TSIG refusals): over \fBtransfers\-out\fR the EDE has
the text "Too many zone transfers in progress"; when the transfer cannot be
started (no thread, no memory) the EDE has no text, so that the answer looks
like any other refusal and tells the client nothing about the server's
state. The reason is in the log of category \fBxfer\-out\fR (\fBAXFR request
denied: quota reached (transfers\-out N)\fR, \fB... cannot start a thread for
the transfer\fR, \fB... out of memory\fR). With \fBsend\-extended\-errors no;\fR
none of the answers to a transfer request (the refusals, FORMERR, SERVFAIL Not
Ready, over TCP and UDP) carries an EDE.
.PP
\fBThe question section of a FORMERR.\fR A FORMERR (of a query, a transfer
request, a NOTIFY or an UPDATE) repeats the question section as BIND 9.20 does
(\fBlib/ns/client.c\fR \fBns_client_error()\fR, \fBlib/dns/message.c\fR
\fBdns_message_reply()\fR and \fBgetquestions()\fR): BIND reads the whole
question section before anything else and echoes it only when it is good. So a
fault found after the questions (two OPT records or an unusable OPT, a malformed
COOKIE or Client Subnet option, an unparsable TSIG, a missing record, QDCOUNT
greater than 1, a meta-type question, a malformed transfer request) echoes every
question; a question section that is not good echoes none: a second question of
another name (names compare without case) or another class, the same question
twice, a question cut short (QDCOUNT larger than the questions present), the
class NONE or ANY in a NOTIFY or UPDATE. Several types of one name and class are
a good section: a QUERY with the questions \fBwww.example.test A\fR and
\fBwww.example.test AAAA\fR is a FORMERR (RFC 9619) that echoes both. A message
whose opcode is not QUERY, NOTIFY or UPDATE echoes no question; an UPDATE echoes
the zone names it read before the bad one (BIND clears the message from the
prerequisite section). A first question whose name is compressed is never echoed
(RFC 1035 \[u00A7]4.1.4). A FORMERR for an option carries the OPT record (RFC
6891 \[u00A7]7); one for an unusable OPT does not. A QUERY with no question
(QDCOUNT 0) is answered NOERROR only when it carries a COOKIE option (a
cookie-only query, RFC 7873 \[u00A7]5.4: the client cookie and a new server
cookie come back); without one it is a FORMERR with no question, with the OPT
when the request had one, and EDE 0 (BIND 9.20 \fBns__client_request()\fR; RFC
9619 allows QDCOUNT 0 but gives it no meaning outside the cookie exchange).
.PP
\fBA question section that is not good\fR (the cases above, for any opcode) is
answered FORMERR before anything else is looked at, as BIND's parse of the
message fails there: \fBno OPT record and so no EDE\fR, whatever the opcode (no
NOTIMP), the EDNS version (no BADVERS), the zone or \fBallow\-transfer\fR (a
transfer request with such a section is a FORMERR for every client). In
particular an UPDATE whose zone class is ANY or NONE, or a NOTIFY of class ANY
or NONE, is a FORMERR without the zone section (RFC 2136 \[u00A7]2.3 and RFC
1996 \[u00A7]4 ask for the class of the zone; it used to be NOTAUTH /
REFUSED). \fBNOTIMP\fR (an opcode other than QUERY, NOTIFY and UPDATE) carries
no question (BIND echoes the question section only for QUERY and NOTIFY) and
keeps the OPT, with EDE 21 (KariDNS; BIND has none). \fBBADVERS\fR echoes the
questions a FORMERR would: those of a good section of a QUERY, NOTIFY or UPDATE,
none for any other opcode.
.PP
\fBAn AXFR over UDP\fR (RFC 5936 \[u00A7]4.2: not defined) is a FORMERR only
after the checks of a transfer, in BIND's order (\fBns_xfr_start()\fR): a name
that is not a zone here -- a \fBforward\fR zone, a name inside a zone, a class
other than IN -- is REFUSED with EDE 20 (BIND: NOTAUTH, see above), a client
that \fBallow\-transfer\fR refuses is REFUSED with EDE 18, and only then the
FORMERR (logged \fBbad zone transfer request: attempted AXFR over UDP
(FORMERR)\fR); a database zone that is out of memory is not read for it. A UDP
IXFR of a name inside a zone is REFUSED with EDE 20 as well (it was answered
with the zone's SOA).
.PP
\fBRepeated FORMERRs are answered.\fR A FORMERR to the same client address, port
and message ID as one sent a moment before is sent again, over UDP and TCP, as
named 9.20 does: its \fBns_client_error()\fR loop check (\fBformerrcache\fR) is
set up again for every request (\fBns__client_setup()\fR), so it never drops an
answer. KariDNS has no such loop guard on purpose.
.SS control-channel { ... }
\fBcontrol\-channel\fR is a statement of its own, at the top level of the
configuration, next to \fBoptions\fR and \fBzone\fR. Written inside \fBoptions
{ ... }\fR it is a configuration error (the server refuses to start and
\fBkaricheck conf\fR reports it) instead of being ignored, which used to leave
the server without a control socket.
.TP 8
\fBsocket "<path>";\fR (alias \fBsocket\-path\fR)
\fIDefault:\fR \fB/var/run/karidns/control.sock\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR UNIX socket for \fBkarictl\fR.
.TP 8
\fBsecret "<base64>";\fR
\fIDefault:\fR none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Shared secret; must match \fBkarictl.conf\fR. Authentication
is an HMAC challenge-response. The placeholder of the sample (\fBUkVQTEFD...\fR)
is a start-up error (and a \fBkaricheck conf\fR error), as it is for a key an
ACL uses.
.TP 8
\fBalgorithm "<name>";\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBhmac\-sha256\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR HMAC of the challenge-response: one of the TSIG algorithm
names above. \fBkarictl.conf\fR must name the same algorithm.
.TP 8
\fBallow\-group "<group>";\fR
\fIDefault:\fR none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR A group name or a numeric gid. Members of that group (any of
the groups of the peer's credentials) may connect to the socket as well as the
user karidns runs as and root; the socket is given to the group with mode
\fB0660\fR. Every client still has to answer the HMAC challenge with a
key. Resolved when the configuration is read; an unknown group is an error. See
\fBRestricted control keys\fR.
.TP 8
\fBkeys { "<key>" [ read\-only yes|no ] [ allow\-commands { <command>; ... } ]; ... };\fR
\fIDefault:\fR none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Restricted control keys: top-level \fBkey\fR statements that
the control channel accepts, each with what it may run. See \fBRestricted
control keys\fR below.
.PP
Any other key is a configuration error that names the key (\fBcontrol\-channel:
unknown option 'port' (supported: socket, secret, algorithm, allow\-group,
keys)\fR): the server refuses to start, a \fBkarictl reload\fR / \fBreconfig\fR
is refused and keeps the running configuration, and \fBkaricheck conf\fR reports
it as an error. The channel is a UNIX socket only; there is no \fBport\fR or
\fBbind\-address\fR. A misspelt \fBsecret\fR or \fBsocket\fR used to be skipped,
which left the channel without the secret or on the default socket without a
word. Servers that run side by side need a \fBsocket\fR each: without it every
one binds \fB/var/run/karidns/control.sock\fR, and the last one to start takes
the path over.
.PP
\fBRestricted control keys (\fBkeys { }\fB).\fR \fBkarictl\fR can be given a key
that may run only some commands, for example for a web tier that has to show the
status of the zones and reload one zone but must never stop the server. The keys
are ordinary top-level \fBkey\fR statements (the statement that also holds TSIG
keys, with \fBalgorithm\fR and \fBsecret\fR); \fBkeys { }\fR lists the ones the
control channel accepts and what each may do:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
key "admin" { algorithm hmac\-sha256; secret "<base64>"; };
key "web"   { algorithm hmac\-sha256; secret "<base64>"; };
key "debug" { algorithm hmac\-sha256; secret "<base64>"; };
\&
control\-channel {
    socket "/var/run/karidns/control.sock";
    secret "<base64>";                      // optional: the unnamed key, full power, as before
    keys {
        "admin";                            // full power
        "web" read\-only yes allow\-commands { reload\-zone; rollback; };
        "debug" read\-only yes allow\-commands { trace; };   // status and the debug level, nothing else
    };
};
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
A key without \fBread\-only\fR (or with \fBread\-only no\fR) has full
power. \fBread\-only yes\fR has BIND's meaning (\fBcontrols { ... read\-only
yes; }\fR): only \fBstatus\fR, \fBzonestatus\fR, \fBobservatory\fR,
\fBdbstatus\fR and \fBstats\fR. \fBallow\-commands { ... }\fR is a KariDNS
extension that adds commands to a read-only key; without \fBread\-only yes\fR it
is an error, because a key that is not read-only can run everything already. The
clauses may be written in either order.
.IP \(bu 2
The names that \fBallow\-commands\fR takes (nothing else is accepted, the
test-only commands included):
.RS 2
.PP
.TS
lb lbw(58)
l lw(58).
\&Name	\&Allows
_
\&\fBreload\fR	\&the full reload: \fBkarictl reload\fR without a zone
\&\fBreload\-zone\fR	T{
\fBkarictl reload <zone> [<view>] [force]\fR only (a zone, never all)
T}
\&\fBreconfig\fR	\&\fBkarictl reconfig\fR
\&\fBstop\fR	\&\fBkarictl stop\fR
\&\fBnotify\fR	\&\fBkarictl notify <zone>\fR
\&\fBretransfer\fR	\&\fBkarictl retransfer <zone>\fR
\&\fBrollback\fR	T{
\fBkarictl rollback <zone>\fR: accepted in the configuration now; until the
command exists the server answers it with the unknown-command error
T}
\&\fBtrace\fR	T{
\fBkarictl trace [<level>]\fR and \fBkarictl notrace\fR (0.7): the debug
level. A name of its own, not part of \fBread\-only\fR, because the debug output
can show more than the read-only commands do; a key without \fBread\-only\fR has
it, as every other command
T}
.TE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
The unnamed \fBsecret\fR (and \fBalgorithm\fR) of the block keep working as they
always did: a full-power key that is logged as \fB(main)\fR. A configuration
with only \fBkeys { }\fR and no \fBsecret\fR is valid.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNothing changes for \fBkarictl\fB or the protocol.\fR The client's
\fBkarictl.conf\fR holds the \fBsecret\fR and the \fBalgorithm\fR of the key it
uses (the name of the key is not sent). The server computes the HMAC of the
challenge for the unnamed secret and for every listed key, each with its own
algorithm, compares all of them in constant time and takes the key that
matches. Two keys (the unnamed secret included) must therefore not have the same
secret: that is a configuration error.
.IP \(bu 2
A command the key may not run is answered with \fBERROR command not allowed for
key "web"\fR (\fBkarictl\fR exits 3); the connection stays open, so the client
can send another command. The key and its permission are fixed when the client
logs in: a \fBreconfig\fR that changes \fBkeys { }\fR applies to the connections
made afterwards, never to one that is open.
.IP \(bu 2
Every command is logged at NOTICE level with the key name, whether it was run or
refused: \fB[Control] key "web": reload example.test\fR and \fB[Control] key
"web": refused: stop\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Errors (the server does not start, a \fBkarictl reconfig\fR / \fBreload\fR is
refused and keeps the running configuration, and \fBkaricheck conf\fR reports
them with file and line): a name that no top-level \fBkey\fR statement defines
(a \fBkey\fR inside a \fBview\fR does not count), a key listed twice, a key
whose algorithm the control channel cannot use, two keys with one secret,
\fBallow\-commands\fR without \fBread\-only yes\fR, a name in
\fBallow\-commands\fR that is not in the table above, and a clause written
twice.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLetting a web tier in: \fBallow\-group\fB.\fR The socket is normally open
only to the user karidns runs as and to root, so a web tier would have to run as
the karidns user, which can read \fBkaridns.conf\fR and the full-power
secret. With \fBallow\-group "<group>";\fR (a group name or a numeric gid) the
members of that group may connect too, and the socket file is given to that
group with mode \fB0660\fR when it is created (without \fBallow\-group\fR it
stays \fB0600\fR, owned by karidns' user). The recommended setup for a web tier
is \fBallow\-group\fR plus a read-only key: the web user is in the group, holds
only the \fBkarictl.conf\fR of its read-only key, and \fBcannot read
\fBkaridns.conf\fB\fR (keep it \fB0640 root:karidns\fR or similar, and the web
user outside that group). Being admitted is only the first step: the HMAC
challenge follows, so a member of the group without a key gets nothing. The
directory of the socket is the operator's job: the group needs search (\fBx\fR)
permission on it, and nobody else should be able to replace the socket (karidns
checks the directory when it starts). Details: the group is looked up when the
configuration is read (start-up, \fBkaricheck conf\fR); a \fBreload\fR runs in
capability mode, where \fB/etc/group\fR cannot be read, so it accepts a numeric
gid or the name that was resolved at start-up, and refuses another name with the
reason (the running configuration is kept; restart the server). The socket is
created once: a changed \fBallow\-group\fR is reported as needing a restart, as
a changed \fBsocket\fR is. The check uses the credentials of the connection
(\fBLOCAL_PEERCRED\fR: the user id and all groups of the peer, supplementary
groups included); the server is FreeBSD-only, so there is no Linux variant
(\fBdag\fR is the only component that builds there). If the process cannot give
the socket to the group (it is not root and not in the group) the server does
not start.
.IP \(bu 2
Use a key of its own for the control channel. \fBkaricheck conf\fR warns when a
listed key is also used for TSIG anywhere (\fBallow\-transfer\fR,
\fBallow\-update\fR, \fBallow\-notify\fR, \fBallow\-query\fR and
\fBmatch\-clients\fR lists, a zone's \fBtsig\-key\fR, \fBprimaries\fR and
\fBalso\-notify\fR entries, \fBserver { keys }\fR, \fBupdate\-policy\fR, an
\fBacl { }\fR block, \fBacl\-ceiling\fR): whoever holds a key for zone
transfers, for example a secondary, could then also control the server.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcontrols { inet ... keys { ... } read\-only ...; }\fR of BIND stays as it
was: read, checked and ignored.
.PP
\fBKeys in a view.\fR A \fBkey\fR statement inside \fBview { }\fR has the same
options and is visible only to that view: its zones' \fBallow\-transfer\fR,
\fBallow\-update\fR, \fBallow\-query\fR, \fBtsig\-key\fR, \fBprimaries\fR /
\fBalso\-notify\fR \fBkey\fR and the view's \fBserver\fR statements may name it,
and a request that reaches the view is verified with the keys of that view and
the global keys (BIND \fBbin/named/tsigconf.c\fR: the ring of a view is the
global keys plus its own). The same name may be used in two different views with
different secrets. A name that is already a global key, or twice in one view, is
a configuration error, exactly as in BIND (\fBlib/isccfg/check.c\fR; the view's
keys do not \fIshadow\fR a global key). A name used in a view that is neither
global nor in that view is rejected for that view (\fBkey 'k' is not defined in
any key {} block\fR). The view is chosen first, by the client's address
(\fBmatch\-clients\fR), and then the request's TSIG is verified against that
view's keys; a \fBkey\fR entry in \fBmatch\-clients\fR is still not
supported. Limits: a named \fBacl { }\fR block, a \fBremote\-servers\fR /
\fBprimaries\fR list and the settings of a database zone are global and can name
only global keys.
.SS view \(dq<name>\(dq { ... }
.TP 8
\fBmatch\-clients { <acl>; ... };\fR
Clients that use this view: an address match list (addresses, prefixes,
\fBany\fR, \fBnone\fR, \fBlocalhost\fR, \fBlocalnets\fR, acl names, nested
lists, \fB!\fR) that may also name TSIG keys with \fBkey <name>\fR; see
\fBChoosing a view by TSIG key\fR below. A view without \fBmatch\-clients\fR
matches every client.
.TP 8
\fBnotify yes|no|explicit|primary\-only|master\-only;\fR
Replaces the \fBoptions\fR value for the zones of this view that have no
\fBnotify\fR of their own.
.TP 8
\fBalso\-notify [ port <n> ] { ... };\fR
Replaces the \fBoptions\fR list for the zones of this view that have no
\fBalso\-notify\fR of their own. See \fBalso\-notify\fR and
\fBremote\-servers\fR below.
.TP 8
\fBallow\-query\fR, \fBallow\-query\-on\fR, \fBallow\-transfer\fR, \fBallow\-update\fR, \fBallow\-notify { <acl>; ... };\fR
Each replaces the \fBoptions\fR statement of the same name for the zones of this
view that have none of their own (a zone's statement replaces the view's, the
view's replaces \fBoptions\fR'; the lists are not merged). A \fBview\fR without
\fBallow\-query\fR uses the \fBoptions\fR statement; both default as described
in the \fBoptions\fR table.
.TP 8
\fBkey "<name>" { ... };\fR
A TSIG key of this view only (see \fBkey\fR).
.TP 8
\fBminimal\-responses yes|no|no\-auth|no\-auth\-recursive;\fR
Replaces the \fBoptions\fR value for the clients of this view. BIND makes the
answer with the options of the view the client came through
(\fBlib/ns/query.c\fR: \fBclient\->view\->minimalresponses\fR), also for a zone
that the view shares with another one.
.TP 8
\fBin\-view\-settings owner|view;\fR
See Zones shared between views.
.TP 8
\fBserver <prefix> { ... };\fR
Server statements of this view. When a view has any, they replace the top-level
\fBserver\fR statements as a whole (BIND uses the first list that exists, it
does not merge); \fBkaricheck conf\fR notes it.
.TP 8
\fBzone "<name>" { ... };\fR
Zones of this view. The same zone name may appear in several views. \fBzone
"<name>" { in\-view "<view>"; };\fR is the zone of an earlier view: see Zones
shared between views.
.PP
\fBChoosing a view by TSIG key.\fR \fBmatch\-clients { key <name>; };\fR selects
the view by the TSIG key of the request, as BIND 9.20 does
(\fBbin/named/server.c\fR \fBget_matching_view_sync()\fR: for each view in the
order defined, the message signature is checked with that view's key ring,
\fBdns_message_checksig(message, view)\fR, and then \fBdns_acl_allowed(srcaddr,
tsig identity, view\->matchclients)\fR decides). The list is an ordinary address
match list: the first element that matches decides, a \fB!\fR element that
matches denies the view, a list in which nothing matches does not select the
view; a \fBkey\fR element matches a request that is signed with a key of that
name \fBand whose signature verifies in this view's key ring\fR (the global keys
and the view's own \fBkey\fR statements: two views may have a key of one name
with different secrets, each is checked against its own). Consequences:
.IP \(bu 2
a request signed with key A and one signed with key B can be given different
views (\fBview "a" { match\-clients { key ka; }; ... }; view "b" {
match\-clients { key kb; }; ... };\fR), and an unsigned request, or one signed
with a key no list names, falls through to the view that matches its address
(\fBmatch\-clients { 192.0.2.0/24; }\fR or \fBany\fR);
.IP \(bu 2
a request whose signature does not verify (BADSIG, BADKEY, BADTIME) is treated
as unsigned for every \fBkey\fR element, so it never selects the view of that
key; if the view the address elements choose is one of the others, the request
is answered there with the TSIG error (NOTAUTH, RFC 8945 section 5.2), exactly
as in BIND;
.IP \(bu 2
put the views that are chosen by key before a view that matches the same address
with \fBany\fR: the first match wins (BIND does the same), so \fBview "world" {
match\-clients { any; }; }; view "a" { match\-clients { key ka; }; };\fR never
reaches \fBa\fR. BIND says nothing about it; KariDNS prints a warning with
\fBfile:line\fR (at start-up, on reload and in \fBkaricheck conf\fR: \fBKariDNS
warning (BIND does not check this): this list names a key, but the view 'world'
(karidns.conf:1) comes first and matches every client, so this view can never be
chosen\fR) when a keyed view follows a view that surely matches every client:
\fBany\fR, \fB0.0.0.0/0\fR together with \fB::/0\fR, a view without
\fBmatch\-clients\fR, or an acl or nested list that is one of these, before any
negated element. The check is conservative (a negated element, or only one
address family, gives no warning) and the configuration is still accepted;
.IP \(bu 2
it applies to every request the server answers through a view: queries over UDP
and TCP, AXFR and IXFR, UPDATE and NOTIFY (they all take the view of the
request; the zone's \fBallow\-transfer\fR, \fBallow\-update\fR,
\fBallow\-notify\fR and \fBallow\-query\fR are then evaluated in that view with
the verified key as before);
.IP \(bu 2
the key must be defined (globally or in the view that names it); an undefined
key is a configuration error with \fBfile:line\fR. A key inside an \fBacl\fR
block that \fBmatch\-clients\fR names counts the same;
.IP \(bu 2
cost: a view whose list has no \fBkey\fR element is matched by address exactly
as before, and the TSIG of a request is parsed and verified only when such a
view with a \fBkey\fR is reached and the message has an additional section; the
check made for the chosen view is reused by the server (the MAC is not verified
twice). With a keyed view in front of the others a signed request is verified
once for each keyed view up to the one chosen.
.PP
The view name is at most 63 characters; a longer name is a configuration
error. Views are checked in the order they are defined; the first match is
used. A query from a client that matches no view is answered as if no zone
matched (REFUSED). Without any \fBview\fR block, all top-level zones are placed
in an implicit view that matches all clients.
.SS Zones shared between views: in-view
.RS 2
.nf
view "internal" { match\-clients { 192.0.2.0/24; };  zone "example.test" { type primary; file "example.test.zone"; }; };
view "external" { match\-clients { any; };            zone "example.test" { in\-view "internal"; }; };
.fi
.RE
.PP
BIND 9.20 (\fBlib/isccfg/check.c\fR \fBcheck_zoneconf()\fR,
\fBbin/named/server.c\fR \fBconfigure_zone()\fR): the zone of the second view is
the zone object of the first, so there is one set of data, one SOA serial, one
IXFR history, one set of DNSSEC records (a pre-signed zone is served as it is),
one NOTIFY per change, and an UPDATE through either view is in both. The target
view must be defined \fBearlier\fR in the file, and the zone must be a primary
or secondary \fBzone { }\fR of that view (\fB'in\-view' zone 'x.test.' does not
exist in view 'v1', or view 'v1' is not yet defined\fR; a view cannot name
itself). Only \fBin\-view\fR, \fBforward\fR and \fBforwarders\fR may be written
in the zone (\fBzone 'x.test.': 'in\-view' used with incompatible zone
options\fR); \fBforward\fR and \fBforwarders\fR are read and have no effect
(this server does not forward for a shared zone). The target is a primary or
secondary \fBzone { }\fR of that view, or -- when the view takes zones from the
database (\fBdatabase { }\fR and not \fBdatabase none;\fR, or a
\fBdatabase\-file\fR / \fBdatabase\-schema\fR of its own) or has a catalog zone
-- \fBa zone of the database or a member of the catalog zone\fR with that name;
such a name cannot be checked when the configuration is read (a database zone
comes and goes), so it is accepted and found when a query, a transfer or a
NOTIFY asks (see below). Catalog zones, program zones and forward zones
themselves cannot be the target. \fBzone { in\-view }\fR outside every
\fBview\fR is the same error (no view is defined before it).
.PP
\fBWhat differs per view.\fR The answer is made with the options of the view the
client came through (BIND \fBlib/ns/query.c\fR: \fBclient\->view\->...\fR):
\fBminimal\-responses\fR of the view, then \fBmax\-udp\-size\fR and the TSIG
keys of the view. The shared response cache has no view in its key; what can
differ between views for the same data (here the effective
\fBminimal\-responses\fR mode, with the RD bit for \fBno\-auth\-recursive\fR) is
part of the key, so views with the same mode share entries and views with
different modes never get each other's bytes. Glue for the NS / MX / SRV targets
of the shared zone that lives in another zone is looked up in the zones of the
\fBclient's\fR view (BIND \fBquery_getzonedb()\fR:
\fBdns_view_findzone(client\->view, ...)\fR; \fBadditional\-from\-auth yes\fR),
so an address of a sibling zone of the owner view is not given to the clients of
a referencing view; a CNAME that leaves the zone is followed in the client's
view the same way. Answers of a shared zone that has such targets are not put
into (or taken from) the response cache when the client came through a view that
does not own the zone. The ACLs are checked before the cache is looked at on
every path (UDP, TCP, TLS, DoH), so a client that is refused is not served from
it.
.PP
\fB\fBin\-view\-settings owner|view;\fB\fR (KariDNS; in \fBoptions\fR,
\fBview\fR and the zone; the zone's value wins over the view's, which wins over
\fBoptions\fR'; default \fBowner\fR) says which ACLs apply to the requests that
come through the \fIreferencing\fR view:
.PP
.TS
lb lbw(59)
l lw(59).
\&Value	T{
allow-query, allow-query-on, allow-transfer, allow-update, allow-notify
T}
_
\&\fBowner\fR (BIND)	T{
The owner zone's statement, else the \fBowner view's\fR, else
\fBoptions\fR'. The referencing view's own statements are not used. The in-view
zone cannot have ACL statements.
T}
\&\fBview\fR	T{
The in-view zone's own statement, else the owner zone's, else the \fBreferencing
view's\fR, else \fBoptions\fR'. The in-view zone may also contain
\fBallow\-query\fR, \fBallow\-query\-on\fR, \fBallow\-transfer\fR,
\fBallow\-update\fR and \fBallow\-notify\fR.
T}
.TE
.PP
Requests through the owner view are not affected (owner zone, owner view,
\fBoptions\fR). \fBtransfer\-acl\-match\fR, \fBupdate\-acl\-match\fR, the zone's
\fBtsig\-key\fR and \fBupdate\-policy\fR are the owner zone's in both
modes. Transfers, NOTIFY targets and sources (\fBalso\-notify\fR, \fBnotify\fR,
\fBnotify\-source\fR), \fBixfr\-history\fR and the other transfer options are
those of the owner zone and the owner view: the zone is one object.
.PP
\fBZones of the database and members of a catalog (in-view phase 2).\fR \fBzone
"x.test" { in\-view "v1"; };\fR where x.test is a zone of the database of v1 (or
a member of a catalog zone of v1) shares that zone with the view. The sharing
view does not copy the zone: its directory has only the name, written in the
configuration, and a lookup that does not find a name in the view's own
directory goes on in the directory of v1 (RFC 1034 4.3.2: the zone that holds
the name, the longest match over both). So a zone that comes into the database
of v1, or leaves it, changes nothing in the sharing view: only the names written
are shared (there is no "every database zone of v1", which BIND does not have
either), a name that is no zone of v1 at the moment is not a zone of the sharing
view (a query below it is answered by the zone above it, or REFUSED), and the
zone is served again when it returns. A zone of the database of v1 that is out
of memory is asked for in the name of v1: the query waits for it (UDP and TCP)
and the zone is read once for both views; an AXFR / IXFR waits for it in the
transfer thread, a NOTIFY or an UPDATE is handled as in v1. The glue of the NS
targets in another zone comes from the \fBview the query came through\fR, and
the ACLs follow \fBin\-view\-settings\fR as for any shared zone (the settings of
a database zone -- \fBALLOW\-AXFR\-FROM\fR, \fBacl\-ceiling\fR -- are the zone's
own). A database zone of the sharing view's own source with the name of a stub
is hidden by it (D-PRIO, as by a \fBzone { }\fR block). \fBkarictl zonestatus\fR
and \fBreload\fR for such a zone take the name of the owner view.
.PP
\fBACLs of a shared database zone when the stub says its own
(\fBin\-view\-settings view\fB).\fR With \fBin\-view\-settings view;\fR a stub
\fBzone "x.test" { in\-view "v1"; allow\-transfer { ... }; allow\-update
{ ... }; };\fR in \fBkaridns.conf\fR replaces, for the requests that come
through the sharing view, the lists the database gives the owner zone:
\fBALLOW\-AXFR\-FROM\fR, \fBALLOW\-DNSUPDATE\-FROM\fR and the TSIG keys of its
metadata. \fB\fBacl\-ceiling\fB does not apply to what the stub writes\fR: the
ceiling bounds what the \fIdatabase\fR may grant (the people who write rows are
not the people who write \fBkaridns.conf\fR), and the stub is the
administrator's own statement, so a client that the stub lists is let in even
when it is outside \fBacl\-ceiling\fR. In the same way the DB-side lists are no
longer looked at for those requests (a client the database lists but the stub
does not is refused through the sharing view; it is still let in through v1,
where the database's lists and the ceiling decide as before). With
\fBin\-view\-settings owner\fR (the default) the stub cannot write these
statements, and the database's lists inside \fBacl\-ceiling\fR decide for both
views.
.PP
\fBA child zone is not shared with its parent.\fR If v1 has the database zones
\fBx.test\fR and \fBsub.x.test\fR and the sharing view v2 names only
\fBx.test\fR (\fBzone "x.test" { in\-view "v1"; };\fR), a query through v2 for a
name at or below \fBsub.x.test\fR is answered from \fBx.test\fR through the
delegation (the referral of RFC 1034 4.3.2, step 3b, when \fBx.test\fR has NS
records for \fBsub\fR), not from \fBsub.x.test\fR: only the zones written in the
sharing view are shared, and a zone of v1 below one that is shared is not shared
with it. Write \fBzone "sub.x.test" { in\-view "v1"; };\fR as well to serve it
in v2. Keep it in mind when a configuration is moved from BIND: the sharing view
does not follow the owner view into a child zone just because both zones are in
the same database; each zone it serves has to be written in the view.
.PP
\fBOperations.\fR \fBkarictl observatory\fR lists the zone once, in its owner
view, with \fBshared to views: <a>, <b>\fR; \fBkarictl status\fR counts it once;
\fBnotify\fR, \fBreload\fR, \fBzonestatus\fR and \fBretransfer\fR take the zone
with or without a view name (the name of a referencing view reaches the owner's
zone). A reload that does not change the sharing keeps the zone; adding,
removing or re-pointing an \fBin\-view\fR is noticed by the reload.
.SS also-notify and remote-servers
Grammar (BIND 9.20 ARM, "also-notify" and "remote-servers"), the same in
\fBoptions\fR, \fBview\fR and \fBzone\fR:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
also\-notify [ port <n> ] { ( <list\-name> | <ipv4> [ port <n> ] | <ipv6> [ port <n> ] ) [ key <name> ]; ... };
remote\-servers <name> [ port <n> ] { ( <list\-name> | <ipv4> [ port <n> ] | <ipv6> [ port <n> ] ) [ key <name> ]; ... };
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fBWhere it applies.\fR The ARM: in \fBoptions\fR it is a global list; in a
\fBzone\fR it "overrides any global statement"; in a \fBview\fR it overrides the
global one for that view. KariDNS reads the list of a zone from the zone's own
statement, else the statement of its view, else \fBoptions\fR. The lists are
never merged; an empty \fBalso\-notify { };\fR in a zone (or view) switches the
inherited list off. A catalog member and a zone without a \fBzone { }\fR use the
view's or the \fBoptions\fR list. A later statement of the same kind in the same
block replaces the earlier one.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBport\fB.\fR \fBport <n>\fR before the \fB{\fR is the port of the addresses
written directly in that statement that have no port of their own; an address's
own \fBport\fR wins. Without any, \fBoptions { port }\fR (53 when \fBoptions\fR
writes none, wherever in the file it is written; \fB\-p\fR does not change
it). BIND 9.20 takes the default port of every such list from the same option:
\fBbin/named/config.c\fR \fBnamed_config_getipandkeylist()\fR starts from
\fBnamed_config_getport(config, "port")\fR, and \fBlib/dns/zone.c\fR
\fBnotify_find_address()\fR sends the NOTIFY to the NS hosts at
\fBview\->dstport\fR, which \fBbin/named/server.c\fR sets from it. So with
\fBoptions { port 5300; }\fR an \fBalso\-notify { 192.0.2.7; }\fR, a
\fBprimaries { 192.0.2.8; }\fR, the NS hosts of a zone and the primary of a
catalog member are all on port 5300 unless a \fBport\fR is written. A list name
takes no \fBport\fR of its own, but the \fBport\fR of the statement it is
written in is passed to it: the addresses of a nested list that have no port
take the \fBport\fR of their own list, else the \fBport\fR of the list that
names it (and so on outwards), else \fBoptions { port }\fR (BIND
\fBnamed_config_getipandkeylist()\fR; the \fBsource\fR of a list is not
inherited).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBList names.\fR A name that is not an IP address must be a
\fBremote\-servers\fR (\fBprimaries\fR, \fBmasters\fR) list of this
configuration; it is replaced by the addresses of the list, with their ports and
keys. A list may name other lists (at most 8 deep; a list that includes itself
is an error) and may be written after the statement that uses it. A \fBkey\fR
written after a list name is the key of those addresses of the list that have
none of their own. An unknown name is a configuration error, shown by
\fBkaricheck conf\fR and by \fBkaridns\fR at startup and reload (a rejected
reload keeps the running configuration). After expansion a statement may have at
most 256 addresses; NOTIFY is sent to at most 64 different addresses per zone
change including the NS hosts.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBkey <name>\fB.\fR The NOTIFY to that address is signed with the TSIG key
\fBname\fR (RFC 8945 section 5.1; RFC 1996 section 4.5 for the NOTIFY itself),
and only an answer that verifies stops the retransmission (RFC 8945 section
5.4). The key must be defined in a \fBkey { }\fR block, otherwise the
configuration is rejected. \fBPrecedence:\fR the \fBkey\fR of an address wins
over the \fBkeys\fR of the \fBserver\fR statement that covers the address, which
wins over the zone's \fBtsig\-key\fR; an address without any (and every NS host)
is sent unsigned. (BIND: the element's key, else the server's; the zone
\fBtsig\-key\fR is KariDNS.)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNot supported (a configuration error, not a silent no-op).\fR \fBtls
<name>\fR (NOTIFY has no encrypted transport; it goes over UDP, or over TCP: see
\fBtcp\-only\fR of the \fBserver\fR statement). \fBsource ( <ipv4> | * )\fR /
\fBsource\-v6 ( <ipv6> | * )\fR before the braces (no port) is the local address
of the NOTIFY messages to the addresses written in that statement, ahead of
\fBnotify\-source\fR; see Transfer and NOTIFY options.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBnotify\fB.\fR \fBnotify yes | no | explicit | primary\-only |
master\-only;\fR in \fBoptions\fR, \fBview\fR and \fBzone\fR (BIND 9.20 ARM
"notify"; boolean spellings \fBtrue\fR, \fBfalse\fR, \fB1\fR, \fB0\fR too). The
zone's value overrides the view's, which overrides \fBoptions\fR'; default
\fByes\fR. \fByes\fR: NOTIFY goes to the apex NS hosts (except the SOA MNAME
host, RFC 1996 section 3.2) and to the \fBalso\-notify\fR list. \fBno\fR: no
NOTIFY is sent. \fBexplicit\fR: only to the \fBalso\-notify\fR list (the NS
hosts are left out). \fBprimary\-only\fR (older \fBmaster\-only\fR): as
\fByes\fR, but only for primary zones; a secondary zone and a catalog member
send nothing after a transfer (KariDNS secondaries pass a transfer on to their
NS hosts and \fBalso\-notify\fR list by default, as BIND does). Every NOTIFY
path follows the setting: updates, reloads, transfers and \fBkarictl notify
<zone>\fR (the ARM says only that \fBrndc notify\fR "resends NOTIFY messages for
the zone"; a zone with \fBnotify no\fR therefore sends nothing). The value is
not part of a database zone's settings text (refused there).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBErrors.\fR A syntax error says what and where, for example \fB[ERROR]
/etc/karidns/karidns.conf:12: zone 'example.test.' also\-notify: expected ';'
after '192.0.2.1' but found '}'\fR; an unknown list or undefined key names the
line of the element: \fB[ERROR] karidns.conf:7: options also\-notify: 'sec' is
neither an IP address nor a remote\-servers / primaries / masters list defined
in this configuration\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDatabase zones.\fR The settings text of a database zone (\fBALSO\-NOTIFY\fR
and the masters of a \fBSLAVE\fR / \fBCONSUMER\fR zone, see Settings of database
zones) takes addresses and ports only (also \fBport <n>\fR before the \fB{\fR):
no list name, no \fBkey\fR, no \fBtls\fR. An address without a port uses
\fBoptions { port }\fR of the running configuration (the settings text is read
without it, so the port is looked up when the NOTIFY or the transfer is made). A
database zone whose settings have no \fBalso\-notify\fR uses the \fBoptions\fR
list (or that of its view) like any zone; one that has it, even an empty one,
uses its own.
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
key "nk" { algorithm hmac\-sha256; secret "..."; };
options { also\-notify { 192.0.2.53; }; };
remote\-servers secondaries port 5353 { 192.0.2.54; 2001:db8::54 key "nk"; };
zone "example.test" { type master; file "example.test.zone"; also\-notify { secondaries; 192.0.2.99 port 5300; }; };
.fi
.RE
.PP
A zone that appears in several views is a separate zone in each view: its own
file, data, additional-section glue from the other zones of the same view, wire
cache settings and \fBprogram\fR plugin. \fBkarictl reload <zone> <view>\fR
reloads one view's copy. If a reload changes a zone's \fBtype\fR (for example
\fBmaster\fR to \fBslave\fR), the zone is recreated with the new type.
.SS Transfer and NOTIFY options: source addresses, secondary timers, limits (BIND 9.20)
The statements below are those of the BIND 9.20 ARM ("Zone Transfers", "Boolean
Options", the \fBzone\fR statement); defaults come from BIND's
\fBbin/named/config.c\fR, ranges from \fBlib/isccfg/check.c\fR. They are written
in \fBoptions\fR, \fBview\fR and \fBzone\fR; for each option separately \fBthe
zone's statement wins over the view's, which wins over \fBoptions\fB'\fR (never
merged); a value out of range is a configuration error with
\fBfile:line\fR. \fBstartup\-notify\-rate\fR ... \fBtransfers\-per\-ns\fR exist
in \fBoptions\fR only.
.TP 8
\fBnotify\-source ( <ipv4> | * );\fR, \fBnotify\-source\-v6 ( <ipv6> | * );\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB*\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR Local address of NOTIFY messages (no port: the 9.20 grammar has
none, a \fBport\fR is an error). \fB*\fR = the system chooses. The \fBsource\fR
of the \fBalso\-notify\fR list an address is written in wins over it, and the
\fBnotify\-source\fR of a \fBserver\fR statement for the address wins over
both. The KariDNS zone statement \fBnotify\-source <address>\fR keeps its old
meaning (either family, no warning); \fBnotify\-source\-v6\fR of the same zone
wins for IPv6.
.TP 8
\fBtransfer\-source ( <ipv4> | * );\fR, \fBtransfer\-source\-v6 ( <ipv6> | * );\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB*\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR Local address of the TCP connections a secondary opens to its
primaries (the zone transfer and the refresh check -- KariDNS checks with an
IXFR or a SOA query over TCP, so both leave from it). The \fBsource\fR /
\fBsource\-v6\fR of the \fBprimaries\fR list the primary is written in wins over
it (\fBsource *\fR counts as not written), and the \fBtransfer\-source\fR of a
\fBserver\fR statement for the primary wins over both.
.TP 8
\fBmin\-refresh\-time\fR, \fBmax\-refresh\-time\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB300\fR, \fB2419200\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR Seconds, 1..4294967295 (0 is an error, as in BIND). The SOA
REFRESH a secondary uses is held to this range (BIND \fBRANGE()\fR; if min > max
the result is max, \fBkaricheck conf\fR warns).
.TP 8
\fBmin\-retry\-time\fR, \fBmax\-retry\-time\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB500\fR, \fB1209600\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR The same for the SOA RETRY. The expire timer is \fBSOA EXPIRE\fR
held to \fB[refresh + retry, 24 weeks]\fR. \fBkarictl zonestatus\fR shows
\fBrefresh=\fR, \fBretry=\fR and \fBexpire=\fR as used. \fBThese defaults are
BIND's: a secondary whose primary publishes REFRESH 60 checks every 300 s unless
\fBmin\-refresh\-time\fB says otherwise.\fR
.TP 8
\fBmax\-transfer\-time\-in\fR, \fBmax\-transfer\-idle\-in\fR, \fBmax\-transfer\-time\-out\fR, \fBmax\-transfer\-idle\-out\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB120\fR, \fB60\fR, \fB120\fR, \fB60\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR Minutes, 0..40320 (28 days); 0 = no limit. an inbound transfer is
given up after the total time (also in the middle of a message that arrives a
few octets at a time), or when no message arrives for the idle time; an outbound
one when it takes longer than the total time or a send waits the idle time for
the client. While it waits, an outbound transfer holds only the zone version it
sends (see "Outbound transfers: one version from start to end" below): a reload
or an UPDATE does not wait for it. (The environment variable
\fBKARIDNS_XFER_MINUTE_SECS=n\fR, 1..60, makes a "minute" n seconds -- a test
hook, like BIND's \fBnamed \-T transferinsecs\fR.)
.TP 8
\fBtransfers\-in\fR, \fBtransfers\-out\fR, \fBtransfers\-per\-ns\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB10\fR, \fB10\fR, \fB2\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR Concurrent inbound transfers, concurrent outbound transfers (a
request over the limit is REFUSED with EDE 0 (Other) "Too many zone transfers in
progress": a reason of the server, not EDE 18 Prohibited), inbound transfers
from one primary address. A refresh over a limit waits for a later tick. \fB0\fR
is accepted (no inbound transfer / every outbound one
refused). \fBtransfers\-out\fR replaces the former fixed limit of 4 transfers of
one zone. Each running outbound transfer is a thread and a TCP connection of its
own; it holds no reader slot of the server while it waits for its client, so
\fBtransfers\-out\fR may be raised above 128 (0.7, R11).
.TP 8
\fBserial\-query\-rate\fR, \fBnotify\-rate\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB20\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR Per second; 0 is raised to 1 (BIND). \fBserial\-query\-rate\fR
limits how many refresh checks start in a second (KariDNS has no separate SOA
query: the IXFR / SOA check is the query); \fBnotify\-rate\fR the first NOTIFY
messages of a second, the rest follow at the next ticks.
.TP 8
\fBstartup\-notify\-rate\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB20\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR Accepted and range-checked, \fBnot enforced\fR: KariDNS sends no
NOTIFY when it starts (only after a zone changed or a secondary transferred), so
there is nothing to limit. \fBkaricheck conf\fR says so.
.TP 8
\fBnotify\-delay\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB5\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR Seconds between two sets of NOTIFY messages for one zone; a
request inside the delay (also \fBkarictl notify\fR) waits for its end.
.TP 8
\fBnotify\-to\-soa yes|no;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBno\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR \fByes\fR: the NS host that is the SOA MNAME also gets a NOTIFY
(otherwise not, RFC 1996 \[u00A7]3.2).
.TP 8
\fBrequest\-ixfr yes|no;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fByes\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR \fBno\fR: the secondary asks for the SOA first (over TCP) and
transfers the whole zone by AXFR only when the primary's serial is newer.
.TP 8
\fBprovide\-ixfr yes|no;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fByes\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR \fBno\fR: an IXFR request from a client that is behind is
answered with the whole zone (an AXFR-style IXFR, RFC 1995 section 4); a client
that already has the current serial (or a newer one, RFC 1982) still gets the
single SOA, over TCP and UDP, because BIND 9.20 \fBlib/ns/xfrout.c\fR
\fBns_xfr_start()\fR answers that poll before it looks at
\fBprovide\-ixfr\fR. BIND allows it in \fBoptions\fR, \fBview\fR and
\fBserver\fR; KariDNS also in \fBzone\fR. A \fBserver\fR statement for the
requester wins over the others.
.TP 8
\fBrequest\-expire yes|no;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fByes\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR \fBno\fR: the transfer request has no EDNS EXPIRE option (RFC
7314).
.TP 8
\fBtry\-tcp\-refresh yes|no;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fByes\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR Accepted; \fBno\fR has no effect (KariDNS checks over TCP only)
and is reported with a warning.
.TP 8
\fBmulti\-master yes|no;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBno\fR
.br
\fIMeaning:\fR Looked at only when a secondary has more than one primary.
.PP
\fBPrimaries in order (BIND \fBrefresh_callback()\fB).\fR A secondary asks its
\fBprimaries\fR in the order written, from the first at every refresh: a primary
that does not answer, answers wrongly or is rejected is skipped; one that has
the same serial is noted and the search goes on (a later one may be ahead); one
that is \fBbehind\fR (older serial, RFC 1982) is logged as \fBserial number (N)
received from primary ... < ours (M)\fR -- not as a failed transfer -- unless
\fBmulti\-master yes\fR, and the search goes on; the first primary that is ahead
is transferred from and ends the refresh. If any primary answered (up to date or
transferred) the SOA REFRESH timer applies, if none did the RETRY timer. Each
primary uses its own \fBport\fR, \fBkey\fR and \fBsource\fR; catalog member
zones use the primaries of their catalog zone. At most 32 primaries are
used. Differences from BIND: the check is an IXFR (or the TCP SOA query of
\fBrequest\-ixfr no\fR), not a UDP SOA query; there is no cache of unreachable
primaries; there is no jitter on the timers.
.PP
\fBSource addresses.\fR Every address a configuration names
(\fBnotify\-source\fR, \fBtransfer\-source\fR, \fBsource\fR of a list, at any
level) is bound once when the server starts: if it cannot be bound the server
does not start (\fBkaridns: Refusing to start: karidns.conf:12: transfer\-source
192.0.2.9: cannot use it as a source address: Can't assign requested
address\fR). The Router (NOTIFY over UDP, an ephemeral port: the address is
\fBnot\fR a listening address) and the connect broker (transfers, and NOTIFY
over TCP, port 0) take the addresses from that start-up set, so \fBan address
that is added by a reload is applied after a restart\fR: the reload answers
\fBOK reloaded (restart needed for: source\-address)\fR, the log names the
statement, and until the restart the system chooses the source. \fBkaricheck
conf\fR accepts a source it cannot test (it may belong to another host) and
lists them.
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
options { transfer\-source 192.0.2.1; notify\-source 192.0.2.1; min\-refresh\-time 60; transfers\-in 20; };
primaries ns\-b source 192.0.2.2 { 198.51.100.1; 198.51.100.2 key "tk"; };
zone "example.test" { type secondary; primaries { ns\-b; 203.0.113.9 port 5353; }; multi\-master yes; };
.fi
.RE
.SS Outbound transfers: one version from start to end (0.7, R11)
An AXFR or IXFR that this server sends is made from \fBone version of the
zone\fR, the one served when the transfer started, from its first message to its
last, whatever happens to the zone meanwhile: a \fBreload\fR or \fBreconfig\fR
that reads a new file, a dynamic UPDATE, a \fBkarictl rollback\fR, a new version
received by a secondary that relays the zone, a database zone that the loader
replaces, the zone removed from the configuration or given another type. This is
RFC 1035 \[u00A7]6.3 (a primary that creates a new version during a transfer
"should continue to send the old version if possible" and "should never send
part of one version and part of another"); RFC 5936 \[u00A7]3 asks the client to
reconstruct the zone as it exists for the given serial, and an IXFR (RFC 1995
\[u00A7]4) is bracketed by the SOA the server had then. The transfer is also
made with the \fBconfiguration as it was when it started\fR (the requester's
location tag, the settings and tag definitions sent in an Extended AXFR,
\fBtransfer\-format\fR, \fBprovide\-ixfr\fR, \fBsend\-extended\-errors\fR): a
reload that changes them, or revokes \fBallow\-transfer\fR, does not change or
end a transfer that runs.
.PP
None of these changes waits for a transfer, and none of them fails because of
one: a \fBreload\fR of a zone that is being transferred answers \fBOK
reloaded\fR, an UPDATE is applied at once (it waited up to 5 s and answered
SERVFAIL before 0.7), a secondary installs the version it received. A client
that reads slowly or not at all (until \fBmax\-transfer\-idle\-out\fR /
\fBmax\-transfer\-time\-out\fR) holds \fBonly the zone version it is sent\fR in
memory: it holds no read section of the server, no retired zone snapshot and no
configuration, so reclamation and reloads go on (\fBkarictl status\fR:
\fBretired zone snapshots waiting\fR stays 0, \fBretired zone versions
waiting\fR counts the version until the transfer ends). A database zone stays in
memory while one of its transfers runs (\fBmemory\-limit\fR takes it out after
the transfer). An IXFR keeps the differences it sends even when later changes
push them out of \fBixfr\-history\fR. What transfers can hold back is bounded:
at most \fBtransfers\-out\fR zone versions at once (one per running transfer;
transfers of the same version share it), plus the IXFR differences those
transfers send, each for at most \fBmax\-transfer\-time\-out\fR (when it is not
0; a client that keeps reading slowly can hold its version that long, one that
stops is cut off after \fBmax\-transfer\-idle\-out\fR).
.SS server <prefix> { ... } (BIND 9.20)
.RS 2
.nf
server <ipv4\-address>[/<length>] | <ipv6\-address>[/<length>] {
    keys <key\-name>;   keys { <key\-name>; };
    transfers <n>;
    transfer\-format one\-answer | many\-answers;
    transfer\-source ( <ipv4> | * );   transfer\-source\-v6 ( <ipv6> | * );
    notify\-source ( <ipv4> | * );     notify\-source\-v6 ( <ipv6> | * );
    request\-ixfr yes|no;  provide\-ixfr yes|no;  request\-expire yes|no;
    edns yes|no;  edns\-udp\-size <n>;  edns\-version <n>;  request\-nsid yes|no;  max\-udp\-size <n>;
    bogus yes|no;  tcp\-only yes|no;  tcp\-keepalive yes|no;  padding <n>;
    send\-cookie yes|no;  require\-cookie yes|no;  query\-source [ address ] ( <ipv4> | * ) [ port ... ];  query\-source\-v6 ...;
};
.fi
.RE
.PP
Top level or inside a \fBview\fR. The statement describes the remote servers an
address or prefix covers (BIND \fBlib/isccfg/namedconf.c\fR
\fBserver_clauses\fR, \fBbin/named/server.c\fR \fBconfigure_peer()\fR). \fBThe
most specific prefix wins\fR (BIND \fBdns_peerlist_addpeer()\fR /
\fBdns_peerlist_peerbyaddr()\fR: the list is ordered longest prefix first; equal
lengths keep the order written; an IPv4 prefix never covers an IPv6
address). \fBA view that has \fBserver\fB statements uses only its own\fR; the
top-level ones are used by the views that have none (BIND takes the first
\fBserver\fR list it finds, view before global; it does not merge). The checks
are those of BIND's \fBcheck_servers()\fR: bits beyond the prefix length
(\fB192.0.2.1/24\fR) are an error, so is the same prefix twice in one list
(\fBalready exists, previous definition: file:line\fR), a \fB\-v6\fR source for
an IPv4 server and a plain source for an IPv6 server (\fBtransfer\-source\-v6 is
not legal for an IPv4 server\fR), an unknown key (\fBunknown key 'k' (in
view 'v')\fR: the key must be global or in the view that uses the statement), an
unknown, ancient (\fBrequest\-sit\fR, \fBsupport\-ixfr\fR) or twice-written
option, and a value that is not a number or boolean. The clamps of
\fBconfigure_peer()\fR apply without a message (\fBedns\-udp\-size\fR and
\fBmax\-udp\-size\fR 512..4096, \fBedns\-version\fR at most 255, \fBpadding\fR
at most 512 with a warning).
.PP
What the options do on an authoritative server (the BIND source places are
\fBlib/dns/zone.c\fR \fBsoa_query()\fR, \fBnotify_send_toaddr()\fR,
\fBzmgr_start_xfrin_ifquota()\fR, \fBxfrin.c\fR, \fBlib/ns/xfrout.c\fR,
\fBlib/ns/client.c\fR):
.TP 8
\fBkeys\fR
The TSIG key that signs the refresh check, the IXFR/AXFR request and the NOTIFY
to that server, and verifies its answers. \fBPrecedence, highest first:\fR the
\fBkey\fR written on the \fBprimaries\fR / \fBalso\-notify\fR element, the
\fBkeys\fR of the \fBserver\fR statement, the zone's \fBtsig\-key\fR
(KariDNS). The \fBkeys\fR of a server statement never makes an \fIincoming\fR
NOTIFY from that server require a signature (BIND uses it for requests only); a
\fBkey\fR written on a \fBprimaries\fR element still does, as before.
.TP 8
\fBtransfer\-source\fR, \fBtransfer\-source\-v6\fR, \fBnotify\-source\fR, \fBnotify\-source\-v6\fR
Local address of the TCP connection to the server / of the NOTIFY to it (the UDP
datagram, or the TCP connection of a NOTIFY over TCP). \fBPrecedence, highest
first:\fR the server statement, the \fBsource\fR of the \fBprimaries\fR /
\fBalso\-notify\fR list the address is written in, the zone, the view,
\fBoptions\fR (\fB*\fR at a level ends the search: the system chooses). The
addresses are bound once at start-up like every source address (see above): an
address new in a reload needs a restart, and one that cannot be bound makes the
server refuse to start, naming the \fBfile:line\fR of the option.
.TP 8
\fBrequest\-ixfr\fR, \fBrequest\-expire\fR
Replace the zone's / view's / \fBoptions\fR' value for requests to that server
(\fBrequest\-ixfr no\fR: the SOA check comes first, signed if the server has a
key).
.TP 8
\fBprovide\-ixfr\fR
Replaces the zone's / view's / \fBoptions\fR' value for IXFR requests \fIfrom\fR
that server (UDP and TCP): one that is behind gets the whole zone (over UDP it
is sent to TCP with TC), one that is up to date still gets the SOA.
.TP 8
\fBtransfers\fR
Replaces \fBtransfers\-per\-ns\fR for that server: at most this many inbound
transfers from the address at a time (\fB0\fR: none).
.TP 8
\fBtransfer\-format\fR
\fBone\-answer\fR: an AXFR or IXFR to that client has one resource record in
each message (RFC 5936 section 2.2 allows it). \fBmany\-answers\fR is the
default.
.TP 8
\fBmax\-udp\-size\fR
Replaces the view's UDP limit for answers to that client (the client's own EDNS
size still applies if smaller). Looked up only when some \fBserver\fR statement
writes it, without allocation.
.TP 8
\fBedns no\fR
The requests carry no OPT record: no EXPIRE option (RFC 7314) and no Extended
AXFR option, so the transfer is a standard one.
.TP 8
\fBedns\-udp\-size\fR, \fBedns\-version\fR, \fBrequest\-nsid\fR
The UDP size and version in the OPT of the requests to the server; an empty NSID
option (RFC 5001). \fBedns\-version\fR other than 0 makes a server answer
BADVERS, as in BIND.
.TP 8
\fBbogus\fR, \fBtcp\-keepalive\fR, \fBpadding\fR, \fBsend\-cookie\fR, \fBrequire\-cookie\fR, \fBquery\-source\fR, \fBquery\-source\-v6\fR
Accepted and checked; \fBno effect in KariDNS\fR (BIND uses them for its
resolver, DS checks of \fBparental\-agents\fR or encrypted transports; KariDNS
sends no DNS COOKIE). \fBkaricheck conf\fR and the server print \fBaccepted, no
effect in KariDNS\fR with \fBfile:line\fR.
.TP 8
\fBtcp\-only\fR
\fByes\fR: the NOTIFY to that server goes over TCP from the start (BIND
\fBnotify_send_toaddr()\fR, \fBdns_peer_getforcetcp()\fR); see NOTIFY over
TCP. The refresh check and the transfers use TCP anyway.
.PP
\fBserver\fR is not part of a database zone's settings text. Example:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
key "xk" { algorithm hmac\-sha256; secret "..."; };
server 192.0.2.0/24 { keys xk; transfer\-source 192.0.2.1; request\-ixfr no; };
server 192.0.2.77   { transfers 1; keys xk; };
zone "example.test" { type secondary; primaries { 192.0.2.77; 192.0.2.78; }; };
view "inside" { match\-clients { 10.0.0.0/8; };
  key "vk" { algorithm hmac\-sha256; secret "..."; };
  server 198.51.100.1 { keys vk; };
  zone "example.test" { type secondary; primaries { 198.51.100.1; }; };
};
.fi
.RE
.SS NOTIFY over TCP
A NOTIFY goes over TCP in two cases, both as BIND 9.20 does it
(\fBlib/dns/zone.c\fR \fBnotify_send_toaddr()\fR and \fBnotify_done()\fR,
\fBlib/dns/request.c\fR):
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBtcp\-only yes;\fB\fR in the \fBserver\fR statement that covers the target
(the most specific prefix of the zone's view, else the top-level ones; it
applies to the addresses of \fBalso\-notify\fR and to the NS hosts alike). The
NOTIFY is not sent over UDP at all.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAfter the UDP attempts.\fR A NOTIFY over UDP that gets no answer after the
last retransmission (\fBnotify\-retries\fR, \fBnotify\-retry\-interval\fR,
\fBnotify\-retry\-backoff\fR: the defaults are BIND's own UDP schedule, 3 tries
5 s apart, then TCP at 15 s), or whose answer cannot be used (the TSIG of an
answer to a signed NOTIFY does not verify: no TSIG, BADSIG, BADKEY, BADTIME), is
made once more over TCP. A bad UDP answer starts the TCP attempt at once, the
remaining retransmissions are not waited for.
.PP
The exchange is one DNS message each way with the two-octet length in front (RFC
1035 section 4.2.2). It has \fBnotify\-tcp\-timeout\fR seconds (default 15,
BIND's TCP timeout of a NOTIFY) for the connection and the answer together. A
failed attempt of \fBtcp\-only\fR is made a second time, as BIND does
(\fBnotify_done()\fR finds the \fBDNS_NOTIFY_TCP\fR flag clear when only the
server statement asked for TCP and retries); after the UDP attempts there is one
TCP attempt. The failure is logged: \fBNOTIFY to 192.0.2.1 port 53 over TCP
failed: <reason>\fR (cannot connect, timed out, the connection was closed before
the answer, the answer has an impossible length, the reply is not an answer to
this NOTIFY, the answer fails TSIG verification ...). The NOTIFY is signed
exactly as over UDP (the \fBkey\fR of the \fBalso\-notify\fR element, else the
\fBkeys\fR of the \fBserver\fR statement, else the zone's \fBtsig\-key\fR; a
fresh time signed for every attempt) and only an answer with the same ID and
QNAME whose TSIG verifies (RFC 8945 section 5.3) counts. The answer is the
peer's: its length is checked before it is read, and a peer that sends nothing,
one octet at a time, or garbage holds only its own attempt for at most
\fBnotify\-tcp\-timeout\fR seconds.
.PP
The connection leaves from the address a UDP NOTIFY would leave from: the
\fBnotify\-source\fR of the server statement, else the \fBsource\fR of the
\fBalso\-notify\fR list, else the zone's, view's, \fBoptions\fR'
\fBnotify\-source\fR / \fBnotify\-source\-v6\fR (\fB*\fR ends the search; see
Transfer and NOTIFY options). Like a transfer, it is made by the connect broker,
not by the sandboxed Backend (which may not \fBconnect()\fR), and it runs in a
thread of its own so that nothing waits for a slow peer: at most 64 exchanges
run at a time and the others wait their turn in the table of pending
NOTIFYs. \fBnotify\-rate\fR counts the first message of an exchange like a first
UDP message. \fBkarictl observatory\fR counts the NOTIFY once (\fBSent\fR),
whichever transport it used.
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
server 192.0.2.53 { tcp\-only yes; keys xk; notify\-source 192.0.2.1; };
zone "example.test" { type master; file "example.test.zone"; also\-notify { 192.0.2.53; }; };
.fi
.RE
.SS database { ... } (in options) and the view database options
Zones can come from a PowerDNS 5.1 database (SQLite3 or PostgreSQL). The loader,
its options, the SQL files, the packages that contain it and the measurements
are described in \fBkaridns\-dbloader(8)\fR. The server never links a database
library: it starts \fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR (the loader), which reads the
database and sends the zones to the Backend over a socket (stream
\fBKDB1\fR). The loader runs as \fBloader\-user\fR, without access to this
configuration file. The Backend builds every zone it receives into a zone of its
own (the same engine as for zone files, so a zone answers byte for byte the same
from a file and from the database) and serves it as a primary (a \fBSLAVE\fR or
\fBCONSUMER\fR zone is a secondary, see "Database zone types" below). File zones
answer at once; DB zones appear as the batches of the loader are published (new
zones become visible when a batch ends and at least once a second during a long
batch), and a line \fBinitial load complete\fR is logged when the first batch is
done. A zone that fails the Backend's checks (the data does not match its
declared count, a record outside the zone, no closing SOA, a malformed
record, ...) is rejected alone: it keeps its last good version, or is absent
when there is none, and \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR shows the reason. A zone from the
database whose name is also defined by a \fBzone { }\fR of \fBkaridns.conf\fR (a
\fBfile\fR, \fBprogram\fR, \fBforward\fR or secondary zone) is hidden by it: the
configured zone answers, a warning is logged, \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR says
\fBshadowed (hidden by a zone in karidns.conf)\fR, and the DB zone is served
again at the next reload after the configured zone is removed. A catalog member
of the same name hides the database zone too, on every path (a catalog update, a
full reconfiguration, and a database zone that arrives after the member): the
member answers, \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR says \fBshadowed (hidden by a catalog
member)\fR, and the database zone comes back as soon as the catalog drops the
member. The settings of a DB zone (who may transfer it or update it,
\fBalso\-notify\fR, \fBixfr\-history\fR, ...) come from its \fBdomainmetadata\fR
and its profile and are applied as described under "Settings of database zones"
below; a zone without any runs with the defaults, so transfers and dynamic
updates are refused. \fBReload.\fR The loader keeps a list of what the server
has (view, zone, \fBdomains.id\fR, serial and the digest of the stream it sent)
and, on \fBkarictl reload\fR, compares it with the database in one snapshot: new
zones, zones whose serial differs, zones the change log lists (an edit that
keeps the serial; the change log needs \fBdb/karidns_ext.sqlite3.sql\fR or
\fBdb/karidns_ext.pgsql.sql\fR, which also ship the other \fBkaridns_*\fR
tables; without it only serials are compared and the loader logs that once) and
zones whose last version was refused are read and converted, and a converted
zone whose digest is the one the server has is not sent. A zone that is no
longer in the database gets a \fBZONE_DELETE\fR and leaves the server with the
next batch. \fBbulk\-threshold\fR decides how a diff reads: above that
percentage of the source's zones one scan of all records (skipping the
unchanged), below it one query per zone. A restarted loader (or a changed
source) compares every zone by digest and sends only what differs from the
server's list, so a loader restart does not resend the zones. \fBkarictl reload
<zone> [force]\fR reads one zone, also one that was just added to the database;
see \fBkarictl(8)\fR. A zone whose last version was refused is offered again by
every reload and every loader restart until it is accepted.
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
options {
    database {
        driver sqlite3;
        database\-file "/var/db/karidns/zones.sqlite3";
        loader\-user "karidns\-db";
        loader\-path "/usr/local/libexec/karidns\-dbloader";
    };
};
view "internal" { match\-clients { 10.0.0.0/8; }; database\-file "/var/db/karidns/internal.sqlite3"; };
view "other"    { database none; };
.fi
.RE
.TP 8
\fBdriver <name>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR required
.br
\fIDescription:\fR \fBsqlite3\fR or \fBpostgresql\fR. The loader must have been
built with it (\fBkaridns\-dbloader \-\-drivers\fR). Restart only.
.TP 8
\fBdatabase\-file "<path>";\fR
\fIDescription:\fR sqlite3: the database of the views without their own
file. The file must be in WAL mode. An error with \fBpostgresql\fR. (The message
that tells the loader about the views, with their names and database paths, must
fit 1 MiB (\fBKDB_MAX_BODY\fR) and lists at most 1024 views; a configuration
that exceeds either is an error when it is read, at start-up, on reload and in
\fBkaricheck conf\fR: \fBdatabase: the CONFIG message for the loader would be N
bytes, above the limit of 1048576\fR. Mark views that take no DB zones with
\fBdatabase none;\fR.)
.TP 8
\fBconninfo\-file "<path>";\fR
\fIDescription:\fR postgresql (required): the libpq conninfo. Mode 0600 or 0400,
owned by \fBloader\-user\fR. An error with \fBsqlite3\fR.
.TP 8
\fBschema "<name>";\fR
\fIDescription:\fR postgresql: the schema of the views without their own. An
error with \fBsqlite3\fR.
.TP 8
\fBloader\-user "<user>";\fR \fBloader\-group "<group>";\fR
\fIDefault:\fR none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Identity of the loader. \fBloader\-user\fR is required when
karidns starts as root (the loader never runs as root); a \fBloader\-user\fR or
\fBloader\-group\fR that resolves to uid/gid 0 (also under another name) is
refused by \fBkaricheck conf\fR and at start (restart only); started as another
user, it must be that user. Restart only.
.TP 8
\fBloader\-path "<path>";\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB/usr/local/libexec/karidns\-dbloader\fR (a build with
\fB./configure \-\-libexecdir=DIR\fR: \fBDIR/karidns\-dbloader\fR;
docs/building.md)
.br
\fIDescription:\fR The loader binary: a regular executable owned by root (or by
the running user when not root) and not writable by group or others; checked at
the start and before every restart. The check is made on the open file that is
then executed (fexecve(2) on FreeBSD), so the file cannot be swapped between the
check and the start; a script (\fB#!\fR) is executed from the descriptor only
when fdescfs(4) is mounted on \fB/dev/fd\fR, otherwise by its path after the
same checks. The loader starts with an empty environment (only \fBTZ\fR) and no
descriptor above 3. Restart only.
.TP 8
\fBacl\-ceiling { ... };\fR
\fIDefault:\fR none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Upper bound for the ACLs of database zones (addresses,
\fB!\fR exclusions, \fBkey "name"\fR of a defined key, named \fBacl\fRs). A
client passes a database ACL only if it is also inside the ceiling; \fBwith no
ceiling, database ACLs refuse everybody\fR. Exact rules under "Settings of
database zones".
.TP 8
\fBacl\-ceiling\-match any|all;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBany\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR How the ceiling combines its entries when it lists both
addresses and keys (milestone B7b): \fBany\fR = a client is inside when its
address matches \fBor\fR its request is signed with a listed key (the meaning of
B7a); \fBall\fR = the address matches \fBand\fR the request is signed with a
listed key. With only one kind of entry in the ceiling that kind decides,
whatever the mode is (\fBkaricheck conf\fR warns that \fBall\fR has no effect
then). BIND's ACLs cannot say "this address and this key", which is why this is
a separate option.
.TP 8
\fBauto\-reload yes;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBno\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Reload by itself when the database changes (milestone B9;
sqlite3; see "Auto reload" below). Off: nothing polls and only \fBkarictl
reload\fR reads the database again. Reloads with \fBkarictl reconfig\fR /
\fBSIGHUP\fR, without restarting the loader.
.TP 8
\fBpoll\-interval <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB2\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Seconds, 1..60: how often the loader asks a view's database
whether anything was committed (\fBauto\-reload yes\fR only); with PostgreSQL
also how often it reads \fBkaridns_reload_requests\fR when no notification came
and how soon it tries again after an error.
.TP 8
\fBcoalesce <ms>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB1000\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Milliseconds, 0..10000: after the first change the loader
waits this long for more before it makes one batch (\fBauto\-reload yes\fR
only); with PostgreSQL also the window in which several reload requests become
one reload.
.TP 8
\fBsigner\-wait yes|no;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fByes\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR A zone with the domainmetadata \fBX\-KARIDNS\-SIGNER\fR =
\fB1\fR is kept signed by \fBkaridns\-signer\fR (0.6.0 item C; see "The signer's
zones" below). With \fByes\fR the loader does not reload such a zone because of
the change log or a moved serial: a change to it is served only after the signer
has signed it and asked for a reload. \fBno\fR: the zone is reloaded like any
other (unsigned changes are served at once). Takes effect with \fBkarictl
reconfig\fR.
.TP 8
\fBdefault\-ttl <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB3600\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR TTL for a \fBrecords.ttl\fR that is NULL, 1..2147483647.
.TP 8
\fBbulk\-threshold <percent>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB25\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR 0..100. When a reload has to read more than this percentage
of a source's zones, the loader reads all records of the source in one scan
(skipping the unchanged zones); below it, the changed zones are read without
scanning the rest: one query per zone, or with PostgreSQL up to 128 zones per
statement. A start, a loader restart or a changed source always compares every
zone.
.TP 8
\fBmax\-zone\-records <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB10000000\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Records of one zone, 2..4294967295.
.TP 8
\fBmax\-zone\-bytes <size>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB1G\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Bytes of one zone on the stream, 65535..64G (K, M, G
suffixes).
.TP 8
\fBdb\-reload\-timeout <s>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB30\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR 1..3600. How long a \fBkarictl reload <zone>\fR of a database
zone waits for the loader's answer; then it gets \fBERROR timeout\fR (the reload
may still be applied). The control thread never waits itself: the client is
parked (at most 32 at once).
.TP 8
\fBingest\-threads <n>;\fR \fBloader\-threads <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB1\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR 1..16 each. \fBingest\-threads\fR is the number of builder
threads of the Backend: with 1 the thread that reads the stream also builds the
zones; with more, complete zones are handed to that many builders (at most that
many zones in flight; read when the server starts). \fBloader\-threads\fR is the
number of workers of the loader: each has its own database connection and
snapshot and reads and converts a disjoint set of zones (consecutive zones in
\fBdomains.id\fR order); the loader writes the results to the stream in
\fBdomains.id\fR order whatever the number is, so the stream (and
\fBkaridns\-dbloader \-\-dump\fR) is byte for byte the same with 1 and with 16
workers. More workers help when converting is the cost (many zones, a cold
start); one zone or a handful is always read by the loader's main thread. Zones
that wait for their turn are held in memory (at most 256 MB). Measured on 4 vCPU
(10^7 records, or 10^6 zones of 11 records): \fBloader\-threads 2\fR cuts a
start by about 40%, \fBingest\-threads 2\fR with it a little more; the defaults
stay 1 (see \fBkaridns\-dbloader(8)\fR, "Measurements").
.TP 8
\fBtransfer\-fetch\-timeout <ms>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB10000\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR D1a, 100..600000 milliseconds: how long an AXFR or IXFR of a
database zone that is out of memory waits for the zone to be read (see "Memory
limit" below). When the time is up the client gets the one error message of RFC
5936 2.2: SERVFAIL with Extended DNS Error 14, Not Ready (RFC 8914 4.15).
.TP 8
\fBfetch\-concurrency <n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBloader\-threads\fR x 16 (at least 16)
.br
\fIDescription:\fR D1a: how many zones may be on their way from the loader at
once while \fBmemory\-limit\fR is set, 2..256. It protects the database: no more
than this many reads are made at a time. A request over the limit \fBwaits\fR
(0.7): the worker parks the query and asks as for any query, and the ingest
thread keeps the request in an ordered queue (the oldest request first, one per
zone, at most 8192 at a time) and makes it when a place is free; if
\fBpark\-timeout\fR runs out first the request is dropped and the query gets
SERVFAIL + EDE 14 like any parked query (counted in \fBpark_timeout\fR, and
\fBqueue_dropped\fR of the \fBfetchq:\fR line). No query is answered "not ready"
for the limit. A query for a zone whose fetch is already on its way waits for it
and asks for nothing. After a reload with another value the places in use are
not taken away: a lower limit takes effect as the fetches end. The loader reads
that many requests in parallel on \fBloader\-threads\fR workers (each with its
own database connection); the warm-up and the walk for apexes use at most half
of it (and at most 8) so that the queries keep the rest. \fBdbstatus\fR shows
the value as \fBconcurrency=\fR in the \fBfetch:\fR line, and the requests that
wait as \fBqueued=\fR in the \fBfetchq:\fR line.
.TP 8
\fBfetch\-rate unlimited|<n>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fBunlimited\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR D1a, changed in 0.7: how many zones per second may be read
from the database for queries (a token bucket with a burst of one second,
1..100000). The default is \fBno limit\fR (\fBunlimited\fR, or \fB0\fR, which
means the same): a query for a zone that is in the database is answered with the
latency of a database read, and the reads are bounded by
\fBfetch\-concurrency\fR alone; with no limit the bucket does not exist and
costs the query path nothing. Set a rate to protect a database that cannot take
that many reads (a remote or shared PostgreSQL, say). The requests over the rate
\fBwait\fR for a token like the requests over \fBfetch\-concurrency\fR (in the
same queue, in order, up to \fBpark\-timeout\fR), they are not refused: at a
rate below what the queries ask for the waits grow to \fBpark\-timeout\fR and
the queries then get SERVFAIL + EDE 14, so the rate is a bound on what the
database sees, not a defence against the clients. The warm-up, the walk for
apexes and the background requests take tokens from the same bucket (the
background requests and the summaries only from a bucket that is more than half
full, and they do not wait: one that finds none is dropped, counted as
\fBrefused_by_rate\fR in \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR). \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR shows
\fBrate=unlimited\fR or \fBrate=N/s\fR. Since 0.6.2 a loader thread reads about
4,400 zones per second (SQLite3, 1M zones of 11 records, 8 vCPU).
.TP 8
\fBmemory\-limit <size>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR D1a: how much memory the zones of the database may hold;
zones that do not fit are read when a query needs them and taken out again. See
"Memory limit" below.
.TP 8
\fBloader\-state\-file "<path>";\fR
\fIDefault:\fR none
.br
\fIDescription:\fR D1a step 7, restart-only: the file the loader keeps the
\fBhot list\fR in (the zones of the database most asked for, hottest first), so
that a restart can read them back into memory before the first query. It has an
effect only with \fBmemory\-limit\fR (the frequencies it is made from are kept
only then). The path is absolute, or relative to \fBoptions { directory
}\fR. The file is written and read by the loader, which runs as
\fBloader\-user\fR: that user needs write and search permission on the
\fBdirectory\fR of the file (the loader writes \fB<path>.tmp\fR and renames it
over the file, so a reader never sees half a file; the temporary file is created
with mode 0600 and never through a link) and read permission on the file. Give
the loader a directory of its own (\fB/var/db/karidns/state\fR, owned by
\fBloader\-user\fR, mode 0700): a directory that others can write in lets them
replace the list the server warms from (the zone names in it can only name zones
of the database; a damaged or planted file cannot make the server read anything
but zones it has, but it can fill the memory with the wrong ones). A missing
file is the first start; a file that is empty, truncated, damaged (its last line
carries the count and a checksum) or larger than 64 MiB is ignored with a log
line (\fBstate file <path> is ignored: <why>\fR) and replaced by the next hot
list. \fBkaricheck db\fR checks the directory and the file. See "Warm-up" below.
.TP 8
\fBhot\-list\-size <n>;\fR \fBhot\-list\-interval <s>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB10000\fR, \fB60\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR D1a step 7: how many zones the hot list keeps (1..100000) and
how often the Backend makes a new one (1..86400 seconds). A list is made from
the frequency counters of the zones (a slice of the registry at a time, like the
cache manager's rounds) and is not sent when no zone was asked for; the loader
merges it into the file: the zones asked for come first, the zones of the old
list nobody asked for since stay behind them up to the size, so a quiet night
does not empty the list.
.TP 8
\fBwarm\-before\-serve <s>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB0\fR (off)
.br
\fIDescription:\fR D1a step 7: hold the queries back, for at most this many
seconds (0..3600), until the zones of the hot list have been read. See "Warm-up"
below.
.TP 8
\fBpark\-max <n>;\fR \fBpark\-timeout <ms>;\fR
\fIDefault:\fR \fB1024\fR, \fB1500\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR D1a, queries for database zones that are not in
memory. \fBpark\-max\fR (1..65536, read when the server starts): how many such
queries ONE WORKER holds while it waits for the zone (the limit is per worker,
not for the server: with N workers the server holds up to N times as many; the
table is allocated at start and its pages are not used until a query
parks). Each entry takes about 4.5 KB (a parked UDP request of up to 4096 bytes
is kept in it), so the largest value, 65536, is up to about 300 MB of address
space per worker once the table is full; the default 1024 is about 4.5 MB per
worker; a query beyond that, or one longer than 4096 bytes, is answered at
once. \fBpark\-timeout\fR (10..60000 milliseconds): how long a parked query
waits; then the answer is SERVFAIL with Extended DNS Error 14, Not Ready (RFC
8914 4.15), as it is when the fetch of the zone failed (signed with the key of a
validly signed request, RFC 8945 5.3; it counts for
\fBrate\-limit\fR). \fBallow\-query\fR (and \fBallow\-query\-on\fR) is evaluated
first, with the same chain as for a zone in memory, before a query parks or the
zone is asked for: a client that may not ask gets REFUSED + EDE 18, uses no
entry of the park table, makes the server read nothing and learns nothing about
the zone. DoT and DoH, which do not park, answer SERVFAIL + EDE 14 for such a
zone (REFUSED for a client that may not ask). A DB zone's own settings cannot
contain \fBallow\-query\fR, so for a zone out of memory the view's and options'
statements decide. Without \fBmemory\-limit\fR nothing takes a zone out of
memory, so no query parks and these options change nothing (see "Memory limit"
below and \fBkaridns\-dbloader(8)\fR, "The fetch stream").
.PP
| \fBserver\-id "<id>";\fR | the host name | postgresql (milestone B11): the
name under which this server writes its row of \fBkaridns_server_status\fR
(1..64 bytes; keep to ASCII letters, digits and \fB. \- _ :\fR: \fBkaricheck
conf\fR warns about anything else). Every server that shares a database needs
its own. When it is not set the host name is used; a host name longer than 64
bytes means no status row is written (one warning in the log, \fBdbstatus\fR
says so) until \fBserver\-id\fR is set. A warning in \fBkaricheck conf\fR with
sqlite3 (ignored). See "Several servers on one PostgreSQL database" below. |
.PP
| \fBalias\-resolver { <ip>; ... };\fR | none | The resolvers for the ALIAS
records of the zones of the database when neither the zone's settings nor
\fBoptions { alias\-resolver }\fR name any (port 53; see POWERDNS ALIAS AND LUA
RECORDS). |
.PP
In a \fBview\fR: \fBdatabase\-file "<path>";\fR (sqlite3) or \fBdatabase\-schema
"<name>";\fR (postgresql) gives the view its own source; \fBdatabase none;\fR
makes it take no DB zones. A view without these uses the global source.
.PP
\fBWhich zones a view gets (\fBkaridns_domain_views\fB, milestone B6b).\fR A
view that uses the global source takes the zones the table
\fBkaridns_domain_views (domain_id, view_name)\fR of that database assigns to it
(\fBdb/karidns_ext.*.sql\fR; edits are in the change log, so \fBkarictl
reload\fR moves a zone between views, with a \fBZONE_DELETE\fR in the old one):
.IP \(bu 2
a domain with \fBno row\fR is served by every view that uses the global source
(D6); a database without the table behaves the same;
.IP \(bu 2
a domain with rows is served by the views they name and by no other; view names
are compared case-insensitively;
.IP \(bu 2
a row that names a view that does not use this database as its source (no such
view, a view with \fBdatabase none;\fR, a view with its own file) is logged once
per batch and ignored; a zone that only such rows map is served by no view (so a
database shared with another server, whose views are not here, is never served
by mistake), and a syslog warning names such zones (the first three and their
count, once per batch);
.IP \(bu 2
a view with its \fBown\fR \fBdatabase\-file\fR / \fBdatabase\-schema\fR takes
all zones of that file; a \fBkaridns_domain_views\fR inside it is ignored; a
view with \fBdatabase none;\fR takes none.
.PP
\fBkaridns\-dbloader \-\-dump ... \-\-map\-view <view>\fR applies the same rule
to a dump. File paths and schema names may contain any valid UTF-8 and are used
byte for byte (\fB%XX\fR is not decoded); invalid UTF-8 and control characters
are configuration errors.
.PP
\fBPostgreSQL (milestone B10).\fR
.PP
\fBdriver postgresql;\fR reads the same PowerDNS 5.1 tables as the SQLite3
driver (\fBkaridns\-dbloader \-\-drivers\fR lists \fBpostgresql\fR when the
loader was built with \fBmake karidns\-dbloader DB_DRIVERS="sqlite3
postgresql"\fR; \fBkaridns\fR itself never links libpq). PostgreSQL 13 or newer.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBConnection.\fR \fBconninfo\-file\fR names a file with the libpq connection
string (\fBhost=/tmp dbname=pdns user=karidns_loader\fR, or a
\fBpostgresql://\fR URI; \fB#\fR starts a comment line). It must be a regular
file owned by \fBloader\-user\fR with mode 0600 or 0400 (it can hold a
password), else the loader refuses it before reading it; its content is never
logged and libpq's own text is not copied into a message when the string is
invalid. A UNIX socket or \fBsslmode=verify\-full\fR is recommended
(\fBkaricheck conf\fR warns otherwise). Keywords in the string win over the
loader's defaults (\fBconnect_timeout=10\fR, TCP keepalives,
\fBapplication_name=karidns\-dbloader\fR). The session is read only
(\fBdefault_transaction_read_only\fR), \fBclient_encoding\fR is \fBSQL_ASCII\fR
(bytes are passed through like the SQLite3 driver does), and
\fBstatement_timeout\fR (15 min per statement or FETCH), \fBlock_timeout\fR (10
s) and \fBidle_in_transaction_session_timeout\fR (1 h) are set.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSchemas.\fR \fBschema "<name>";\fR (global) and \fBdatabase\-schema
"<name>";\fR (per view) set the \fBsearch_path\fR of the connection to that
schema; a view without one uses the global source and
\fBkaridns_domain_views\fR, exactly as with SQLite3.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBReading.\fR A consistent read is \fBBEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ
READ ONLY\fR; every scan is a cursor fetched 1000 rows at a time, so a table of
10^7 records is never held in memory. The loader role needs \fBSELECT\fR on the
PowerDNS tables and the \fBkaridns_*\fR tables and only the column privilege
\fBSELECT (domain_id, active, published)\fR on \fBcryptokeys\fR (it never reads
key material): \fBdb/karidns_loader_role.pgsql.sql\fR grants exactly that
(\fBpsql \-v role=karidns_loader \-v schema=public \-f
db/karidns_loader_role.pgsql.sql DB\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBErrors.\fR A lost connection, an administrator shutdown, a statement or lock
timeout and similar transient SQLSTATEs make the loader back off (1, 2, 4 ... 30
s) and read again with a new connection; other errors (a missing table or
column, a denied privilege) fail the view and are shown by \fBkarictl
dbstatus\fR; the last good zones keep being served.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBChange log and auto reload.\fR \fBdb/karidns_ext.pgsql.sql\fR (schema version
3; version 2 added \fBxid\fR) creates \fBkaridns_changes\fR with statement-level
triggers (one row per distinct domain per statement) and \fBNOTIFY
karidns_changes\fR. With \fBauto\-reload yes;\fR the loader keeps one connection
per view that does \fBLISTEN karidns_changes\fR; its socket is in the loader's
\fBpoll()\fR set, so a commit is seen at once (about the \fBcoalesce\fR window
plus the time to read and build the zones), not at the next
\fBpoll\-interval\fR. After a reconnect the loader compares what changed
meanwhile. Rows become visible when their transaction commits, in another order
than their \fBseq\fR numbers were taken, so each log row carries the \fBxid8\fR
of its transaction and the loader's position in the log is the \fBxmin\fR of its
snapshot, not a \fBseq\fR: a transaction that took a lower \fBseq\fR but commits
later is never skipped (\fBdbstatus\fR shows that position as \fBapplied change
seq\fR). Rows of transactions that are still open are read again by the next
batch (the comparison after them is idempotent). The log keeps the newest
1,000,000 rows; a loader that finds its position pruned compares every zone.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSchema version 1\fR (\fBkaridns_changes\fR without \fBxid\fR,
\fBkaridns_zone_tagdefs\fR without \fBid\fR) is read: the loader serves it
without a change log (zones are compared by serial; \fBkaridns_zone_tagdefs\fR
in physical order) and says so; \fBdb/karidns_ext_v1_to_v2.pgsql.sql\fR converts
a database (SQLite3: \fBdb/karidns_ext_v1_to_v2.sqlite3.sql\fR). Versions 2 and
3 are read as they are (version 3 adds the schema \fBkaridns_auth\fR and the
audit log, which the loader does not use); a \fBkaridns_schema_version\fR above
3 is refused by both drivers.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSeveral servers on one database\fR (milestone B11):
\fBkaridns_reload_requests\fR / \fBkaridns_server_status\fR and
\fBserver\-id\fR, see the next section.
.PP
\fBSizes and times (milestone B13).\fR Measured on 4 vCPU with SQLite3: 10,000
zones of 1,000 records (10^7 records) are served 24 s after the start (13 s with
\fBingest\-threads 2; loader\-threads 2;\fR) and use about 1.1 GB in the
Backend; 1,000,000 zones of 11 records take 33 s (19 s) and about 3 GB. A
restart reads the whole database again (there are no persisted images in this
release), and so does a restarted loader before it reports its first batch,
while the Backend keeps answering. \fBkarictl reload <zone>\fR of one zone takes
about 20-30 ms at 10,000 zones and 76 ms at 100,000 zones
(\fBtests/run_db_zoneadd_perf_test.sh\fR) and 5-11 ms at 1,000,000 zones (the
loader looks the zone up by name). At 1,000,000 zones a one-zone change is
served 15-25 ms after the command (100 changes); the occasional 0.13-0.52 s of
the beta (the control thread's one-second walk of every zone, and the
grace-period wait whose sleeps doubled to 131 ms) is gone; auto reload follows a
commit within about \fBpoll\-interval\fR plus \fBcoalesce\fR (1.0-1.3 s with 1 s
and 200 ms). If the loader dies it is restarted after 1 s, 2 s, 4 s ... 60
s. All figures, the thread sweep and the recommended \fBingest\-threads\fR /
\fBloader\-threads\fR are in \fBkaridns\-dbloader(8)\fR, "Measurements".
.PP
\fBAuto reload (\fBauto\-reload yes;\fB, milestone B9).\fR Every
\fBpoll\-interval\fR seconds the loader asks each view's database, on a
connection it keeps open for that, for \fBPRAGMA data_version\fR (a change made
by any other connection; one pragma, whatever the size of the database). Only
when it changed does it look at the end of the change log \fBkaridns_changes\fR:
no new rows, nothing to do. The first change starts a coalescing window of
\fBcoalesce\fR milliseconds (later changes do not extend it); when it ends the
loader makes one \fBbatch of kind \fBauto\fB\fR for that view: the same
comparison as \fBkarictl reload\fR (the zones the change log lists since the
last load, zones whose serial differs, zones the Backend refused,
\fBZONE_DELETE\fR for zones that left the database, and the digest gate: a zone
whose data did not change is not sent). The serial need not change. A change is
visible after at most \fBpoll\-interval\fR + \fBcoalesce\fR + the time to read
and build the zones (about 3 to 4 s with the defaults, measured in
\fBtests/run_db_autoreload_test.sh\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
A view that shares the global source polls and loads on its own (its zones come
from \fBkaridns_domain_views\fR); a view with its own \fBdatabase\-file\fR has
its own connection.
.IP \(bu 2
A gap in the change log (rows pruned before the loader read them) or a log that
was reset makes the batch compare every zone. A \fBdatabase file that was
replaced\fR by another file (another inode) or deleted is noticed by name at
every poll: the loader reopens it and compares every zone; while the file is
missing the zones keep being served, \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR shows the error, and
the loader tries again at every poll. A failed poll or load is logged once,
shown by \fBdbstatus\fR and retried at the next poll.
.IP \(bu 2
Without \fBkaridns_changes\fR (a plain PowerDNS database) auto reload still
notices every commit, but compares zones by serial only, so an edit that keeps
the serial is not picked up (\fBkarictl reload <zone>\fR reads one zone). The
loader says so in the log once at the start of the view. Apply
\fBdb/karidns_ext.sqlite3.sql\fR to get the full behaviour.
.IP \(bu 2
Switching \fBauto\-reload\fR on with \fBkarictl reconfig\fR or \fBSIGHUP\fR
checks once for what changed while it was off; switching it off, or changing
\fBpoll\-interval\fR / \fBcoalesce\fR, takes effect at once. An explicit
\fBkarictl reload\fR is answered as always, between auto batches (a batch in
progress finishes first); the auto batch that follows finds nothing to send.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBkarictl dbstatus\fR shows \fBauto reload: on (poll\-interval ...,
coalesce ...)\fR, the number of auto batches since the start and, per view, the
time of the last poll, the change log position of the last complete load
(\fBapplied change seq\fR), the changes seen and not applied yet (\fBpending
changes\fR) and the last error; the last batch line says \fBkind auto\fR. The
loader reports these with the stream message \fBAUTO_STATUS\fR (see
\fBdns_db_stream.h\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThe signer's zones (\fBsigner\-wait\fB).\fR \fBkaridns\-zone import
\-\-signer\fR (and \fBkaridns\-signer(8)\fR after it) puts the metadata
\fBX\-KARIDNS\-SIGNER\fR = \fB1\fR on a zone it keeps signed. A change to such a
zone, written by anyone else (the WebAdmin, a script, PowerDNS), changes records
that have no valid signature yet; a validating resolver must never see a changed
RRset with a stale signature. So with \fBsigner\-wait yes;\fR (the default) the
loader leaves the version it has: a change in the change log, or a serial that
moved, does not make it read the zone. The signer sees the change in the log,
signs the zone and writes the signed data with its serial, then asks for the
reload: on PostgreSQL a row in \fBkaridns_reload_requests\fR, anywhere with
\fBkarictl reload <zone>\fR. A reload request reads the zone as it is, and the
loader remembers, for each zone of the signer that a request read, the position
of the change log up to which it read it (the highest seq with SQLite3, the xmin
of the snapshot with PostgreSQL): the rows of that zone up to that position are
in what the request read, so the poll of the auto reload that reaches them later
does not put the zone on the list again; a change made after the request
does. What is \fBnot\fR held back: a zone the Backend does not have yet (a new
zone is loaded whole, also with the metadata), a full comparison (a loader that
starts, restarts, finds its place in the change log gone, or sees the database
file replaced; \fBkarictl reload\fR of every zone; a \fBforce\fR request) and a
zone the Backend refused. In those moments an unsigned change can be served; the
signer catches up within seconds. \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR shows the zones that
wait, under the view: \fBwaiting for the signer: 2 zone(s): example.test,
example.net\fR (the first six names, then \fBand N more\fR); the line is absent
when no zone waits, and a signer that stopped shows as a count that does not go
down. PowerDNS on the same database reads the database live and serves a change
at once: documented in \fBkaridns\-dbloader(8)\fR. The setting is not used by a
database without the metadata. The waiting set is per view (a zone that waits in
one view does not hold the same name back in another), and a waiting zone that
loses the metadata is read as it is at the next batch. A plain \fBkaridns\-zone
import\fR into such a zone replaces the data and keeps its DNSSEC records and
the signer's metadata (\fBkaridns\-zone(8)\fR); the loader then holds the new
data back until the signer has signed it.
.IP \(bu 2
PostgreSQL does not poll: see "PostgreSQL" below (\fBLISTEN\fR);
\fBpoll\-interval\fR is then only the interval of a liveness check and of the
reconnect attempts.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBA PostgreSQL hot standby\fR refuses \fBLISTEN\fR ("cannot execute LISTEN
during recovery"), so nothing announces a commit there. The loader says so once
("auto reload: the server refuses LISTEN (a standby?); the change log is read
every N s instead") and, every \fBpoll\-interval\fR seconds, reads a fingerprint
of \fBkaridns_changes\fR (the number of rows and the highest \fBseq\fR, one
index-only count); when it changed, the batch is made as after a
notification. The latency is then the \fBpoll\-interval\fR, the \fBcoalesce\fR
window and the replication lag (about 0.8-1.0 s with \fBpoll\-interval 1\fR and
\fBcoalesce 200\fR on a local standby, see karidns-dbloader). Reload requests
(\fBkaridns_reload_requests\fR) and \fBkarictl reload\fR work as on a primary;
the status row cannot be written on a read-only server: that is logged once and
shown by \fBdbstatus\fR, and the server keeps serving.
.PP
\fBSeveral servers on one PostgreSQL database (milestone B11).\fR The tables
\fBkaridns_reload_requests\fR and \fBkaridns_server_status\fR
(\fBdb/karidns_ext.pgsql.sql\fR, per schema like the other \fBkaridns_*\fR
tables; PostgreSQL only, they are optional and need no new schema version: apply
the file again to a version 2 database) let a management tool make every server
reload and see what each of them did. This is the "WebAdmin inserts a row"
design of the release plan (4.8): no network path to the DNS hosts and no agent.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAsking for a reload.\fR After the data is committed, insert a row: \fBINSERT
INTO karidns_reload_requests DEFAULT VALUES;\fR (every zone of the schema),
\fBINSERT INTO karidns_reload_requests (zone) VALUES ('example.test');\fR (one
zone), or with \fBforce\fR true (compare and send everything even when the
Backend has the version; like \fBkarictl reload <zone> force\fR). The
\fBgeneration\fR of the row (an identity column) numbers the requests. An
\fBAFTER INSERT\fR trigger sends \fBNOTIFY karidns_reload\fR; only the newest
1000 requests are kept. A \fBBEFORE INSERT\fR trigger takes a transaction-level
advisory lock on the table so that two requesters get their generations in the
order they commit, which is what lets a loader remember "the last generation I
applied" without ever skipping a request; the second requester waits until the
first commits, so commit soon after inserting. Because the request row is
committed after the data, a replica that shows the request already has the data
(WAL order).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBWhat each server does.\fR Every \fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR of a PostgreSQL view
whose schema has \fBkaridns_reload_requests\fR keeps one more connection (one
per schema, shared by the views that read it) that does \fBLISTEN
karidns_reload\fR (its socket is in the loader's \fBpoll()\fR set) and, every
\fBpoll\-interval\fR seconds, reads the rows above the last generation it
applied; a standby that cannot \fBLISTEN\fR is served by that timer
alone. \fBauto\-reload\fR is \fBnot\fR needed. At start the highest generation
counts as applied: the startup load, which comes after the connection is opened,
covers it. Requests that arrive within the \fBcoalesce\fR window are one reload:
a request for all zones (zone NULL), a hole in the generations (requests pruned
or rolled back), or more than 64 different zones makes it a comparison of the
whole schema (a batch of kind \fBdiff\fR, the same as \fBkarictl reload\fR),
otherwise each listed zone is reloaded (kind \fBzone\fR); \fBforce\fR is the OR
of the requests. A zone name that is not a valid name is ignored and reported as
a failed zone. A request counts as applied only after every view that reads the
schema was loaded; when the database cannot be read the same requests are read
again at the next poll.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBWhat each server reports.\fR When the Backend has answered the zones of that
reload, the loader writes one row to \fBkaridns_server_status\fR
(\fBserver_id\fR, \fBgeneration\fR = the highest request applied,
\fBapplied_at\fR, \fBok\fR, \fBfailed_zones\fR) in a \fBBEGIN READ
WRITE ... COMMIT\fR of its own (an upsert on \fBserver_id\fR). \fBok\fR is false
when any zone failed: one the loader could not read or convert (a record that
does not parse) or one the Backend rejected; \fBfailed_zones\fR then has one
\fBzone: reason\fR line per zone (at most 64 lines and 4 KiB, then \fB... and N
more\fR; control characters, DEL and bytes above 0x7E are written as
\fB\eDDD\fR). The outcome of the startup load is reported the same way when
requests exist. \fBSELECT server_id, generation, ok, applied_at FROM
karidns_server_status;\fR is the overview: a server that is down, or behind,
keeps its older generation. "At the same time" means within seconds with visible
status; it is not atomic.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThe role.\fR \fBdb/karidns_loader_role.pgsql.sql\fR gives the loader role
\fBSELECT\fR on \fBkaridns_reload_requests\fR (with the other \fBkaridns_*\fR
tables) and \fBINSERT, UPDATE\fR on \fBkaridns_server_status\fR when the table
exists (run it again after adding the tables). It stays
\fBdefault_transaction_read_only = on\fR; only the status write is an explicit
\fBREAD WRITE\fR transaction on the watcher's own connection, never inside the
\fBREPEATABLE READ\fR snapshot a load reads from.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBWhen the status cannot be written\fR (no privilege, the table is gone, the
connection is lost) the server keeps serving and keeps applying requests; the
loader is not put into an error state. It logs the error once (again when the
text changes), tries the write again every 10 s and \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR shows
it (below).
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBkarictl dbstatus\fB\fR shows, per view that has the tables, \fBreload
requests (view v): server\-id ns1, last applied generation 7, ok yes, status
written <time>, last error: none\fR (\fBok no\fR when a zone of the last reload
failed; \fBstatus written never\fR before the first write; the last error is the
text of a failed read of the requests or write of the status). The loader
reports this in the optional tags 6..11 of the \fBAUTO_STATUS\fR stream message
(\fBdns_db_stream.h\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNot replicated:\fR Dynamic UPDATE (the changes are discarded when the zone
reloads, as for file zones); a request cannot make a server do anything but
reload.
.PP
\fBSeveral servers as primaries (milestone B11b).\fR Two or more servers that
read one database can all be the primary of a zone: a secondary can take the
zone from any of them, or from all of them in turn. For that, the same database
state (and the same configuration) must give the same zone on every server, and
it does; \fBtests/run_db_dual_primary_test.sh\fR checks it with two servers.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThe same on every server.\fR \fBkaridns\-dbloader \-\-dump\fR of one state is
byte for byte the same from separate processes (with 1 or 16
\fBloader\-threads\fR, per schema, per \fB\-\-map\-view\fR): the loader orders
every scan by the primary keys of the tables (the tag definitions of a schema
version 1 database by their physical position) and nothing in the stream depends
on the process, the host or the time. The SOA serial is the one stored in the
database (no \fBSOA\-EDIT\fR emulation; the metadata kind is ignored with a
warning). The AXFR of every zone is the same RR sequence, in the same order, not
only the same set: NSEC / NSEC3 zones that are rebuilt from the PowerDNS
presigned layout, tinydns zones and \fB$LOCATION\fR / ECS tagged zones (as each
requester address sees them), zones of views, and the ALIAS and LUA RRs
themselves. Query answers are the same too (the order of the RRs of an RRset is
the stored one; KariDNS does not rotate it). LUA records are never run (see
POWERDNS ALIAS AND LUA RECORDS), so they need no care.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBIXFR (RFC 1995 \[u00A7]4, \[u00A7]6).\fR The differences a server keeps
(\fBixfr\-history\fR) are in memory and are the versions \fIthat server\fR
loaded; a server that was restarted has none, and a server that applied two
edits in one reload (the \fBcoalesce\fR window) has one step where another has
two. An IXFR from version X is the chain of the differences from X up to the
current version. Two servers give \fBthe same answer when both hold the same
chain\fR (for example X to the current version is one step on both: that step is
the same RRs in the same order). A server that does not hold X answers with the
whole zone, AXFR style, which RFC 1995 \[u00A7]4 allows ("if incremental zone
transfer is not available, the entire zone is returned"); \[u00A7]6 describes
the same trade-off for servers that condense versions. The secondary ends in the
same state either way. What is \fInot\fR promised is that two servers answer one
IXFR with the same bytes when their histories differ.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNot the same on every server\fR (listed so that nothing is a surprise):
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
\fIALIAS expansion.\fR With an \fBalias\-resolver\fR the A and AAAA answers at
an ALIAS name are what each server's resolver gave at the moment of its lookup
(addresses, remaining TTL, stale answers after a failure), so two servers can
differ, also between two queries of one server. The zone transfers carry the
ALIAS RR itself and are the same. \fBkaricheck conf\fR warns when
\fBserver\-id\fR and an \fBalias\-resolver\fR are both set.
.IP \(bu 2
\fIDynamic UPDATE\fR is applied to the server that receives it and is not
mirrored (D-UPDATE); persistent changes go through the database and a reload
request. The update is gone when that zone reloads.
.IP \(bu 2
\fItinydns time stamps and countdown TTLs\fR are evaluated at the moment of the
query or transfer with the clock of the server: when a time stamp passes, the
servers differ by the difference of their clocks, and the serial does not change
(keep the servers on NTP). An Extended AXFR carries the time stamps themselves.
.IP \(bu 2
\fIServer cookies\fR are made with a random secret per process unless
\fBcookie\-secret\fR is set; use the same \fBcookie\-secret\fR on all servers
that answer for one address or are used by one client (RFC 7873 \[u00A7]6, RFC
9018 \[u00A7]5).
.IP \(bu 2
\fITime.\fR "At the same time" is within seconds (see the reload requests
above): a request that has been applied by ns1 and not yet by ns2 shows two
serials, and \fBauto\-reload\fR makes each server follow on its own
schedule. Compare \fBkaridns_server_status\fR (or \fBdag <zone> SOA\fR at each
server) to see that both are at the state you expect. Servers that read
different replicas also see the states of different moments.
.IP \(bu 2
\fIConfiguration.\fR Options that change the data a server serves
(\fBdefault\-ttl\fR, \fBixfr\-history\fR, \fBmax\-zone\-records\fR,
\fBacl\-ceiling\fR, \fBalias\-resolver\fR, ...) must be the same on all servers,
and so must the zones that are not in the database.
.RE
.PP
Everything except \fBdriver\fR, \fBloader\-user\fR, \fBloader\-group\fR,
\fBloader\-path\fR, \fBloader\-state\-file\fR and the presence of the block
reloads (\fBSIGHUP\fR / \fBkarictl reload\fR): the Backend then sends the loader
a CONFIG with the new generation. These five are logged as "take effect only
after a restart" (\fBwarm\-before\-serve\fR is read once, at the start).
.PP
The supervisor starts the loader after the other processes. A loader exit is not
fatal: it is restarted after 1, 2, 4 ... 60 s (back to 1 s after 10 minutes of
uptime), with a new socket handed to the Backend. File zones keep answering
meanwhile. \fBSIGHUP\fR to all processes (\fBpkill \-HUP karidns\fR) does not
affect the loader; it ends on \fBSIGTERM\fR with the other processes. The
Backend treats everything from the loader as untrusted: a protocol violation is
logged once, the stream is closed and the loader is restarted. \fBkaricheck
conf\fR checks the block without opening a database (driver in the loader,
users, loader file, SQLite WAL and mode, conninfo-file). More than 1024 views
that take DB zones (not marked \fBdatabase none;\fR) are a configuration error
for \fBkaridns\fR and \fBkaricheck conf\fR: the CONFIG message that tells the
loader its views lists at most 1024.
.PP
\fBkaricheck db [view]\fR (B12) checks the database before the data goes live:
it runs \fBkaridns\-dbloader \-\-check\fR (read only, the same reader as the
load) as \fBloader\-user\fR when root runs it, and reports the schema, every
zone with the checks of \fBkaricheck zone\fR, the settings texts against the
keys and \fBacl\-ceiling\fR, the views a zone is mapped to, and the zones of
\fBkaridns.conf\fR that hide a database zone (see \fBkaricheck(8)\fR "Database
checks"). \fBkaricheck conf\fR still checks the block without a connection.
.SS Memory limit (D1a)
\fBdatabase { memory\-limit <size>; }\fR (\fBK\fR, \fBM\fR, \fBG\fR suffixes, 1M
to 1024G; unset = no limit, every database zone stays in memory and nothing in
this section applies) is a target for what the zones of the database hold in
memory, not a hard limit: the versions in use, the previous versions kept for
rollback, \fBand\fR the ones that were replaced or taken out but are not freed
yet -- except, since 0.7, retired versions that a reader or a transfer holds
(\fBheld\fR, see below): taking zones out cannot free them, so they are not
weighed against the limit. Since 0.7 it counts what a zone holds while it is in
memory: its version (the image and the glue block, each at the size the
allocator really holds, not the size asked for), the IXFR differences, \fBand
what the zone's entry costs by itself\fR -- the entry (about 0.65 KB with the
allocator's rounding), the lock it makes (about 0.1 KB on FreeBSD) and, for a
database zone, its settings block: about 0.85 KB per zone, which is about 40% of
a zone of eleven records (before 0.7 the limit counted only the version and the
image, 1.3 KB of the 3.2 KB such a zone needs). The entry's share is counted
with the zone's first version and goes with it when the zone is taken out of
memory. It does not count the directory (every zone of the database, about 0.45
KB each, listed by \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR), the park tables or the loader's
process. A reload of \fBkaridns.conf\fR changes it: within 50 ms when a limit is
set, within a second when there was none.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBmemory\-limit\fB is not a limit on the size of the process (design
8.1).\fR It counts what the zones in memory hold, not what the process holds on
top: the directory (every zone of the database, in memory or not), the response
cache, the park tables, the threads' buffers and the allocator's slack. \fBThe
formula (0.7):\fR the resident size of the Backend is about \fB70 MB + 0.45 KB x
(zones in the database) + the \fBresident\fB bytes of the \fBcache:\fB line of
\fBkarictl dbstatus\fB\fR, and \fBresident\fR stays at 90% to 95% of the limit
once the limit is reached. The loader is a separate process and comes on top
(see below). The formula was checked on the build host (FreeBSD 15.1 amd64,
jemalloc, 8 virtual CPUs, SQLite3, zones of eleven records and of about 100
records, every zone asked for once so that the limit evicts) at 100,000 and
1,000,000 zones with limits of 64M, 256M and 1G: it is within \-4% to +9% of all
eight points (1,000,000 zones: 566 MB at \fB64M\fR, 732 MB at \fB256M\fR, 1,409
MB at \fB1G\fR; the formula gives 577, 760 and 1,471 MB). \fBBefore 0.7 the
limit did not count the entry, its lock and the allocator's rounding\fR, so the
Backend needed about 2.5 times the limit for the zones; the figure of "about 700
MB at \fB512M\fR" that this manual gave for a million zones was wrong for small
zones. \fBSizing example:\fR to keep the Backend within about 1.5 GB of RAM with
a million zones, the formula gives 70 MB + 0.45 GB for the directory + the
limit, so \fBmemory\-limit 1G\fR is the largest that fits (\fB512M\fR: about 1.0
GB; \fB1G\fR: about 1.5 GB); never set the limit to the RAM you have, and leave
room for a burst of transfers (the limit is a target, see below). The figures
are those of one host and one data set, not a guarantee: watch \fBresidency\fR
and \fBcache\fR in \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR and the process's own resident size
for a few days before relying on a figure. The tables below show the
measurements.
.RS 2
.PP
\fBMemory by number of zones.\fR The resident size of the Backend (\fBps \-o
rss\fR) 10 seconds after the load, in MB, one \fBkaridns\fR on an idle
host. \fISmall\fR is a zone of 11 records (SOA, 2 NS, A, MX, 6 A hosts),
\fImedium\fR one of 98 records. "DB, all resident" is a database without
\fBmemory\-limit\fR.
.PP
