Before any zone is asked for, the Backend holds only the directory: 67 MB at
100,000 zones and about 340 MB at 1,000,000. With the same zones and no limit
the loader needed 54 MB (100,000 zones) and 213 MB (1,000,000).
.PP
\fBHow to read it.\fR The Backend is a fixed part (about 70 MB with the threads'
buffers and the park tables), plus about 0.45 KB for each zone of the database
that is in the directory (also for a zone that is out of memory;
\fBmemory\-limit\fR does not bound it), plus what the zones in memory hold,
which is the part \fBmemory\-limit\fR bounds (at 90% to 95% of it). The loader
is not bounded by it either: with \fBmemory\-limit\fR it read the summaries of
all the zones at the start, so it needed about 550 MB at 1,000,000 zones and 635
MB once zones were being read for queries (about 65 to 150 MB at 100,000 zones),
and it must be counted on top of the Backend (without a limit it is smaller,
about 0.2 KB a zone, because it only streams). \fBRule of thumb:\fR
\fBmemory\-limit\fR = (the memory you can give the server - 70 MB - 0.45 KB x
zones - the loader's share) / 1.1, rounded down, and then at most 1.5 times that
in a burst (see below). For 1,000,000 small zones and 2 GB for the Backend and
the loader together that is about \fB512M\fR (Backend about 1.0 GB, loader 0.6
GB). If the zones asked for in a day fit under the limit nothing is taken out;
if the working set is larger, a higher limit trades memory for fewer waits
(\fBmisses\fR and \fBhit_rate\fR in \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR). Without a limit,
plan about 2.5 KB for each small zone (and the zone's own size for a larger one)
on top of the fixed part. Since 0.7 the server does this arithmetic itself and
says it in syslog (next item).
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBfetchq:\fB (0.7): where a fetch is held up.\fR \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR has
the line \fBfetchq: in_flight=N peak=N table_full=N ring_dropped=N park_full=N
park_timeout=N loader_busy=N ingest_busy=P% hist=A,B,C,D,E\fR right after the
\fBfetch:\fR line. Everything is counted since the server started, except
\fBin_flight\fR (now) and \fBingest_busy\fR (the last window). Read it from the
query to the install: \fBpark_full\fR counts queries that found the worker's
table of parked queries full (\fBpark\-max\fR) and were answered "not ready" at
once; \fBring_dropped\fR, queries whose request for the zone could not be handed
from the worker to the ingest thread (the worker's ring of 128 was full: the
ingest thread is behind); \fBfetch\-rate\fR, \fBfetch\-concurrency\fR and
\fBmemory\-limit\fR refuse requests before the fetch is made
(\fBrefused_by_rate=\fR and \fBshed=\fR of the other lines); \fBtable_full\fR,
requests refused because all 256 places of the fetch table were taken (reachable
only without \fBmemory\-limit\fR, which stops requests at
\fBfetch\-concurrency\fR, at most 256, first); \fBin_flight\fR and \fBpeak\fR
are the fetches on their way now and the most there have been at one time
(compare \fBpeak\fR with \fBfetch\-concurrency\fR to see whether the limit is
the one that held requests back); \fBloader_busy\fR, fetches the loader turned
away because its own queue of requests was full; \fBhist\fR is the time from the
request to the install of the zone, for the fetches that succeeded, in five
counts (up to 1 ms, up to 10 ms, up to 100 ms, up to 1 s, more);
\fBpark_timeout\fR, parked queries whose zone was not there before
\fBpark\-timeout\fR ran out; \fBingest_busy\fR is the share of the last ten
seconds in which the ingest thread was working and not waiting for something to
do (0 for the first ten seconds; near 100% means the thread is the
limit). Typical readings: a high \fBingest_busy\fR with a slow \fBhist\fR points
to the Backend (ingest thread or install), a \fBhist\fR with most counts in the
last two buckets and a low \fBingest_busy\fR to the loader or the database,
\fBtable_full\fR or \fBring_dropped\fR above 0 to more simultaneous misses than
the fetch path takes.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSOA questions for a zone that is out of memory.\fR The summary of a zone that
sends NOTIFY (the effective \fBnotify\fR is not \fBno\fR) or has
\fBallow\-transfer\fR also carries the zone's apex: the SOA (MNAME, RNAME, TTL,
minimum; the refresh, retry and expire are the summary's), the NS RRset (at most
8 targets, the lowest TTL of the RRs, RFC 2181 5.2) and the addresses of the NS
targets that are inside the zone (the \fBA\fR and \fBAAAA\fR records of those
names: at most 8 for a name, in the order of the zone, the lowest TTL of the
records of one type), which the answer carries in Additional (glue, RFC 2181 9)
as the zone in memory does. A question for the SOA of the apex of such a zone (a
secondary that polls the serial) is answered from it, without reading the zone,
with the same bytes the zone in memory gives (RFC 1035 3.3.13: the SOA in
Answer; the NS RRset in Authority unless \fBminimal\-responses\fR leaves it
out), and through the same checks as every answer (\fBallow\-query\fR, TSIG,
\fBrate\-limit\fR, EDNS). Everything the summary cannot give makes the query
wait for the zone as before: DO = 1 (the RRSIGs), a cookie, ECS or another EDNS
option the body of a query handles, another type, an NS target in another zone
of the view (a sibling: the zone in memory takes its addresses from that zone;
with \fBadditional\-from\-auth no\fR there is no glue at all, with
\fBin\-domain\fR only the targets at or below the apex count), a zone with
\fB$LOCATION\fR / ECS tags or tinydns attributes, a zone with more than 8 NS
records or more than 8 addresses for one NS target, and a zone whose data has
been in memory since the summary (the apex is dropped when the data is
installed, except one that has the loader's addresses of NS hosts outside the
zone (see "NOTIFY for a zone that is out of memory") and describes the version
that is installed: it stays, so that a zone that is taken out of memory again
keeps them; a zone that the cache manager takes out of memory gets a new one,
built from the image that leaves, so the SOA question that follows does not read
it again). The size of the answer on UDP is the one of the zone in memory (the
EDNS size of the client, \fBudp\-bufsize\fR, 512 without EDNS). A summary that
comes without an apex (the settings of the zone said \fBnotify no\fR and no
\fBallow\-transfer\fR) gets one when a reload makes the zone keep it: after
\fBkarictl reconfig\fR or SIGHUP the Backend asks the loader for the summary of
every zone that is out of memory, keeps an apex now and has none (at most 8 at a
time on the fetch stream; the zones are not read, so \fBmemory\-limit\fR does
not hold these requests back, also while the database zones are over the limit;
\fBfetch\-rate\fR still paces them, and the queries come first: a summary for
the apex takes a token only from a bucket that is more than half full; a request
for the zone and one for its summary do not stand in for each other, and a
summary that was left out because the zone was on its way is asked for again if
that fetch fails). A zone for which the loader sent a summary without any apex
(more than 8 NS names, more than 8 addresses for a name, location tags or
tinydns attributes, no usable SOA) is not asked again by the next reload; it is
asked again after its serial changes. A summary for a zone whose fetch is on its
way is applied as it is when it describes the version the directory has (it
brings the apex); the body of that zone is left out of memory as before. The
garbage that summaries leave (replaced apex blocks and settings) is published as
soon as 1024 entries are waiting, not at the pace of the delta interval (after a
failed publication, out of memory, the delta interval is the pace again). A
fetch of the whole zone that comes back after the directory has learnt of
another version of the zone (a newer summary or body arrived while it was on its
way) is dropped, not installed; the requests that wait for it are woken and ask
again. The bytes of versions that were replaced or taken out and are not freed
yet (\fBretired\fR) do not count for the hard limit that sheds fetches for
queries, and a zone that a dynamic UPDATE changed counts as pinned. A zone whose
body the Backend leaves out of memory because of the limit is answered
\fBNOT_RESIDENT\fR (KDB1), the loader then sends its summary with the apex. Cost
per zone: 8 bytes in the directory element and, for the zones that keep it, a
block of 40 bytes plus the names as wire names (MNAME, RNAME and the NS targets,
about 20 bytes each) plus the allocator's rounding: about 130 to 160 bytes for a
zone with two name servers outside the zone, so about 150 MB for a million zones
that all keep it (a zone with \fBnotify no\fR and no \fBallow\-transfer\fR keeps
none); each address of an NS target inside the zone adds 10 bytes (A) or 22
bytes (AAAA), so a zone whose two name servers are inside it, each with an A and
an AAAA record, is 64 bytes more (before the allocator's rounding).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThe directory.\fR Every zone of the database is in the directory of its view
(name, serial, SOA timers, settings, digest); the data is in memory only for the
zones the limit leaves room for. A query for a zone whose data is out of memory
waits (up to \fBpark\-timeout\fR) while the loader reads the zone on its fetch
stream (\fBkaridns\-dbloader(8)\fR), and is answered from it. \fBThe start reads
the directory only\fR (design 3.3): with \fBmemory\-limit\fR set the loader
sends, for a zone the Backend has no data of, its summary instead of the zone
(the settings, the serial, the SOA timers; no \fBrecords\fR, no tags), at the
start, after a restart of the loader and in every full comparison. A zone is
read when a query, a transfer or the warm-up asks for it (the fetch stream); a
pinned zone is read right after its summary. With a million zones of eleven
records the start takes about 3 s and the Backend holds about 200 MB instead of
20 s and 2.5 GB (build host, 4 vCPU, \fBloader\-threads 4\fR, \fBmemory\-limit
1g\fR). A zone that has its data in memory is sent whole when it changes, as
before; a changed zone that is out of memory is sent as a summary (its new
serial reaches the directory; the next read gets the new data). Without
\fBmemory\-limit\fR nothing changes: the loader streams every zone at the start
and the Backend keeps them all. When the limit is only reached later (a zone
read for a query), the zones of a batch that do not fit are still discarded as
in step 5: they are counted as \fBcold\fR in \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBWarm-up (the hot list and the state file).\fR With \fBmemory\-limit\fR and
\fBloader\-state\-file\fR the Backend makes, every \fBhot\-list\-interval\fR
seconds, a list of the \fBhot\-list\-size\fR zones with the highest frequency
(the same counters the cache manager uses: a query that had to wait counts, and
so does every query a zone in memory answers, folded in from the zone's own
query counter; the pass over the registry is done in slices of 20000 zones, so
it does not hold the lock longer than a cache round does, and the query path
does no new work). The list goes to the loader (\fBHOT_LIST\fR on the first
stream, in parts when it is longer than one message holds: at most 16384 zones
and 1 MiB each), which merges it into its state file and answers
\fBHOT_ACK\fR. After a start, and after every restart of the loader, the loader
sends the zones of the file back (\fBWARM_LIST\fR, once, after its startup load:
an empty list when there is no usable file), and the Backend puts them, in that
order, in the list of zones to read (the list a pinned zone and the walk for
apexes use, too). They are read \fBwhole\fR, with the priority \fIwarm\fR: after
the fetches that queries wait for, through \fBfetch\-rate\fR (a zone of the list
takes a token only from a bucket that is at least half full, so the queries come
first) and \fBmemory\-limit\fR (not while the database zones are above the limit
or \fBfetch\-concurrency\fR reads are in flight), at most 8 at a time and only
while fewer than 8 fetches (at most half of \fBfetch\-concurrency\fR) are in
flight. \fBThe warm-up stops at the high water mark of the limit (95%)\fR: the
zones in flight are counted at the average size of the zones of the list read so
far (one at a time until the first is in), and when the next zone would take
memory above the mark the rest of the list is dropped (\fBdbstatus\fR counts
them under \fBmemory limit\fR; the log says so), so the warm-up never makes the
cache manager take a zone out of memory that is in use. A zone of the list that
a query read meanwhile, or that is gone, is skipped. The zones the warm-up read
have no queries yet, but each starts with a frequency of its rank in the list
(the first has the number of zones in the list, the last has 1), so the cache
manager does not take out the zones that were read to be kept before the ones
that nobody asked for; the queries that follow add to it. The room for the zones
in flight is counted twice (one zone of the list at a time near the mark), so
the warm-up passes the mark by about one zone at most. A list is never trusted:
the loader accepts at most 100000 zones, the parts in order, only views of its
CONFIG and canonical names; the Backend accepts at most 100000 zones, only names
of its directory, and a zone twice is read once.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBwarm\-before\-serve\fB.\fR With \fBwarm\-before\-serve <s>\fR the workers
do not read their sockets until the zones of the list have been read (the
loader's list has come and nothing of it is waiting or in flight) or \fB<s>\fR
seconds have passed, whichever comes first. \fBWhat is held is the reading of
the sockets, not the answer\fR: the kernel queues the UDP datagrams (up to the
socket buffer) and the TCP connections wait in the listen queue, so a client
that asks during the wait gets its answer when it ends (or retries, as for any
slow server) and sees a server that is slow for a few seconds, not one that
answers SERVFAIL, which a resolver would remember against the server. Answering
SERVFAIL during the wait was not chosen: a resolver that gets SERVFAIL marks the
server as bad and stops asking for a while, which is the failure the option is
meant to avoid. The control channel is not held: \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR shows
\fBwarm\-before\-serve: holding the queries (N ms left)\fR, then \fBqueries
accepted after N ms\fR, and \fBkarictl reload\fR and the others work. The wait
begins when the Backend has started its database thread, so it also covers the
load of the directory, and it ends at the time given even when the loader has
not delivered a list (no loader, a database that does not answer), so a broken
database cannot keep the server mute. Without \fBloader\-state\-file\fR (or
without \fBmemory\-limit\fR) there is no list and the wait ends when the
directory is loaded. The default is off: the server accepts queries as soon as
the directory is read, and a query for a zone that is not in memory waits
(\fBpark\-timeout\fR) as before.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBkarictl dbstatus\fB shows it.\fR \fBresidency: directory=N resident=N
out_of_memory=N evictions=N\fR (the zones of the directory with and without
data); \fBqueries: answered=N parked=N hit_rate=P%\fR (the queries the zones
answered, counted by the zones themselves, and the ones that had to wait for
their zone; the hit rate is the share answered by a zone that was already in
memory; \fBparked\fR is the number since the start; the queries a version
answered are kept when a new version replaces it or it is taken out of memory;
the \fBresidency\fR line is counted at most once a second, so it can be up to a
second old); \fBwarm: received=0|1 queued=N loaded=N skipped=N (memory limit N,
already in memory or gone N) failed=N pending=N in_flight=N\fR (the progress of
the list); \fBwarm\-before\-serve: off | holding the queries (N ms left) |
queries accepted after N ms\fR; and \fBstate\-file: <path>
at_start=loaded|missing|corrupt, ignored|unreadable, ignored (N zones)
hot_lists_sent=N last_sent=<time> (N zones) acks: written=N unchanged=N failed=N
last_ack=<what> (N zones in the file)\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBfetchq:\fB (0.7): where a fetch is held up.\fR \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR has
the line \fBfetchq: in_flight=N places=N peak=N table_full=N ring_dropped=N
park_full=N park_timeout=N loader_busy=N ingest_busy=P% hist=A,B,C,D,E queued=N
queue_peak=N waited=N queue_dropped=N queue_full=N\fR right after the
\fBfetch:\fR line. Everything is counted since the server started, except
\fBin_flight\fR and \fBqueued\fR (now) and \fBingest_busy\fR (the last
window). Read it from the query to the install: \fBpark_full\fR counts queries
that found the worker's table of parked queries full (\fBpark\-max\fR) and were
answered "not ready" at once (true overload); \fBring_dropped\fR, queries whose
request for the zone could not be handed from the worker to the ingest thread
(the worker's ring of 128 was full: the ingest thread is behind); the requests
that cannot be made at once because of \fBfetch\-concurrency\fR,
\fBfetch\-rate\fR or \fBmemory\-limit\fR \fBwait\fR (0.7): \fBqueued\fR is how
many wait now, \fBqueue_peak\fR the most there have been, \fBwaited\fR how many
had to wait (since the start), \fBqueue_dropped\fR how many ran out of
\fBpark\-timeout\fR while they waited (their queries get SERVFAIL + EDE 14 from
their workers, see \fBpark_timeout\fR), and \fBqueue_full\fR how many were
refused because the queue (8192 requests) was full, which is the only refusal of
a query left (also counted in \fBshed\fR of the \fBcache:\fR line); a
\fBqueued\fR that stays above 0 while \fBin_flight\fR is 0 and the bucket is not
empty would be a request that is stuck: please report it; \fBtable_full\fR,
requests refused because all 256 places of the fetch table were taken (normally
0: \fBfetch\-concurrency\fR, at most the 256 places, stops the requests of whole
zones first, so only a request that comes while summaries of zones are in flight
can find the table full next to a \fBfetch\-concurrency\fR of 256);
\fBin_flight\fR and \fBpeak\fR are the fetches on their way now and the most
there have been at one time (compare \fBpeak\fR with \fBfetch\-concurrency\fR to
see whether the limit is the one that held requests back, and \fBwaited\fR to
see how many it held), \fBplaces\fR the places of \fBfetch\-concurrency\fR in
use now: the whole-zone fetches that are in flight (0 at rest; a value that
stays above 0 while \fBin_flight\fR is 0 would be a place that was not given
back: please report it); \fBloader_busy\fR, fetches the loader turned away
because its own queue of requests was full; \fBhist\fR is the time from the
request to the install of the zone, for the fetches that succeeded, in five
counts (up to 1 ms, up to 10 ms, up to 100 ms, up to 1 s, more);
\fBpark_timeout\fR, parked queries whose zone was not there before
\fBpark\-timeout\fR ran out; \fBingest_busy\fR is the share of the last ten
seconds in which the ingest thread was working and not waiting for something to
do (0 for the first ten seconds; near 100% means the thread is the limit; the
thread handles at most 64 requests or about a millisecond at a time and then
reads the loader's answers, so a flood of queries cannot keep it from installing
the zones that arrive). Typical readings: a high \fBingest_busy\fR with a slow
\fBhist\fR points to the Backend (ingest thread or install), a \fBhist\fR with
most counts in the last two buckets and a low \fBingest_busy\fR to the loader or
the database, a \fBqueued\fR that is high and a \fBpeak\fR equal to
\fBfetch\-concurrency\fR to a limit that is too low for the load,
\fBqueue_dropped\fR or \fBpark_timeout\fR above 0 to more simultaneous misses
than the fetch path takes.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTaking zones out (the cache manager).\fR A thread looks every 50 ms, at a
slice of at most 20000 zones of the registry at a time (the lock is held for a
time that does not grow with the number of zones - measured on the build host:
about 3 ms for a slice of 20000 zones that all have data, 1.3 ms per round with
1,000,000 zones in the registry; a pass over all zones takes N/20000 rounds),
and the ageing below is applied to each zone once in a pass. Above 95% of the
limit it takes zones out of memory until the zones taken out so far would bring
the total to 90%, in the order of the lowest \fIfrequency x cost / size\fR first
(GDSF; the cost is a fixed part plus a part per byte, so a small zone that is
asked for as often as a big one is kept first). The frequency is the zone's
query counter, folded into a counter per zone at each round and halved every 10
seconds (which is how old popularity is forgotten); the queries that had to wait
for the zone count in it too. A zone that came into memory less than 100 ms ago
is not taken out (its waiting queries are answered first), and a more valuable
zone is not taken out while cheaper ones that are only too young would cover
what is left to free. The bytes of a zone that was taken out count against the
limit until its entry is freed (a reader of the old snapshot may hold it: after
the next publication of the zones and the drain of the older snapshot, typically
100 ms to a second), and are discounted from what the next round has to take
out. Pinned zones are never taken out (with \fBcache pin;\fR the pinned zones
alone may exceed the limit: a warning is logged and every other zone is taken
out).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAdmission.\fR A zone that is read because a query waits for it is kept only
if it is asked for at least as often as the zones the last round took out;
otherwise it answers the waiting queries and is the first to go. The same for a
zone with \fBcache no\-cache;\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPrevious versions (\fBkarictl rollback\fB).\fR The version that a database
install replaces is kept for \fBrollback\-window\fR (default 1 hour) and counts
against the limit as its own class, \fBprevious\fR (\fBcache: ... previous=N\fR,
and the line \fBzone versions kept for karictl rollback\fR of \fBkarictl
dbstatus\fR; \fBkarictl status\fR has the same numbers). Nobody reads it, so
when the zones are above 95% of the limit the cache manager drops previous
versions first, the one whose time ends first first, and takes a zone out of
memory only for what is still to be freed after that. A zone that was rolled
back is \fBpinned\fR like one that a dynamic UPDATE changed (the old version
exists only in memory), until the database's data change.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBA read and a change at the same time.\fR A zone that was read for a query is
installed only if the version it brings is the one the directory has when it is
installed, or the directory has not changed since the read was asked for (a
changed zone whose body was left out of memory has already given the directory
its new serial; the older data is dropped and the waiting queries ask again, the
newer data is installed). A read that ends with its data dropped does not lose
the request for the zone's summary that was waiting behind it: the zone is put
on the warm list again. A dynamic UPDATE looks the zone up and then takes the
writer lock; the zone can be taken out of memory (or replaced by a load) in the
few microseconds between the two, and the UPDATE then fails with SERVFAIL and is
sent again by the client (RFC 2136 3.8 leaves the order of concurrent UPDATEs
open; a zone that came into memory less than 100 ms ago is not taken out, so the
window needs a worker stopped for longer than that).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPins.\fR The metadata \fBX\-KARIDNS\-CACHE\fR (or \fBcache <mode>;\fR in a
profile) is \fBpin\fR (never taken out), \fBno\-cache\fR (taken out as soon as
its queries are answered) or \fBauto\fR (the default: frequency decides). A
pinned zone is read at the start even above the limit.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThe limit is a target, not a hard cap.\fR Plan for up to about 1.5 times the
value in normal operation when database batches change zones that are in memory
(about 1.1 times without batches), and set \fBmemory\-limit\fR with that margin
below the memory the process may use for zone data. The cache manager enforces
it; usage can exceed it for a while, by design: (1) a zone is installed (read
for a query, or a changed zone of a batch) before the next round of the manager
can take another out, and the manager runs every 50 ms; (2) a zone that was
taken out counts against the limit until its entry is freed, which happens after
the next publication of the zones and the drain of the older snapshot (typically
100 ms to a second), and a version that a reader or a long transfer (AXFR) still
holds counts as long as it is held; (3) a zone that came into memory less than
100 ms ago is not taken out, and a more valuable zone is not taken before
cheaper ones that are only too young would cover what is left. What keeps the
overshoot bounded: no zone is read for a query while the database zones are
above \fB110%\fR of the limit, nor while \fBfetch\-concurrency\fR reads are
already in flight (default \fBloader\-threads\fR x 16) (the request of the query
then waits, for room or for a place, see below), and \fBfetch\-rate\fR, when it
is set, limits how fast zones are read. The expected overshoot is the zones read
in about a second at the rate the queries ask for zones that are out of memory:
with the load of \fBtests/run_db_cache_test.sh\fR (4 clients, about 1000 queries
a second, 28% of them for zones that are out of memory) the largest value seen
was 108% of the limit; the test accepts at most 115% plus 256 KB. Where the 1.5
comes from: what is served stops growing by fetches at 110 % of the limit, but a
batch installs the changed zones that are in memory regardless; on top come for
a moment the old versions a batch replaces (freed after a grace period that
normally ends in milliseconds, at the latest when the ingest has published 1024
more zones or at the end of the batch: up to the size of every zone in memory if
one batch replaces all of them), the zones taken out until their entry is freed,
and the previous versions until the manager drops them (they count and go
first). So the bound in theory is about twice the limit plus the previous
versions, for a batch that replaces every zone in memory at once; measured with
the zones and the load of \fBtests/run_db_cache_test.sh\fR (800 zones,
\fBmemory\-limit 3m\fR, 4 clients) and a batch that changes every zone every 2 s
or 100 zones every second, \fBpeak\fR was 1.34 to 1.52 times the limit (1.10
without batches). \fBThe exception:\fR while a thread stays long in its read
section (rare; \fBkarictl status\fR \fBretired zone versions waiting\fR, and the
warning after 60 s), the versions replaced or taken out meanwhile come on top
until it leaves (measured: 1.6 times the limit with three replacements of every
zone in memory during a 90 s hold; 2.2 times when queries brought 620 more zones
in during a 60 s hold, \fBtests/run_db_memlimit_reader_test.sh\fR);
\fBlong_reader=1\fR in the \fBcache:\fR line of \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR shows
such a reader (sampled every second); the cache manager does not count them
(option B, 0.7) and does not take zones out or leave a batch's zones out for
them, and a version a stalled outbound zone transfer pins is counted the same
way.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBOver the hard line: room on demand (0.7).\fR The 110% line that keeps the
overshoot bounded (above) never turns a query away. A query for a zone that is
out of memory while the zones in memory are above the line is parked like any
other (UDP and TCP; DoT and DoH do not park and get SERVFAIL + EDE 14 for such a
zone) and its zone is asked for; the ingest thread, seeing the zones in memory
above the hard line, takes zones out of memory \fBnow\fR (no waiting for the
next round of the manager) and makes the request when the zones in memory are
back under the line; the query is answered from the zone, on its first try (this
is for servers on which queries for zones that do not fit \fBmemory\-limit\fR
are normal: a million zones and a small limit). The request waits in the same
queue as the requests that wait for a place of \fBfetch\-concurrency\fR or a
token of \fBfetch\-rate\fR: the oldest first, one entry per zone (a second query
for the same zone asks for nothing), holding the mark of its zone and nothing
else (no place and no token are taken until the request is made, so the requests
that wait cannot use up the pool). If there is no room before
\fBpark\-timeout\fR (nothing can be taken out: everything that is left is
pinned, changed by an UPDATE, or too young), the request is dropped and the
queries get SERVFAIL + EDE 14 when their own time is up, like any parked query
(counted in \fBpark_timeout\fR of the \fBfetchq:\fR line and in
\fBroom_timeouts\fR; the retry of the client then finds the zones that the
manager has taken out meanwhile). A reload (\fBkarictl reconfig\fR, SIGHUP)
while requests wait loses none of them and leaves nothing behind: they go on
waiting for what the new settings ask (a reload that takes \fBmemory\-limit\fR
away makes them go at once). Zone transfers wait in the same queue; the
background fetches (a CNAME or the glue of a name server) and the warm-up are
not queries and do not wait: they are dropped above the line as before.
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
\fBWhat is taken out, and how thrashing is avoided.\fR The same order as the
cache manager's round: zones marked evict-next first, then the lowest frequency
per byte (GDSF), so the coldest zones go first; pinned zones, zones a dynamic
UPDATE changed and zones that a transfer reads never go; a zone that is not in
memory (being fetched) has nothing to take. \fBOnly as much as is needed\fR:
what the hard line is exceeded by, plus room for the requests that wait (their
number times the average size of the zones in memory), and never below the
manager's low mark (90% of the limit), so a burst of waiting requests cannot
empty the cache. \fBThe minimum residency is kept for every zone that is not
needed\fR: a zone that came into memory less than 100 ms ago is taken only when
the older zones do not cover what is needed, and only when it has answered at
least one query (so nobody waits for it: a parked query waits for a zone that is
\fInot\fR in memory, and a woken one is answered within a turn of the worker's
loop) and has been in memory for 10 ms; a zone that has served nobody yet, such
as one that was just fetched for a query that is still being answered, is never
taken before its 100 ms are over. The slice of the registry that is looked at is
the manager's (20000 zones at a time, a few slices at most for one request), and
the scan is not repeated within 10 ms when it found nothing, so a burst cannot
make the thread scan the registry for every request. \fBdbstatus\fR shows the
counts: \fBroom_evictions\fR (zones taken out on demand, also in
\fBevictions\fR) and \fBroom_relaxed\fR (of those, the ones younger than 100
ms).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBWhat it costs.\fR The memory of a zone that is taken out is freed after the
next publication of the zones and the drain of the older snapshot (100 ms to a
second, see above) and counts in \fBretired\fR until then, while the fetches
that the room is made for go on (at \fBfetch\-rate\fR, when it is set): the
zones in memory plus \fBretired\fR can therefore pass the 110% by about the
zones read in that time. Set \fBmemory\-limit\fR with that margin, and
\fBfetch\-concurrency\fR (and \fBfetch\-rate\fR) to what the host and the
database can read.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBkarictl dbstatus\fB\fR has a line \fBcache:
limit=... resident=... retired=... previous=... inflight=... held=... long_reader=... evictions=... rounds=... cold=... marked=... misses=... shed=... pinned=... peak=... victim_freq=... room_evictions=... room_relaxed=... room_waits=... room_timeouts=... room_waiting=...\fR:
\fBretired\fR is what was replaced or taken out and is not freed yet,
\fBinflight\fR the part of it that was taken out, \fBheld\fR (0.7) the part of
it that a reader or a transfer holds (versions kept after a short wait for their
grace period did not end, or retired more than a second ago, and while
\fBlong_reader\fR is 1 every retired version except those taken out
(\fBinflight\fR) and the previous versions dropped under memory pressure, which
are not shown on their own: so \fBheld\fR + \fBinflight\fR can be below
\fBretired\fR; not weighed against the limit), \fBlong_reader\fR (0.7) 1 while a
thread of the server has been in a read section for a second or more, \fBcold\fR
the zones that came without data, \fBmarked\fR the zones that were read but are
not kept (admission), \fBmisses\fR the queries that had to wait for a zone,
\fBshed\fR the requests that were refused (0.7: only the true refusals are left:
a request that found the wait queue full, and the background requests that were
dropped; a query that waits for a place, a token or room is not shed),
\fBpeak\fR the largest resident + retired + previous seen when a zone was
installed or a round ran, \fBroom_evictions\fR the zones taken out on demand for
queries that waited for room and \fBroom_relaxed\fR the ones of them that were
younger than the minimum residency, \fBroom_waits\fR the requests that had to
wait for room (counted once each), \fBroom_timeouts\fR those of them that ran
out of \fBpark\-timeout\fR before there was room, \fBroom_waiting\fR those that
wait for room now (all since the start except \fBroom_waiting\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBZone transfers of a zone that is out of memory (AXFR, IXFR).\fR The client is
checked first, as \fBallow\-query\fR is for a parked query:
\fBallow\-transfer\fR (the zone's \fBALLOW\-AXFR\-FROM\fR within
\fBacl\-ceiling\fR, the zone's TSIG keys, \fBtransfer\-acl\-match\fR), a bad or
missing TSIG, \fBtransfers\-out\fR and the other statements decide from the
settings the directory kept for the zone, and a request that is refused is
answered REFUSED (or NOTAUTH / FORMERR) exactly as for a zone in memory,
\fBwithout the zone being read\fR. A request that may be made is handed to a
transfer thread (a thread of its own, as for a zone in memory; no worker waits),
which asks the Backend's ingest thread for the zone through the same fetch
stream as the queries (\fBfetch: issued=\fR counts it) and polls for it: the
request obeys \fBfetch\-concurrency\fR, \fBfetch\-rate\fR and
\fBmemory\-limit\fR (room above 110% of the limit) like a query's and waits in
the same queue (0.7; an entry is dropped after \fBpark\-timeout\fR, and the
transfer asks again every 100 ms, which makes a new one) until
\fBtransfer\-fetch\-timeout\fR (default 10 s). A zone that cannot be had in that
time (the loader is down or cannot read it, the memory stays over the limit, the
queue stays too long for the time) gets the single error message of RFC 5936
2.2: SERVFAIL with Extended DNS Error 14 (Not Ready, RFC 8914 4.15), with the
question copied and signed with the key of a validly signed request (RFC 8945
5.3); the connection is not just dropped (RFC 5936 4.1.2). A zone for which the
loader has no usable version (state ERROR) is answered SERVFAIL at once. Once
the zone is published in the usual way the transfer is the usual one: it counts
in the zone's \fBactive_axfr\fR, so the cache manager does not take the zone out
of memory while the transfer runs, and its answer is the answer of a zone in
memory (the same record set for AXFR, for an IXFR from an old serial the whole
zone in the AXFR-style answer of RFC 1995 4, because the history of differences
is kept in memory only and a zone that was out of memory has none). A zone that
\fBthe transfer\fR brought into memory is marked evict-next (and with it
"brought by a transfer"): the cache manager takes it out as soon as the transfer
is over, before any other zone and also when there is no memory pressure, so
that transfers (a secondary that transfers a million zones) do not push the
zones the queries use out of memory. The mark is not made, or is dropped, when
the zone is pinned (\fBcache pin\fR), was changed by a dynamic UPDATE (it is
never taken out), was in memory before (a transfer from memory changes nothing),
is asked for more often than the zones the manager took out last (its
frequency), or when a query asks for it while the transfer runs (the manager
sees the query counter move at its next round). \fBAn IXFR from a client that is
up to date\fR (the serial it sends equals the serial of the directory's version
or is newer, RFC 1982) is answered with the one SOA of RFC 1995 2 \fBfrom the
summary's apex, without reading the zone\fR (a secondary that polls with IXFR
does not make the server read its zones; the answer has the same SOA, the OPT
record of the first message, the RFC 7314 EXPIRE option and the KariDNS
extension echo that the zone in memory gives, and is signed when the request
was); it needs the apex of the version the directory has (a zone with
\fBallow\-transfer\fR or \fBnotify\fR has one, see above), otherwise the zone is
read. An IXFR without the client's SOA is FORMERR (RFC 1995 3) without a
read. \fBAn IXFR over UDP\fR (RFC 1995 4.2) is a zone transfer question too:
\fBallow\-transfer\fR (the zone's \fBALLOW\-AXFR\-FROM\fR, \fBacl\-ceiling\fR,
the TSIG key of the request) is checked first, for a zone in memory and for one
that is out of memory, and a client that may not transfer is REFUSED with EDE 18
(as BIND's \fBns_xfr_start()\fR does before it looks at the transport; before
this change a UDP IXFR was answered to anybody who could ask); a client that may
gets, for a zone that is out of memory, the one SOA from the summary's apex
without a read (the SOA only, TC=1 when the client is behind so that it asks
again over TCP), byte for byte what the zone in memory answers, and waits for
the zone only when the apex is missing or describes another version. Requests of
transfer threads and of workers for a zone take turns in the Backend's request
queue (a busy worker cannot starve a transfer). A zone whose NOTIFY waits in the
ring is not taken out of memory meanwhile. \fBkarictl dbstatus\fR counts the
waits, the zones got, the failures, the summary answers and the marks.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNOTIFY for a zone that is out of memory (RFC 1996).\fR When the serial of a
zone changes while its data is out of memory -- the loader sends a summary with
a new serial, or a body that is left out of memory because of the limit and then
the summary -- the Backend sends the NOTIFY messages without reading the zone,
and when a body of another serial than the directory had is installed for a zone
that was out of memory (a changed zone the loader sent whole because it thought
the zone was in memory), where the install of a new entry sends none by
itself. The NOTIFY is asked for when the summary's apex of the new version is
here (an apex of another version is never used); the ingest thread queues the
zone (a ring of 1024; a zone that does not fit is marked and queued later by the
control thread's sweep as the ring empties, so a change of more zones than the
ring holds is notified in full, 256 zones a second at most; \fBkarictl
dbstatus\fR: \fBnotify of zones out of memory: queued= deferred=\fR) and the
control thread, which owns the table of NOTIFYs that await an answer, sends it
within a second: to the \fBalso\-notify\fR addresses (the zone's
\fBALSO\-NOTIFY\fR metadata, the view's, options') and, unless \fBnotify
explicit\fR, to the addresses of the NS hosts of the apex except the host named
in the SOA MNAME (\fBnotify\-to\-soa\fR), exactly as for a zone in memory, with
\fBnotify\-source\fR, the \fBserver\fR statement, the zone's \fBtsig\-key\fR,
\fBnotify\-rate\fR and the retransmissions of RFC 1996 3.6. The addresses of an
NS host come, in this order, from the glue of the apex (hosts inside the zone),
from a zone of the view that is in memory (its image), and from the \fBaddresses
the loader found in the database by name\fR (tag 22 of ZONE_BEGIN,
\fBkaridns\-dbloader(8)\fR; hosts outside the zone in any zone of the same
source, whether that zone is in memory or not). They are kept with the apex
block (only for a zone that sends NOTIFY to its NS hosts; at most 8 for a host,
1152 bytes; a zone with \fBnotify no\fR, \fBnotify explicit\fR, or none of them
pays nothing) and freed with the garbage of the retired snapshot. A server
start, a restart of the loader and a reload that changes nothing send nothing: a
zone that is new to the directory, or whose serial did not change, is not
"changed" (the first load of a zone in memory sends none either). A zone \fBin
memory\fR whose NS host is in another zone of the view that is out of memory (a
provider's zone) finds the address, besides in the image of a sibling that is in
memory, in the glue of the apex of that zone when the host is one of its own NS
hosts inside it (\fBns1.provider.test\fR of \fBprovider.test\fR); a host that is
not an NS host of its own zone has no address for a zone in memory (the loader's
addresses come with the summary of the zone that has the NS record, not with its
body), so that zone gets no NOTIFY to that host, as before; \fBnotify\-delay\fR
does not apply to a zone that is out of memory (there is no per-zone state to
keep it in), and the observatory counts of \fBnotify_sent\fR are not kept for it
(the log line \fB[Notify] zone '...' (data out of memory): NOTIFY to N
address(es)\fR is).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBA CNAME or the glue of a name server that leads into a zone that is out of
memory.\fR The answer is what it is without that zone, and the zone is asked for
in the background so that the next query finds it. A CNAME (or DNAME) of a zone
in memory whose target is in another zone of the view that is in the directory
without its data is answered with the CNAME alone: the chain stops there, as it
does at a name this server is not authoritative for (RFC 1034 4.3.2: the
resolver follows the CNAME itself with a query of its own, which then finds the
zone, parked and read as any query). The glue of an NS, MX or SRV target that a
sibling zone out of memory holds is left out of Additional (RFC 9471 3.2: glue
from a sibling is a SHOULD, and data that is not at hand is a good reason to
leave it out; RFC 2181 9). Neither answer is stored in the response cache (it
would be served after the zone is back). The request goes through the fetch ring
like the others, with no waiting and no I/O in the query path: the Backend hands
(view, zone) to the ingest thread (one request for a zone in 5 seconds per
Backend; none while a fetch of the zone is on its way or the zone has no usable
version), as a request of priority \fIbackground\fR: it is subject to
\fBmemory\-limit\fR (a request over the hard limit is dropped), to
\fBfetch\-concurrency\fR (dropped when no place is free or queries wait for
one), to \fBfetch\-rate\fR (a whole zone asked for in the background takes a
token only from a bucket that is more than half full, so the queries come first;
counted in \fBrefused_by_rate\fR when it is dropped), and, once the data is
here, the cache manager admits the data as any zone that comes into memory
(admission, design 8.3: above the high-water mark a zone that is asked for less
often than the zones taken out last is marked to be the first to go), so a
client that makes up names leading into zones nobody reads cannot push the zones
that are read out of memory. A name that is in none of the zones of the view
asks for nothing. A zone that only such a background fetch brought into memory
is marked to be the first to go when \fBmemory\-limit\fR needs room (above the
high-water mark), so many cold name servers behind the glue of one zone do not
push the zones the queries use out of memory; unlike a zone that only a transfer
brought, which is taken out when the transfer is over, it stays while there is
room, so that the next query finds it (the mark is dropped when a query is
answered from the zone; \fBmarked=\fR of the \fBtransfers of zones out of
memory\fR line counts both). A request that finds no free slot of the transfer
threads is not remembered as made: the next query makes it again.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNot yet.\fR A NOTIFY \fIfor\fR a zone that is out of memory (a primary zone
accepts none) is REFUSED , an UPDATE for it is checked (SOA question, apex,
\fBallow\-update\fR from the settings kept in the element); one that may be made
waits for the zone like a query (UDP and TCP; the zone is read, the UPDATE is
applied to it when it is there, and it does not count for the zone's frequency),
and gets SERVFAIL + EDE 14 only where nothing can wait (TLS, DoH, a full park
table, the time is up). A zone changed by a dynamic UPDATE is \fBpinned\fR: it
is never taken out of memory until the next load from the database replaces it
(the change would be lost, since UPDATEs are kept in memory only); \fBkarictl
dbstatus\fR shows how many zones are pinned that way (\fBupdated: N zone(s)\fR)
and \fBkarictl dbstatus <zone>\fR marks the zone. These come with the next steps
of D1 (design 7). The settings of a zone out of memory are the ones it had when
it was taken out (an edit of its metadata reaches it when the zone is read or
sent again), and \fBallow\-query\fR is checked from them. A zone of the database
that another view shares by \fBin\-view\fR is looked up in the directory of its
own view (see "Zones shared between views"), so the cache manager may take it
out of memory like any other.
.SS Settings of database zones (milestone B7a)
A zone in \fBkaridns.conf\fR takes its per-zone options from its \fBzone { }\fR
statement; a database zone takes them from its PowerDNS \fBdomainmetadata\fR
and, optionally, from a \fBprofile\fR. The loader turns the metadata into a
\fIsettings text\fR in \fBzone { }\fR syntax and sends it with the zone; the
Backend parses it with the configuration parser in a restricted mode and applies
it. The text is treated as hostile: only the statements listed below are
accepted, and anything else (\fBfile\fR, \fBtype\fR, \fBprogram*\fR,
\fBforwarders\fR, \fBrate\-limit\fR, \fBecs\-tags\fR, \fBinclude\fR, a named
\fBacl\fR of \fBkaridns.conf\fR, an unknown word, unbalanced braces, an ACL
entry that is not an address) rejects that zone alone: it keeps its last good
version \fBand its last good settings\fR (or is absent when it has none), and
\fBkarictl dbstatus <zone>\fR shows the reason. A TSIG key that the settings
name must be defined in \fBkaridns.conf\fR or the zone is rejected the same way.
.TP 8
\fBALLOW\-AXFR\-FROM\fR (one address or prefix per row)
\fISettings statement:\fR \fBallow\-transfer { <addr>; };\fR
.br
\fINotes:\fR \fBAUTO\-NS\fR, a name or anything else that is not an address or
prefix is left out with a warning of the loader (the list never becomes wider).
.TP 8
\fBTSIG\-ALLOW\-AXFR\fR (one key name per row)
\fISettings statement:\fR \fBallow\-transfer { key "<name>"; };\fR
.br
\fINotes:\fR Both kinds: \fBallow\-transfer { ...; key "..."; };
transfer\-acl\-match any;\fR
.TP 8
\fBALLOW\-DNSUPDATE\-FROM\fR, \fBTSIG\-ALLOW\-DNSUPDATE\fR
\fISettings statement:\fR \fBallow\-update { ... };\fR
.br
\fINotes:\fR Both kinds: \fBupdate\-acl\-match all;\fR is added. A row that is
not an address or prefix (or not a valid key name) is left out with a warning; a
kind whose rows are all left out makes it \fBallow\-update { none; };\fR (with a
warning), so that the other kind alone does not allow more than was written.
.TP 8
\fBALSO\-NOTIFY\fR
\fISettings statement:\fR \fBalso\-notify { ... };\fR
.br
\fINotes:\fR \fBip\fR, \fBip:port\fR, \fB[ip]:port\fR. Addresses only (no list
name, no key); without it the zone uses the \fBoptions\fR / view
\fBalso\-notify\fR.
.TP 8
\fBAXFR\-MASTER\-TSIG\fR
\fISettings statement:\fR \fBtsig\-key "<name>";\fR
.br
\fINotes:\fR The key of the primary of a secondary zone (\fBSLAVE\fR /
\fBCONSUMER\fR, milestone B7b). It is \fBnot\fR a key that may transfer the
zone.
.TP 8
\fBdomains.master\fR (a column, not metadata)
\fISettings statement:\fR \fBmasters { <addr> [port <n>]; ... };\fR
.br
\fINotes:\fR Only for \fBSLAVE\fR and \fBCONSUMER\fR zones: see "Database zone
types". \fBmasters\fR and \fBprimaries\fR are the same statement.
.TP 8
\fBX\-KARIDNS\-<OPTION>\fR
\fISettings statement:\fR \fB<option> <value>;\fR
.br
\fINotes:\fR \fBNOTIFY\-RETRIES\fR, \fBNOTIFY\-RETRY\-INTERVAL\fR,
\fBNOTIFY\-RETRY\-BACKOFF\fR, \fBNOTIFY\-TCP\-TIMEOUT\fR, \fBIXFR\-HISTORY\fR,
\fBADDITIONAL\-FROM\-AUTH\fR, \fBDISABLE\-AUTO\-TC\-FLAG\fR,
\fBZONE\-UDP\-BUFSIZE\fR, \fBZONE\-TCP\-MSS\fR, \fBZONE\-TCP\-WINDOW\fR,
\fBZONE\-TCP\-SNDBUF\fR, \fBCACHE\fR (\fBpin\fR, \fBno\-cache\fR or \fBauto\fR:
see "Memory limit"), with the ranges of the zone options. An invalid value or an
unknown option is dropped with a warning.
.TP 8
\fBX\-KARIDNS\-PROFILE\fR
\fISettings statement:\fR the profile of the zone
.br
\fINotes:\fR See below.
.TP 8
\fBX\-KARIDNS\-ECS\-TRUSTED\-RESOLVERS\fR
\fISettings statement:\fR \fBecs\-trusted\-resolvers { <addr>; ... };\fR
.br
\fINotes:\fR Addresses and prefixes, a leading \fB!\fR excludes; bounded by
\fBacl\-ceiling\fR when queries are answered: see "Location / ECS tags and
tinydns zones in the database".
.TP 8
(a profile text or the loader's own text, no metadata kind)
\fISettings statement:\fR \fBalias\-resolver { <addr> [port <n>]; ... };\fR
.br
\fINotes:\fR May be written in a profile (\fBkaridns_profiles\fR); the ALIAS
resolvers of the zone, see POWERDNS ALIAS AND LUA RECORDS. Not sent with
Extended AXFR.
.TP 8
\fBPRESIGNED\fR, \fBNSEC3PARAM\fR, \fBX\-KARIDNS\-FORMAT\fR
\fISettings statement:\fR (zone flags, not settings)
.br
\fINotes:\fR \fBX\-KARIDNS\-FORMAT tinydns\fR: B8.
.TP 8
\fBX\-KARIDNS\-SIGNER\fR
\fISettings statement:\fR (not a setting of the zone)
.br
\fINotes:\fR \fB1\fR: \fBkaridns\-signer\fR keeps the zone signed. The reader
ignores it; the loader reads it for \fBsigner\-wait\fR (an auto reload does not
read the zone because of a change in the database; see "The signer's zones").
.PP
Other kinds are ignored; those that change answers in PowerDNS (\fBSOA\-EDIT\fR,
\fBNSEC3NARROW\fR, ...) get a warning once per zone; \fBENABLE\-LUA\-RECORDS\fR
with the value 1 rejects the zone (LUA records are not run). A zone with
\fBALLOW\-AXFR\-FROM\fR rows but none of them usable gets \fBallow\-transfer {
none; }\fR.
.PP
\fBPresigned zones (\fBPRESIGNED=1\fB).\fR The NSEC / NSEC3 records PowerDNS
does not store are rebuilt from the rows (\fBdns_presigned.h\fR). Only an NS cut
hides the names below it from the chain, as in PowerDNS (names below a
delegation have \fBauth\fR 0 and no NSEC3 owner); a name below a \fBDNAME\fR is
\fBnot\fR hidden, so it needs an \fBRRSIG(NSEC3)\fR owner of its own like any
other name of the zone, or the zone fails to load with \fBno RRSIG(NSEC3) owner
for '<name>'\fR. Without an \fBRRSIG(NSEC3PARAM)\fR at the apex the
\fBNSEC3PARAM\fR is rebuilt with the SOA MINIMUM as its TTL (the TTL the signer
used is not known) and the loader warns about it.
.PP
\fBMatch modes: \fBtransfer\-acl\-match any|all;\fB and \fBupdate\-acl\-match
any|all;\fB\fR are zone options of \fBkaridns.conf\fR too. They only matter for
an ACL that has both addresses and keys. The settings of a database zone (and
the settings a secondary receives from its primary) are always evaluated in this
way and never in BIND's first-match order; for them "the default" below is what
applies when the text does not say. A zone of \fBkaridns.conf\fR that does not
write the options uses BIND's first-match order since the ACL batch of the BIND
compatibility work (see "Address match lists"). For \fBallow\-transfer\fR:
\fBall\fR (the default for these settings, as in earlier versions) needs a
matching address \fBand\fR a listed key; \fBany\fR accepts a matching address
\fBor\fR a request signed with a listed key. For \fBallow\-update\fR: \fBany\fR
(the default) accepts a matching address \fBor\fR a request signed with a listed
key; \fBall\fR needs every kind of entry that is written to match (addresses
only: the address; keys only: the key; both: the address \fBand\fR the key). The
loader writes \fBtransfer\-acl\-match any;\fR and \fBupdate\-acl\-match all;\fR,
which is the PowerDNS meaning (AXFR is allowed by \fBALLOW\-AXFR\-FROM\fR or by
a \fBTSIG\-ALLOW\-AXFR\fR signature; an UPDATE needs
\fBALLOW\-DNSUPDATE\-FROM\fR and a \fBTSIG\-ALLOW\-DNSUPDATE\fR
signature). PowerDNS also adds its server-wide \fBallow\-axfr\-ips\fR and
\fBallow\-dnsupdate\-from\fR (default \fB127.0.0.0/8, ::1\fR) to these lists;
KariDNS applies exactly the lists in the database. \fBkaricheck conf\fR warns
when an option has nothing to choose between.
.PP
\fB\fBacl\-ceiling\fB.\fR A database cannot widen access beyond what
\fBkaridns.conf\fR allows. The ceiling is a list of addresses (a prefix,
\fBany\fR, \fB!\fR exclusions; the first matching entry decides, as in every
address list) and \fBkey "<name>"\fR entries. A client is \fBinside the
ceiling\fR when its source address matches the address entries or its request
was verified (RFC 8945 \[u00A7]5.2) with a key the ceiling names. A request to a
database zone (AXFR/IXFR or dynamic update) is allowed only if the zone's ACL
(with its match mode) allows it \fBand\fR the client is inside the ceiling; so a
database ACL \fBany;\fR means "every client the ceiling lets in", and an address
the database lists but the ceiling does not cover is usable only with a request
signed by a ceiling key. With \fBno ceiling configured, every database ACL
refuses everybody\fR (logged once; \fBkaricheck conf\fR warns). File zones are
not subject to the ceiling. \fB\fBacl\-ceiling\-match all;\fB\fR (milestone B7b)
changes "inside the ceiling" for a ceiling that lists both addresses and keys:
the request must come from a listed address \fBand\fR be signed with a listed
key. That is something BIND's ACLs cannot express (a key in an address match
list is an alternative to the addresses, or a second ACL ANDs nothing), and it
is the strict form for a server whose database may be edited by many people:
only the transfer agents (a known address) that also hold the key get
in. \fBany\fR (the default) is the form described above. The ceiling and the
keys are read from the active configuration for every request, so \fBkarictl
reconfig\fR takes effect at once, without reading any data again. Entries that
can never match because of the ceiling are warned about when the settings are
first installed and listed by \fBkarictl dbstatus <zone>\fR (\fBallow\-transfer
198.51.100.0/24 lies outside acl\-ceiling\fR). When the settings are written for
a secondary (\fBzone_settings_render\fR), an address entry counts as inside the
ceiling only if \fBone non-negated ceiling entry covers the whole prefix\fR and
no negated ceiling entry touches it; a prefix that several narrower entries
cover together, or that has a hole made by a \fB!\fR entry, is left out (it used
to be judged by its first and last address only). A \fB!{ any; }\fR inside a
negated list in an ACL cannot be written as a flat list: the settings text of a
database zone with it is refused, and the list sent to a secondary is
\fBnone\fR. A TSIG key that a database ACL names but \fBkaridns.conf\fR no
longer defines (after a reload) closes that ACL: transfers (or updates) of the
zone are refused for everybody until the key is back; the log, \fBkarictl
dbstatus <zone>\fR (\fBsettings error\fR) and the summary line say so.
.PP
\fBProfiles.\fR \fBkaridns_profiles (name, settings)\fR holds named settings
texts in the same statements; a zone names its profile with \fBdomainmetadata\fR
\fBX\-KARIDNS\-PROFILE\fR. The profile is the base and the zone's own metadata
overrides it \fBstatement by statement\fR: a statement the zone's metadata
produce (\fBallow\-transfer\fR, \fBalso\-notify\fR, an option, ...) replaces the
profile's statement of the same name completely, including its keys. The merge
is done in the Backend: the loader sends the profile text in a \fBPROFILE\fR
message before the first zone of the batch that uses it and the zone's own text
in \fBZONE_BEGIN\fR, and the Backend parses the profile text, the zone text and
the two together (profile first), so a profile cannot swallow the zone's
statements and a hostile loader cannot get a statement past the whitelist by the
way it splits them. Settings with the same profile name, profile text and zone
text are shared by all zones that have them (one parsed block; \fBkarictl
dbstatus\fR counts the distinct ones). An unknown profile fails the zone in the
loader (\fBthe profile 'x' is not defined in karidns_profiles\fR); a profile
text the Backend refuses rejects every zone that uses it. A profile text is
limited to printable ASCII, TAB and LF and 65,535 bytes; the Backend keeps at
most 16 MiB of profile text per batch.
.PP
\fBEdits and reload.\fR The digest of a zone covers the data \fBand\fR the
settings text, the profile name and the profile text, so an edit of metadata or
of a profile that keeps the serial is applied by \fBkarictl reload <zone>\fR and
by \fBkarictl reload\fR, and \fBkarictl reload <zone>\fR of an unchanged zone
says \fBunchanged\fR. The triggers of \fBdb/karidns_ext.*.sql\fR log every
domain whose \fBX\-KARIDNS\-PROFILE\fR names an inserted, edited, renamed or
deleted profile, so \fBkarictl reload\fR finds the zones of an edited profile
through the change log. \fBkarictl reload\fR and \fBkarictl reconfig\fR re-check
the settings of all database zones against the new configuration (the log says
how many name a key that is not defined).
.SS Database zone types (milestone B7b)
\fBdomains.type\fR decides what the loader makes of a row (PowerDNS 5.1 names,
case-insensitive):
.TP 8
\fBNATIVE\fR, \fBMASTER\fR
a primary zone; its data comes from \fBrecords\fR
.TP 8
\fBSLAVE\fR
a \fBsecondary\fR zone: the masters come from \fBdomains.master\fR, the TSIG key
from the \fBAXFR\-MASTER\-TSIG\fR metadata (it must be defined in
\fBkaridns.conf\fR, or the zone is rejected like any zone that names an
undefined key). The data comes by AXFR/IXFR, never from \fBrecords\fR
.TP 8
\fBCONSUMER\fR
a \fBcatalog consumer\fR: like a \fBzone { type slave; catalog\-zone yes; }\fR
of \fBkaridns.conf\fR (RFC 9432); the member zones the catalog lists are
created, transferred from the same masters and removed again like those of a
configured catalog zone. Masters and key as for \fBSLAVE\fR
.TP 8
\fBPRODUCER\fR
the catalog zone itself is not supported yet (a later 0.6.x release): it is
reported (\fBZONE_FAILED\fR, \fBkarictl dbstatus <zone>\fR) and not served, so
secondaries that take the catalog from KariDNS do not get it. Its member zones
(\fBdomains.catalog\fR) are ordinary zones and are served, so a PowerDNS
database that has catalog zones can be migrated; \fBkaricheck db\fR reports the
\fBPRODUCER\fR zone as a warning, not an error
.TP 8
anything else
the zone is skipped and reported
.PP
\fBSOA serial 0.\fR A zone whose apex SOA serial is 0 is loaded and served with
serial 0, and the loader logs a warning once for each version of the zone it
sends (\fBzone 'x.test.' (domains.id N): the SOA serial is 0; ...\fR), not on
every reload of an unchanged zone. PowerDNS may compute the served serial
(\fBSOA\-EDIT\fR, auto-serial); KariDNS uses the stored number, so secondaries
and NOTIFY see serial 0 until the zone gets a higher serial. \fBkaricheck db\fR
gives the same finding as a warning.
.PP
\fBMasters.\fR \fBdomains.master\fR is a list of addresses separated by commas
(blanks are tolerated), each \fBip\fR, \fBip:port\fR, \fB[ipv6]:port\fR or a
bare IPv6 address (PowerDNS syntax). A host name, a bad port, an empty or NULL
column fails that zone in the loader (\fBdomains.master entry 'x' is not an IP
address[:port] (host names are not supported)\fR); at most 16 entries are
used. The loader writes them as \fBmasters { 192.0.2.1 port 5353; 2001:db8::1
port 53; };\fR in the settings text of the zone (an entry without a port is
written with \fBport 53\fR, which is what PowerDNS uses; a \fBmasters\fR entry
typed by hand into a settings text or \fBkaridns.conf\fR without a port takes
\fBoptions { port }\fR instead, as in BIND), next to \fBtsig\-key\fR and the
usual ACLs (\fBALLOW\-AXFR\-FROM\fR and so on: a database secondary can itself
be transferred, bounded by \fBacl\-ceiling\fR), and sends \fBZONE_BEGIN\fR with
the zone kind secondary (or catalog) and \fBno data\fR (\fBrr_count 0\fR, no
\fBZONE_DATA\fR). \fBmasters\fR and \fBprimaries\fR are the same statement; in a
settings text they accept IP addresses only (at most 32).
.PP
\fBHow the Backend runs it.\fR The Backend keeps an ordinary zone entry for the
zone and the secondary machinery of the control thread does the rest, exactly as
for a \fBzone { type slave; }\fR: the first transfer at the first tick (about a
second after the zone arrived), then the SOA refresh / retry / expire timers (an
expired zone answers SERVFAIL, RFC 1034 \[u00A7]4.3.5), IXFR with a fallback to
AXFR (RFC 1995), NOTIFY accepted from the listed masters and, when
\fBtsig\-key\fR is set, only when signed with that key (RFC 1996, RFC 8945
\[u00A7]5.2), \fBalso\-notify\fR towards downstream secondaries after every
transfer. As for configured secondaries, the masters are asked in order, each at
every refresh until one is ahead (see Transfer and NOTIFY options), and the
options of the view and of \fBoptions\fR (timers,
\fBtransfer\-source\fR, ...) apply; a master's \fBsource\fR cannot be written in
the settings text.
.PP
\fBData lives in memory only.\fR PowerDNS writes what a slave transfers into the
database; KariDNS does not. After a restart of \fBkaridns\fR a \fBSLAVE\fR zone
is empty until its first transfer, and the rows of \fBrecords\fR of a
\fBSLAVE\fR zone (a copy PowerDNS made) are ignored. A restart of the
\fBloader\fR costs nothing: the loader compares the settings of every zone with
what the Backend has (the digest of a secondary covers its settings text,
profile and kind, not its data) and sends nothing for an unchanged zone, so no
zone is transferred again.
.PP
\fBChanges.\fR A change of \fBdomains.master\fR, of \fBAXFR\-MASTER\-TSIG\fR, of
the ACL metadata or of the profile is applied by the next reload (\fBkarictl
reload\fR, \fBkarictl reload <zone>\fR, the change log): the settings of the
zone are replaced, the zone keeps its data, and a refresh from the new masters
is started at once (an IXFR from the serial it holds; use \fBkarictl retransfer
<zone>\fR for a full transfer when the new master has an older serial, which a
secondary refuses to follow). A zone whose type changes between \fBNATIVE\fR and
\fBSLAVE\fR, or between \fBSLAVE\fR and \fBCONSUMER\fR, gets a new entry (the
data of the old one goes). A deleted row removes the zone with the next reload,
and a deleted \fBCONSUMER\fR takes the member zones it created with
it. \fBkarictl retransfer <zone>\fR works for \fBSLAVE\fR and \fBCONSUMER\fR
zones; \fBkarictl reload <zone>\fR re-reads the settings (it does not start a
transfer), \fBkarictl notify <zone>\fR sends NOTIFY to the zone's
\fBalso\-notify\fR addresses; \fBkarictl dbstatus <zone>\fR shows the masters,
the key, the last successful transfer and its serial.
.PP
\fBPrecedence (D17-1).\fR A catalog member of the same name hides a database
zone of that name on every path, a \fBSLAVE\fR or \fBCONSUMER\fR included, and
the database zone is served again when the member goes away (also when the whole
catalog consumer is deleted). A \fBzone { }\fR of \fBkaridns.conf\fR of the same
name hides the database zone as before; a hidden \fBSLAVE\fR makes no transfers.
.SS Location / ECS tags and tinydns zones in the database (milestone B8)
A zone file with \fB$LOCATION\fR / \fB$ECS\-SUBNET\fR tags or a tinydns data
file is not a thing a PowerDNS database can hold, so KariDNS keeps the
\fBparsed\fR result in its own tables (\fBdb/karidns_ext.*.sql\fR, created
together with the change-log triggers; PowerDNS ignores them, so on a database
that PowerDNS also serves the tags and the tinydns time stamps and locations
have no effect there):
.TP 8
\fBkaridns_record_tags (record_id, location_tag, ecs_tag)\fR
the \fB$LOCATION\fR and \fB$ECS\-SUBNET\fR tag in force for that row of
\fBrecords\fR (NULL = none). A tag name is 1-63 printable characters without
blanks; \fBnone\fR and \fBdefault\fR are reserved (they clear a tag in a zone
file). Tags compare without regard to case. A row with a tag is answered to the
clients that match the tag, in addition to the untagged rows.
.TP 8
\fBkaridns_zone_tagdefs (domain_id, kind, tag, cidr)\fR
one row per CIDR: \fBkind\fR \fBlocation\fR (\fB$LOCATION\-TAG\fR), \fBecs\fR
(\fB$ECS\-SUBNET\-TAG\fR) or \fBtinydns\fR (a \fB%\fR line: \fBtag\fR is the 1-2
character location code, \fBcidr\fR an IPv4 prefix). \fBThe order of the rows of
a zone is the order of the definitions\fR (the first definition that matches a
client wins; consecutive rows of one tag are one definition): insert them in
file order. SQLite3 reads them in \fBrowid\fR order.
.TP 8
\fBkaridns_record_tinydns (record_id, ttd, loc, countdown)\fR
the tinydns time stamp (unix time of the TAI64 label; NULL = none), the 1-2
character location code of the line, and \fBcountdown\fR (a record with TTL 0
and a time stamp: its TTL counts down to the time stamp, 2 to 3600 s). A time
stamp on a record with a TTL is the time from which the record is served.
.TP 8
\fBdomainmetadata\fR \fBX\-KARIDNS\-FORMAT\fR = \fBtinydns\fR
the zone is a tinydns zone (time-dependent TTLs, answers never cached), also
when no row has a time stamp or a location.
.TP 8
\fBdomainmetadata\fR \fBX\-KARIDNS\-ECS\-TRUSTED\-RESOLVERS\fR
the zone option \fBecs\-trusted\-resolvers\fR: addresses and prefixes, separated
by commas or blanks, one or several rows, a leading \fB!\fR excludes (first
match decides). An entry that is not an address or prefix fails the zone.
.PP
\fBHow it travels.\fR The loader sends these tables in the zone's
\fBZONE_DATA\fR as Extended AXFR records, written by the same encoder as
Extended AXFR out (\fBxext_*\fR in \fBdns_wire.c\fR); \fBZONE_BEGIN\fR flag bit1
(extended) says the records are there, bit2 (tinydns) is the tinydns flag. The
Backend reads them with the transfer parser a secondary uses and accepts them
only in a zone that announced them; a malformed definition, state or wrapper
record, a tag longer than 63 octets, more than 4096 tinydns locations or an
\fBECS_TRUSTED\fR record rejects the zone. The digest of a zone covers the
attributes and the tinydns flag, so an edit of a tag or a definition is applied
by \fBkarictl reload\fR. A database zone is transferred out as Extended AXFR to
a KariDNS secondary like a zone file (\fBallow\-transfer\fR as usual): the
secondary answers every client like the primary.
.PP
\fB\fBacl\-ceiling\fB and the trusted resolvers.\fR The list of
\fBX\-KARIDNS\-ECS\-TRUSTED\-RESOLVERS\fR is bounded by the ceiling \fBwhen a
query is answered\fR: a resolver is trusted only if the zone's list and the
\fBaddress entries\fR of \fBacl\-ceiling\fR both accept its address (keys of the
ceiling do not matter: ECS trust has no key; with no ceiling configured nobody
is trusted; a zone without the metadata uses the \fBecs\-trusted\-resolvers\fR
of \fBoptions\fR, which is not bounded). \fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR / the Backend
log the entries that lie outside the ceiling once per distinct settings text. A
server that is a secondary of a database zone gets the list in the Extended AXFR
(it is the primary's data) and applies its own configuration to it. \fBA list
that is part of the zone's data is not bounded by the ceiling:\fR an
\fBecs\-trusted\-resolvers\fR list carried in the zone image itself (a zone
file's own definition, or what a primary sends in a zone transfer) is used as it
is and is returned before the \fBacl\-ceiling\fR check; the ceiling bounds only
the lists that come from the settings text of a database zone
(\fBX\-KARIDNS\-ECS\-TRUSTED\-RESOLVERS\fR). A database operator who must not be
able to widen the trusted resolvers therefore must not have a way to put such a
list into the zone data (for instance through a transfer from a primary that the
operator controls).
.PP
\fBWhat does not carry over.\fR (1) The SOA serial that a tinydns \fB.\fR line
takes from the data file's modification time: the database stores the number the
importer saw; whoever edits the zone bumps it (a \fBZ\fR line carries an
explicit serial). (2) The data file as text: comments, line grouping, one line
that writes into two zones (\fB=\fR makes an A and a PTR: two independent rows
in two zones), a data file with many zones (the importer writes one zone per
SOA). (3) Several SOA rows for the apex, and tags or tinydns attributes (time
stamp, location) on a SOA row (milestone B8t-1): \fBkaridns\-dbloader\fR reads
them like any other row (the SOA rows must have one serial; the SOA without
attributes, else the first row, is the reference SOA and gives the zone serial;
a presigned zone cannot have them) and the rules of "Several SOA records" below
apply to a zone file. Milestone B8t-2: the stream between the loader and the
Backend is the Extended AXFR version 3 format, which carries them (the frame is
the reference SOA, \fBSOA_SET\fR announces the number of SOA rows and each SOA
row is a body RR in a wrapper record, with its tinydns attributes and tags). The
Backend checks what the loader sent: every SOA at the apex, one serial (the one
\fBZONE_BEGIN\fR declares), no two SOA rows for the same clients, the opening
SOA is the reference SOA, the number announced; a zone that fails a check is
rejected and the last good version keeps serving. At most 4096 SOA rows. (4)
\fBkaridns_*\fR rows of a record that is disabled or does not exist are ignored
with a warning.
.PP
\fBImporter.\fR \fBkaridns\-zone(8)\fR writes all of this: \fBkaridns\-zone
import <zone> <file>\fR for a zone file with tags, \fBkaridns\-zone import
\-\-all data\fR for a tinydns data file (apply \fBdb/karidns_ext.*.sql\fR
first), and \fBkaridns\-zone export\fR writes a zone, or every zone, back as a
zone file. (\fBtests/db/zone2sql\fR, which prints the same rows as SQL, is the
test tool.)
.SS Address match lists (ACLs)
\fBallow\-query\fR, \fBallow\-query\-on\fR, \fBallow\-transfer\fR,
\fBallow\-update\fR and \fBallow\-notify\fR (in \fBoptions\fR, \fBview\fR and
\fBzone\fR), \fBmatch\-clients\fR, \fBecs\-trusted\-resolvers\fR and \fBacl\fR
take an address match list, as in BIND 9.20 (ARM "Address Match Lists"):
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
address_match_list = address_match_element; ...
address_match_element = [ ! ] ( <ip_address> | <ip_prefix> | key <key_id> | <acl_name> | { address_match_list } )
.fi
.RE
.PP
An element is an IPv4/IPv6 address, a prefix, \fBany\fR, \fBnone\fR,
\fBlocalhost\fR, \fBlocalnets\fR, \fBkey "<name>"\fR, the name of an \fBacl\fR
block or a nested \fB{ ... }\fR list; a leading \fB!\fR negates it. \fBThe
elements are tried in order and the first one that matches decides\fR: a
non-negated match allows, a negated match denies, and a client that matches
nothing is denied (ARM: "If there is no match, access is denied"; "Order of
insertion is significant"). \fBnone\fR matches nothing. A \fBkey\fR element
matches a request that is signed with a TSIG key of that name and whose
signature verified (RFC 8945 \[u00A7]5.2), whatever the client's address; key
names are compared as domain names (case and a trailing dot do not matter), and
a key must be defined in a \fBkey { }\fR block. A nested list matches when
\fIits own\fR first match is a non-negated one; a negative match inside it
counts as "no match" for the element that holds it (BIND's \fBlib/dns/acl.c\fR
does the same), which is what makes \fB!{ !192.0.2.1; any; }; key "k1";\fR
BIND's idiom for "from 192.0.2.1 \fBand\fR signed with k1". In
\fBallow\-update\fR the bare name of a defined key is accepted as well
(\fBallow\-update { k1; };\fR is \fBkey "k1"\fR).
.PP
\fBacl "<name>" { ... };\fR defines a named list; it may be written before or
after its first use, may contain \fBkey\fR, other \fBacl\fR names, nested lists
and \fB!\fR, and is evaluated like a nested list at the place where its name
stands (\fB!name\fR denies a client the list allows; a client the list excludes
with \fB!\fR is "no match" and the next element decides). An \fBacl\fR that
contains itself, directly or through others, and lists nested more than 8 deep
are configuration errors, as is any entry that is not an address, a prefix, a
built-in name, a defined \fBacl\fR (or key) -- \fBkaricheck conf\fR reports them
with \fBfile:line\fR. \fBecs\-trusted\-resolvers\fR looks at the address only:
\fBkey\fR is an error there, and it reads the list as flat entries (a
\fB!{ ... }\fR block negates each of its entries). \fBmatch\-clients\fR without
a \fBkey\fR element is read the same way (flat, by address); with one it is
evaluated like \fBallow\-query\fR, with the TSIG key of the request (see
"Choosing a view by TSIG key" in the \fBview\fR section).
.PP
\fBWhere the statements apply.\fR For each of the five statements the list that
applies to a zone is the zone's own, else its view's, else the one in
\fBoptions\fR (BIND 9.20 ARM: the zone statement "overrides" the options
statement); the lists are never merged, and \fB{ }\fR written at a lower level
matches nobody. Defaults: \fBallow\-query\fR and \fBallow\-query\-on\fR allow
everybody (so a configuration that has no \fBallow\-query\fR anywhere is exactly
as before); \fBallow\-transfer\fR and \fBallow\-update\fR allow nobody ("If
allow-transfer is not specified, then the default is none"); \fBallow\-notify\fR
adds nobody to the zone's primaries and applies to secondary zones only.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBallow\-query\fB\fR is checked for every QUERY (opcode 0) for a name in a
zone, \fBbefore\fR anything is looked up and \fBbefore\fR the shared response
cache: a client that is not allowed gets REFUSED (AA=0) with EDE 18 (Prohibited,
RFC 8914 \[u00A7]4.19) and a question section, nothing of the zone (not even
that the name does not exist, or that the zone has expired). It applies on UDP
and TCP alike, to zones of every type (\fBmaster\fR, \fBslave\fR, \fBforward\fR,
\fBprogram\fR, database zones, catalog members) and to a UDP IXFR/SOA-only
answer. The cached answers do not depend on the client; because the check comes
first, an answer cached for a client that may ask is never given to one that may
not (tested with the cache warm). A CNAME that leads into another zone of the
view is followed only if the client may ask \fIthat\fR zone as well; otherwise
the answer ends with the CNAME that has been collected (NOERROR) and is not
cached, and glue taken from another zone is left out for such a client. A
request signed with a key is checked with that key (\fBkey\fR elements); a
request whose TSIG fails is answered NOTAUTH before this check, as before. Zone
transfers (AXFR/IXFR over TCP) are governed by \fBallow\-transfer\fR only and
NOTIFY by the primaries and \fBallow\-notify\fR; \fBallow\-query\fR does not
apply to them. \fBUPDATE\fR (RFC 2136) to a primary zone is checked against
\fBallow\-query\fR (and \fBallow\-query\-on\fR) first, as BIND does
(\fBlib/ns/update.c\fR, \fBcheckqueryacl()\fR): a client that may not query the
zone may not update it either, whatever \fBallow\-update\fR or
\fBupdate\-policy\fR says -- REFUSED with EDE 18, logged in category
\fBupdate\-security\fR as \fBupdate 'zone/IN' denied due to allow\-query\fR
(\fBerror\fR when the zone has an update ACL or an \fBupdate\-policy\fR,
\fBinfo\fR without), on UDP and TCP and for a database zone that is out of
memory (nothing is read for it). A client that passes \fBallow\-query\fR is then
checked against \fBallow\-update\fR / \fBupdate\-policy\fR as before. An UPDATE
to a secondary zone is refused without this check (it would be forwarded in
BIND).
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBallow\-query\-on\fB\fR is checked against the address the query came to,
with the same effect. On TCP it is the local address of the connection
(\fBgetsockname\fR), on UDP the destination address of the datagram (the
Frontend reads \fBIP_RECVDSTADDR\fR / \fBIPV6_PKTINFO\fR from every UDP socket,
wildcard or not, and passes it to the Backend with the packet). If the address
is unknown the only element that can match is \fBany\fR. The answer is refused
when either \fBallow\-query\fR or \fBallow\-query\-on\fR does not allow the
query.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBallow\-transfer\fB\fR may be written in \fBoptions\fR and \fBview\fR;
\fBkey\fR elements, \fB!\fR, nested lists and named lists work as above. See
"Order of evaluation and \fBtransfer\-acl\-match\fR" below for what changed for
lists that mix addresses and keys.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBallow\-update\fB\fR may be written in \fBoptions\fR and \fBview\fR. A zone
that is not a primary refuses updates whatever the lists say.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDatabase zones\fR take \fBallow\-query\fR and \fBallow\-query\-on\fR from
their view and \fBoptions\fR like any zone (their settings text cannot carry
them), and \fBallow\-notify\fR for a database secondary. Their
\fBallow\-transfer\fR / \fBallow\-update\fR come from the settings text only and
stay bounded by \fBacl\-ceiling\fR: the statements of \fBoptions\fR and
\fBview\fR are not a default for them (a database zone without an ACL refuses
transfers and updates, as before).
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fBallow\-notify\fB\fR: for a secondary zone a NOTIFY is accepted when it
comes from one of the zone's primaries (with the key rule of \fBprimaries\fR)
\fBor\fR the list allows the sender; a \fBkey\fR element is the TSIG key the
NOTIFY is signed with. A catalog member has no \fBzone { }\fR: its primaries are
those of its catalog zone (the primaries the transfer uses; the copy of the
first one kept in the member is the fallback when the catalog zone is not in the
view), with the key rule of a configured secondary (the key of the primaries
element, else the \fBtsig\-key\fR of the catalog zone), so a NOTIFY from the
primary of the catalog starts the member's refresh at once.
.PP
\fBOrder of evaluation and \fBtransfer\-acl\-match\fB /
\fBupdate\-acl\-match\fB.\fR Before this release KariDNS read
\fBallow\-transfer\fR as "an address in the list \fBand\fR a listed key" when it
had both kinds of entry, and \fBallow\-update\fR as "an address in the list
\fBor\fR a listed key", ignoring the order of the entries. Without
\fBtransfer\-acl\-match\fR / \fBupdate\-acl\-match\fR a configuration file now
uses BIND's evaluation for both:
.TP 8
\fBallow\-transfer { 192.0.2.1; key "k"; };\fR
\fIbefore:\fR address \fBand\fR key
.br
\fInow (no *-acl-match):\fR first match: the address \fBor\fR the key
(\fBallow\-transfer { key "k"; 192.0.2.1; };\fR also accepts an unsigned request
from 192.0.2.1) -- \fBmore permissive\fR
.br
\fIto keep the old meaning:\fR \fBtransfer\-acl\-match all;\fR in the zone (or
write \fB{ !{ !192.0.2.1; any; }; key "k"; };\fR)
.TP 8
\fBallow\-transfer { 192.0.2.1; };\fR with \fBtsig\-key "k"\fR in the zone
\fIbefore:\fR address \fBand\fR a request signed with the key
.br
\fInow (no *-acl-match):\fR unchanged: the list says who may ask, and a zone
that has a \fBtsig\-key\fR and addresses in its list still needs a request
signed with the \fBtsig\-key\fR (or a key of the list). With a list of keys
only, the \fBtsig\-key\fR is one more key that may transfer, as before
.TP 8
\fBallow\-update { !192.0.2.9; 192.0.2.0/24; key "k"; };\fR
\fIbefore:\fR the address list matched \fBor\fR the request was signed with a
listed key (\fB!\fR entries only ever removed addresses)
.br
\fInow (no *-acl-match):\fR first match: a request from 192.0.2.9 is refused
even when it is signed with k -- stricter
.br
\fIto keep the old meaning:\fR \fBupdate\-acl\-match any;\fR
.TP 8
addresses only, or keys only
\fIbefore:\fR unchanged
.br
\fInow (no *-acl-match):\fR unchanged
.PP
\fBkaricheck conf\fR warns about the first and the third row for every zone that
writes its own list (see karicheck(8)), so a configuration that has become more
permissive is found before it is loaded. Writing \fBtransfer\-acl\-match
any|all;\fR / \fBupdate\-acl\-match any|all;\fR keeps the meaning of the earlier
releases exactly (\fBany\fR/\fBall\fR as described in the zone table): the list
is then split into its address entries (evaluated in order, as above) and its
key entries, and combined as the option says; the options cannot be used with a
negated key (\fB!key "k"\fR), which is a configuration error there. The lists of
\fBdatabase zones\fR are not affected: the settings the database loader writes
(see "Settings of database zones") are evaluated as before, with
\fBtransfer\-acl\-match any\fR / \fBupdate\-acl\-match all\fR written by the
loader, \fBacl\-ceiling\fR as the bound, and no \fBacl\fR names,
\fBlocalhost\fR, \fBlocalnets\fR or nesting in them; the same holds for the
settings a secondary receives from its primary (they replace what \fBoptions\fR
or the view say, but not a statement written in the secondary's own \fBzone {
}\fR; the settings a primary sends contain only the zone's own
\fBallow\-transfer\fR, not the one of its view or of \fBoptions\fR).
.PP
\fB\fBlocalhost\fB and \fBlocalnets\fB\fR (BIND 9.20 ARM, \fBacl\fR):
\fBlocalhost\fR matches the IPv4 and IPv6 addresses of all network interfaces of
the host, \fBlocalnets\fR any host on an IPv4 or IPv6 network for which the host
has an interface (the address and netmask of the interface; for IPv6 the prefix
length the system reports). The Backend runs in capability mode and cannot ask
the kernel for its interfaces, so the server takes \fBone snapshot of the
interfaces at start-up\fR (\fBgetifaddrs(3)\fR, before the privileges are
dropped) and evaluates the two names against it. \fBA reload does not take a new
snapshot, and BIND's periodic re-scan (\fBinterface\-interval\fB) does not
exist: an address added or removed after start-up is noticed after a restart.\fR
Interfaces that are down are not part of the snapshot. In the flat lists that
\fBmatch\-clients\fR, \fBecs\-trusted\-resolvers\fR and the settings sent to
secondaries use, \fBlocalhost\fR/\fBlocalnets\fR stand for the
addresses/networks of the same snapshot.
.SS zone \(dq<name>\(dq { ... }
.TP 8
\fBtype <type>;\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR all
.br
\fIDescription:\fR \fBmaster\fR (alias \fBprimary\fR, the default), \fBslave\fR
(alias \fBsecondary\fR), \fBforward\fR, or \fBprogram\fR. Case-insensitive. The
BIND zone types of a resolving or caching server, \fBhint\fR, \fBstub\fR,
\fBstatic\-stub\fR, \fBmirror\fR, \fBredirect\fR (and \fBdelegation\-only\fR of
older BIND versions), are accepted so that a stock BIND \fBnamed.conf\fR can be
read: the zone is \fBnot loaded\fR, the rest of its block is skipped, and a
warning with the file and line says so (\fBkaricheck conf\fR shows
it). \fBforward\fR keeps its KariDNS meaning (this server relays the queries of
the zone; in BIND it is a resolver-side type, which \fBkaricheck conf\fR
notes). A zone with \fBin\-view\fR has no \fBtype\fR. Any other value is an
error.
.TP 8
\fBin\-view "<view>";\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR (no type)
.br
\fIDescription:\fR The zone is the zone of the same name in the view
\fB<view>\fR, which must be defined earlier (a \fBzone { }\fR of it, or a zone
of its database or catalog): one set of data, one NOTIFY, one IXFR history. Only
\fBforward\fR, \fBforwarders\fR and \fBin\-view\-settings\fR may be written with
it (the ACL statements with \fBin\-view\-settings view\fR). See Zones shared
between views.
.TP 8
\fBin\-view\-settings owner|view;\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR with \fBin\-view\fR
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Whose ACLs apply to the requests that come through this view.
.TP 8
\fBfile "<path>";\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR master, slave
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Zone file. For a secondary zone the file is optional: if it
exists it is loaded at startup as the initial data, and the zone is then
refreshed from the primary. Transferred data is kept in memory only and is never
written to the file, so after a restart a secondary serves the file's data (or
nothing) until its first transfer. Ignored (with a warning) for \fBforward\fR
and \fBprogram\fR zones. Records whose owner is not at or below the zone name
(out-of-zone data, including address records for name servers outside the zone)
are not loaded; each is logged as "ignoring out-of-zone data", as BIND does (RFC
1034 \[u00A7]4.2). A secondary likewise skips and logs out-of-zone records
received in a transfer instead of rejecting the transfer. It does reject a
transfer that has a record after the final SOA in the same message (RFC 5936
\[u00A7]2.2), one that ends without a SOA at the apex of the zone, and one with
records of the extension class (65302) when the primary did not answer the
Extended AXFR option. A record that the zone file parser accepts but whose RDATA
cannot be encoded for its type (for example a WKS port name that is not in the
built-in service table) is also left out with a warning ("its RDATA cannot be
encoded"); the rest of the zone is served and transferred. \fBkaricheck\fR
reports such a record as an error.
.TP 8
\fBfile\-format bind|tinydns;\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR master
.br
\fIDescription:\fR \fBbind\fR (default) or \fBtinydns\fR (djbdns \fBdata\fR
file). See TINYDNS ZONE FORMAT.
.TP 8
\fBprimaries [ port <n> ] { ( <list> | <addr> [port <n>] ) [key <name>]; ... };\fR (also \fBmasters\fR)
\fIApplies to:\fR slave, catalog
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Primary servers (BIND 9.20 ARM "primaries"; list names,
\fBport\fR, \fBkey\fR, errors: see \fBalso\-notify\fR and
\fBremote\-servers\fR). NOTIFY is accepted from any listed address, and when
that address has a \fBkey\fR (or else the zone has \fBtsig\-key\fR) only when
signed with it; the refresh and transfer ask the entries \fBin order\fR (a
failure, an up-to-date answer or a primary that is behind goes on to the next;
the first one that is ahead is transferred from): SOA, IXFR and AXFR requests to
an entry are signed with its \fBkey\fR, else with the zone's \fBtsig\-key\fR,
and the answers are verified with the same key (the \fBkey\fR of the primary
wins over \fBtsig\-key\fR). An address may also be a prefix (\fB10.0.0.0/8\fR,
as before: it only widens who may send NOTIFY; do not use it for the first
entry). A list name is replaced by the addresses of the list. \fBtls\fR is a
configuration error. \fBsource ( <ipv4> | * ) [ source\-v6 ... ]\fR before the
braces is the local address of the connections to the addresses written in that
statement (an address taken from a named list keeps that list's source). A later
\fBprimaries\fR/\fBmasters\fR statement replaces the earlier one. Port default:
\fBoptions { port }\fR (53 when not written; a catalog member uses the same
default for the primary of its catalog zone). Transfer requests carry the EDNS
EXPIRE option (RFC 7314); the returned value (capped by SOA EXPIRE) sets the
zone's expire timer, so a secondary that transfers from another secondary
expires no later than its source. The refresh sends IXFR (AXFR when the zone has
no data yet, or after \fBkarictl retransfer\fR). Every message of the answer
must carry the request's ID, QR=1 and OPCODE 0, and the first one the request's
question (RFC 5936 \[u00A7]2.2.1); otherwise the transfer is rejected and
logged. An answer with an error RCODE ends the transfer at once and is logged
with the RCODE (and the TSIG error, when the primary rejected the zone's
\fBtsig\-key\fR); a primary that answers IXFR with FORMERR or NOTIMP is asked
once more with AXFR (RFC 1995 \[u00A7]4).
.TP 8
\fBtsig\-key "<name>";\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR slave, master
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Key used to sign SOA/AXFR/IXFR requests to the primary. On a
primary it is also accepted for incoming transfers. Outgoing NOTIFY messages of
the zone are signed with it.
.TP 8
\fBallow\-query { <acl>; ... };\fR, \fBallow\-query\-on { <acl>; ... };\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR all
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Who may ask this zone ordinary questions, and on which local
addresses (BIND 9.20 ARM). Replaces the \fBview\fR and \fBoptions\fR statements;
REFUSED with EDE 18 otherwise. See Address match lists.
.TP 8
\fBallow\-notify { <acl>; ... };\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR slave
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Who may send NOTIFY for this zone in addition to its
\fBprimaries\fR. Replaces the \fBview\fR and \fBoptions\fR statements.
.TP 8
\fBallow\-transfer { <acl>; key "<name>"; ... };\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR master, slave
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Who may transfer the zone (AXFR/IXFR over TCP); replaces the
\fBview\fR and \fBoptions\fR statements (written \fB{ }\fR it allows
nobody). \fBWithout an \fBallow\-transfer\fB at any level and without
\fBtsig\-key\fB, transfers are refused.\fR The list is evaluated in BIND's order
(first match decides; a \fBkey\fR element matches a request signed with that
key; the zone's \fBtsig\-key\fR still has to sign the request when the list has
addresses); with \fBtransfer\-acl\-match\fR written it is the older "address and
key" / "address or key" combination: see Address match lists. At most 4
transfers per zone run at the same time. The first message of an answer carries
an OPT when the request had one (RFC 5936 \[u00A7]2.2.5). An IXFR request needs
the client's SOA in the Authority section (RFC 1995 \[u00A7]3), otherwise it
gets FORMERR; a client whose serial is the same as or newer than the zone's (RFC
1982 comparison) gets the current SOA only (RFC 1995 \[u00A7]2), also over
UDP. A transfer request for a zone that has no data yet (a secondary before its
first transfer) gets SERVFAIL with EDE 14 (Not Ready).
.TP 8
\fBalso\-notify [ port <n> ] { <addr> [port <n>] [key <name>]; <list>; ... };\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR master
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Additional servers that receive NOTIFY (RFC 1996) when the
zone changes; replaces the list of the view or \fBoptions\fR (grammar, lists,
keys: see \fBalso\-notify\fR and \fBremote\-servers\fR). NOTIFY is also sent to
the addresses of the apex NS hosts, except the SOA MNAME host (RFC 1996
\[u00A7]3.2). NOTIFY is sent after an UPDATE, after \fBkarictl notify\fR, after
a reload (\fBkarictl reload\fR, \fBreconfig\fR, SIGHUP) that changed the zone's
serial (not at the first load at startup), and by a secondary after each
transfer. Each NOTIFY goes over UDP (over TCP for a server with \fBtcp\-only
yes\fR, see NOTIFY over TCP) and is retransmitted until the server answers with
the same ID and QNAME from the address and port it was sent to (RFC 1996
\[u00A7]3.6); see \fBnotify\-retries\fR (by default 2 retransmissions 5 s apart,
then TCP). When the zone has \fBtsig\-key\fR, every NOTIFY is signed with that
key and only an answer whose TSIG verifies ends the exchange (RFC 8945
\[u00A7]5.4); an answer that fails verification is logged and ignored, and the
NOTIFY is made over TCP at once. A NOTIFY that gets no answer over UDP after the
last retransmission is made once over TCP.
.TP 8
\fBnotify yes|no|explicit|primary\-only|master\-only;\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR master, slave
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Per-zone override of the view and \fBoptions\fR value
(default \fByes\fR): \fBno\fR sends nothing, \fBexplicit\fR only to
\fBalso\-notify\fR, \fBprimary\-only\fR / \fBmaster\-only\fR only for primary
zones. See \fBalso\-notify\fR and \fBremote\-servers\fR.
.TP 8
\fBnotify\-retries <n>;\fR, \fBnotify\-retry\-interval <seconds>;\fR, \fBnotify\-retry\-backoff fixed|exponential;\fR, \fBnotify\-tcp\-timeout <seconds>;\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR master, slave
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Per-zone override of the \fBoptions\fR values below.
.TP 8
\fBixfr\-history <n>;\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR master, slave
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Per-zone override of the \fBoptions\fR value: how many past
versions are kept as IXFR differences, 0\[u2013]32 (default 4; see the options
table). \fB0\fR answers every IXFR request with the whole zone.
.TP 8
\fBmax\-zone\-records <n>;\fR, \fBmax\-zone\-bytes <size>;\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR secondary
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Per-zone override of the \fBoptions\fR values: the most RRs
and message octets of one inbound AXFR/IXFR (see the options table).
.TP 8
\fBtransfer\-acl\-match any|all;\fR, \fBupdate\-acl\-match any|all;\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR master, slave
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Which kinds of entry of \fBallow\-transfer\fR /
\fBallow\-update\fR have to match when the list has both addresses and keys:
\fBtransfer\-acl\-match all\fR = address \fBand\fR key, \fBany\fR = address
\fBor\fR key; \fBupdate\-acl\-match any\fR = address or key, \fBall\fR = address
and key. \fBNot written\fR (the default), a list in \fBkaridns.conf\fR is
evaluated in BIND's order: the first element that matches decides ("Address
match lists" above; before, an \fBallow\-transfer\fR with addresses and keys
meant \fBall\fR). The options are what the database loader writes for database
zones, whose settings are evaluated as before (see "Settings of database
zones"). \fBkaricheck conf\fR warns when a list has become more permissive.
.TP 8
\fBnotify\-source "<addr>";\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR master
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Source address of outgoing NOTIFY messages (either family;
BIND's \fBnotify\-source\fR / \fBnotify\-source\-v6\fR and the other transfer
and NOTIFY options -- \fBtransfer\-source\fR, \fBmin\-refresh\-time\fR,
\fBmax\-transfer\-time\-in\fR, \fBmulti\-master\fR, \fBrequest\-ixfr\fR, ... --
are also written in a \fBzone\fR, see Transfer and NOTIFY options).
.TP 8
\fBallow\-update { <acl>; key "<name>"; ... };\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR master
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Enables Dynamic Update (RFC 2136) for matching clients or
TSIG keys; replaces the \fBview\fR and \fBoptions\fR statements. The list is
evaluated in BIND's order (first match decides) unless \fBupdate\-acl\-match\fR
is written. Updates are kept in memory only. An update sent to a secondary zone
is answered with REFUSED (EDE 18) whether or not the client matches
\fBallow\-update\fR: KariDNS does not forward updates to the primary (RFC 2136
\[u00A7]6); a warning is printed at load time. Processing follows RFC 2136
\[u00A7]3: an update whose zone section does not name a zone of the client's
view gets NOTAUTH; update RRs that \[u00A7]3.4.2 says to ignore (a CNAME next to
other data or other data next to a CNAME, an SOA whose serial is not newer,
deletion of the apex SOA, of the apex NS RRset or of the last apex NS) are
ignored and the rest of the update is applied; an error anywhere leaves the zone
unchanged. An update that replaces the SOA with a newer serial keeps that
serial; any other change increments the serial by one. An update that changes
nothing (only prerequisites, or only ignored or already present records) answers
NOERROR without changing the serial or sending NOTIFY.
.TP 8
\fBcatalog\-zone yes;\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR master, slave
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Marks the zone as a catalog zone (RFC 9432, schema version 2:
exactly one \fBversion.<zone> TXT "2"\fR is required; a second version TXT
record makes the catalog zone broken and it is not processed, RFC 9432
\[u00A7]4.1, \[u00A7]5.1). Member zones listed under \fBzones.<zone>\fR are
served as secondary zones that transfer from the catalog zone's first
\fBmasters\fR entry.
.TP 8
\fBrate\-limit { ... };\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR all
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Per-zone RRL block (replaces the server-wide one).
.TP 8
\fBalerts { ... };\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR all (not database zones)
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Per-zone threshold alerts: \fBqps\-above\fR,
\fBservfail\-ratio\-above\fR, \fBrrl\-drops\-above\fR,
\fBtransfer\-failures\-above\fR; a name written here replaces the \fBoptions\fR
value for the zone (see \fBalerts\fR).
.TP 8
\fBecs\-tags { ... };\fR, \fBlocation\-tags { ... };\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR master, slave
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Per-zone tag definitions.
.TP 8
\fBecs\-trusted\-resolvers { ... };\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR master, slave
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Per-zone trusted ECS resolvers.
.TP 8
\fBprimary\-settings yes|no;\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR slave
.br
\fIDescription:\fR \fByes\fR (default): use the settings the primary sends with
the zone (Extended AXFR version 3: \fBallow\-transfer\fR,
\fBtransfer\-acl\-match\fR, \fBecs\-trusted\-resolvers\fR,
\fBadditional\-from\-auth\fR, \fBzone\-udp\-bufsize\fR); a statement written in
this \fBzone {}\fR still wins. \fBno\fR: ignore them. See "Secondary zones:
Extended AXFR version 3 and the settings of the primary".
.TP 8
\fBadditional\-from\-auth yes|in\-domain|no;\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR all
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Per-zone override of the \fBoptions\fR value.
.TP 8
\fBdisable\-auto\-tc\-flag yes|no;\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR program
.br
\fIDescription:\fR See PROGRAM ZONE PLUGINS. Any other value is an error.
.TP 8
\fBzone\-tcp\-mss\fR, \fBzone\-tcp\-window\fR, \fBzone\-tcp\-sndbuf\fR, \fBzone\-udp\-bufsize\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR all
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Per-zone transport settings; see TRANSPORT TUNING.
.TP 8
\fBalias\-resolver { <addr> [port <n>]; ... };\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR master, slave
.br
\fIDescription:\fR The resolvers that expand the ALIAS records of this zone;
replaces the \fBoptions\fR value. See POWERDNS ALIAS AND LUA RECORDS.
.TP 8
\fBforwarders [ port <n> ] { <addr> [port <n>]; ... };\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR forward
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Upstream servers; see FORWARD ZONES. An address without a
port takes the \fBport\fR written before the \fB{\fR, else \fBoptions { port
}\fR, else 53 (BIND 9.20 \fBbin/named/server.c\fR \fBconfigure_forward()\fR); an
address with its own port keeps it.
.TP 8
\fBforward\-timeout <ms>;\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR forward
.br
\fIDescription:\fR Total time budget in milliseconds (default 2000), shared by
the forwarders: each one gets the remaining time divided by the number of
forwarders not tried yet.
.TP 8
\fBprogram "<path>";\fR, \fBprogram\-args { "<arg>"; ... };\fR, \fBprogram\-user "<user>";\fR, \fBprogram\-timeout <ms>;\fR, \fBprogram\-max\-failures <n>;\fR, \fBprogram\-reload\-line yes|no;\fR
\fIApplies to:\fR program
.br
\fIDescription:\fR See PROGRAM ZONE PLUGINS.
.SH TRANSPORT TUNING (TCP MSS / WINDOW, UDP PAYLOAD SIZE)
These directives correspond to \fBdag\fR's \fB+tcp\-mss\fR, \fB+tcp\-window\fR
and \fB+bufsize\fR. Each has a server-wide form in \fBoptions {}\fR and a
per-zone override in \fBzone {}\fR. All of them default to "not set", which
keeps the previous behaviour (OS defaults for TCP, a 1232-byte UDP payload cap).
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
options {
    tcp\-mss 1220;          # 536..65495
    tcp\-window 256K;       # 4K..64M, K/M suffix allowed
    udp\-bufsize 1232;      # 512..4096
};
\&
zone "example.com" {
    type master;
    file "/usr/local/etc/namedb/master/example.com.zone";
    zone\-tcp\-mss 1200;     # 536..65495
    zone\-tcp\-window 128K;  # 4K..64M (receive side, SO_RCVBUF)
    zone\-tcp\-sndbuf 2M;    # 4K..64M (send side, SO_SNDBUF; e.g. faster AXFR)
    zone\-udp\-bufsize 1400; # 512..4096
};
.fi
.RE
.PP
Out-of-range or malformed values (\fB1k\fR for an MSS, \fB2MB\fR,
\fB65M\fR, ...) are rejected at load time instead of being clamped, so a tuning
setting can never be silently ignored.
.TP 8
\fBtcp\-window\fR
\fIApplied to:\fR \fBSO_RCVBUF\fR and \fBSO_SNDBUF\fR of every TCP listener
.br
\fIWhen:\fR before \fBlisten()\fR
.br
\fIEffect:\fR Inherited by accepted connections, so it covers the initial window
advertised in the SYN-ACK. \fBNeeds a restart\fR: listeners are not recreated on
reload. Also the default for secondary-zone transfers (see below).
.TP 8
\fBtcp\-mss\fR
\fIApplied to:\fR \fBTCP_MAXSEG\fR of each accepted connection
.br
\fIWhen:\fR right after \fBaccept()\fR
.br
\fIEffect:\fR Lowers the MSS KariDNS \fBsends\fR with. Picked up on reload by
new connections.
.TP 8
\fBzone\-tcp\-mss\fR
\fIApplied to:\fR \fBTCP_MAXSEG\fR of the connection
.br
\fIWhen:\fR after a query for the zone is read
.br
\fIEffect:\fR Same as \fBtcp\-mss\fR, and can only lower the MSS further (see
limits).
.TP 8
\fBzone\-tcp\-window\fR
\fIApplied to:\fR \fBSO_RCVBUF\fR of the connection
.br
\fIWhen:\fR after a query for the zone is read
.br
\fIEffect:\fR Receive window, within the window scale already agreed in the
handshake.
.TP 8
\fBzone\-tcp\-sndbuf\fR
\fIApplied to:\fR \fBSO_SNDBUF\fR of the connection
.br
\fIWhen:\fR after a query for the zone is read
.br
\fIEffect:\fR Send buffer; the setting that matters for large responses and
AXFR/IXFR. Applied before the transfer thread takes over the connection.
.TP 8
\fBudp\-bufsize\fR / \fBzone\-udp\-bufsize\fR
\fIApplied to:\fR UDP response size cap and the payload size advertised in the
response OPT
.br
\fIWhen:\fR per query
.br
\fIEffect:\fR Replaces the built-in 1232. The requestor's own EDNS payload size
still wins when it is smaller; TCP responses are not affected.
.SS Limits of the per-zone TCP settings
A DNS server only learns which zone a TCP connection is for once it has read the
query, i.e. after the three-way handshake. The \fBzone\-tcp\-*\fR settings are
therefore applied with \fBsetsockopt()\fR to the already established connection,
which means:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBMSS can only go down.\fR On FreeBSD, \fBTCP_MAXSEG\fR on an established
connection accepts only values at or below the current MSS (larger values fail
with \fBEINVAL\fR and are ignored), and the MSS the client was told in the
SYN-ACK does not change. \fBzone\-tcp\-mss\fR therefore only shrinks the
segments KariDNS sends, and once lowered the value stays for the rest of the
connection. If \fBtcp\-mss\fR and \fBzone\-tcp\-mss\fR (or two zones queried
over one connection) disagree, the smallest value wins. The SYN-ACK MSS itself
comes from the route MTU; to change it, use the route MTU or a packet-filter MSS
clamp (for example pf \fBscrub max\-mss\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBThe window scale is fixed by the handshake.\fR \fBzone\-tcp\-window\fR can
resize the receive buffer only within that scale. Queries are small, so for a
DNS server the send side (\fBzone\-tcp\-sndbuf\fR) is usually the one worth
tuning.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSetting a buffer turns off the kernel's automatic sizing\fR for that socket
(FreeBSD \fBnet.inet.tcp.sendbuf_auto\fR / \fBrecvbuf_auto\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSeveral zones on one connection.\fR With \fBtcp\-connection\-reuse\fR, a
client can query more than one zone over the same connection. The buffers are
switched per query, and only when the value changes. A query for a zone without
\fBzone\-tcp\-window\fR / \fBzone\-tcp\-sndbuf\fR puts back the value the socket
had before any zone setting was applied (that is, the \fBtcp\-window\fR value or
the OS default).
.IP \(bu 2
Values above \fBkern.ipc.maxsockbuf\fR are truncated by the kernel;
\fBtcp\-window\fR logs a warning when that happens.
.PP
When no zone uses \fBzone\-tcp\-*\fR, the TCP query path does no extra zone
lookup and no \fBsetsockopt()\fR calls.
.SS Secondary zones: transfers from the primary
For a \fBtype slave;\fR / \fBtype secondary;\fR zone, KariDNS is the TCP
\fIclient\fR of the zone transfer, and the connection to the primary is opened
by the privilege-separated connect broker. The same settings apply there, taken
from the zone and falling back to the server-wide values (catalog member zones
have no \fBzone {}\fR block and use the server-wide values):
.PP
.TS
lb lbw(27) lbw(28)
l lw(27) lw(28).
\&Socket option	\&Value used	\&When
_
\&\fBSO_RCVBUF\fR	T{
\fBzone\-tcp\-window\fR, else \fBtcp\-window\fR
T}	T{
before \fBconnect()\fR, so it covers the window advertised in the SYN; this is
the one that speeds up receiving a large AXFR/IXFR
T}
\&\fBSO_SNDBUF\fR	T{
\fBzone\-tcp\-sndbuf\fR, else \fBtcp\-window\fR
T}	\&before \fBconnect()\fR
\&\fBTCP_MAXSEG\fR	\&\fBzone\-tcp\-mss\fR, else \fBtcp\-mss\fR	T{
after \fBconnect()\fR: lowers the send MSS only, for the same FreeBSD reason as
above
T}
.TE
.SS Secondary zones: Extended AXFR version 3 and the settings of the primary (milestone B8t-2)
Between two KariDNS servers the transfer carries more than the RRs (EDNS option
65153, see "Extended AXFR" in
\fBKariDNS_How_to_use_ECS_and_location.md\fR). Version 3 adds two things, both
in private-use records of CLASS 65302 (RFC 6895 \[u00A7]3).
.PP
\fBVersions.\fR The secondary asks for version 3. A primary answers version 2
and 3 with a stream of the version asked; a version 2 secondary of a version 3
primary therefore keeps working (it is refused only for a zone with several or
restricted SOA records, see "Several SOA records"), and a version 3 secondary of
a version 2 primary keeps its tags and tinydns attributes: a version 2 primary
does not know version 3 and answers a standard transfer, so the secondary stops
that transfer at its first message (nothing is installed), asks again with
version 2 and uses the version 2 stream (no \fBSOA_SET\fR, no settings). It
remembers per zone, in memory, that the primary speaks version 2 and asks for
version 2 directly from then on; after 6 hours (\fBXFR_EXT_REPROBE_SECS\fR) it
asks for version 3 again, so an upgraded primary is found. A primary that
answers a standard transfer to version 2 as well (not KariDNS) costs one extra
request and is then remembered the same way (version 3 asked, a standard answer
accepted at once, re-probed after 6 hours). Upgrading the secondaries first
therefore does not degrade service. Any other version, or a request without the
option, is a standard transfer. A version 3 stream of a plain zone (one
unrestricted SOA) differs from the version 2 stream by the version in the OPT,
the settings record, and by sending the trusted resolvers of the
\fIconfiguration\fR in the settings instead of as \fBECS_TRUSTED\fR (65407); the
zone data is the same.
.PP
\fBThe SOA set.\fR For a zone with several or restricted apex SOA records (see
"Several SOA records") the stream is: the reference SOA (frame), \fBSOA_SET\fR
(65409: flags, number of apex SOA records), \fBSETTINGS\fR, the definitions,
then every record in file order with each apex SOA as a TINYDNS_WRAP (65406)
record that carries its attributes (and the \fBLOC_STATE\fR / \fBECS_STATE\fR
records of its tags), the resetting state records, the frame. The secondary does
not store the frame as data, takes its serial and timers from it, and ends up
with exactly the SOA set of the primary; a stream that announces another number
of SOA records, has a SOA of another serial or not at the apex, or puts
\fBSOA_SET\fR anywhere but right after the opening SOA is refused. A change of
the SOA set or of a SOA attribute reaches a secondary with a full transfer (an
IXFR request is answered with an AXFR for such a zone, and also when an earlier
version of it had a SOA set).
.PP
\fBThe settings of the primary.\fR The primary sends one \fBSETTINGS\fR record
(65408): the zone's effective settings as text in the syntax of a \fBzone {}\fR
block, the statements of the restricted parser of "Settings of database
zones". The statements sent, each with the value the primary applies (the zone's
own, else \fBoptions\fR'):
.PP
.TS
lbw(35) lbw(36)
lw(35) lw(36).
\&Statement	\&Sent when
_
T{
\fBallow\-transfer { <addresses> key "<name>"; ... };\fR and
\fBtransfer\-acl\-match\fR
T}	T{
the zone has an \fBallow\-transfer\fR (or a \fBtsig\-key\fR, which on a primary
may also transfer): the addresses (with their \fB!\fR), the key \fInames\fR
T}
\&\fBecs\-trusted\-resolvers { ... };\fR	T{
the zone or \fBoptions\fR has the list and the zone's own data has none
T}
T{
\fBadditional\-from\-auth\fR, \fBzone\-udp\-bufsize\fR
T}	\&the zone or \fBoptions\fR sets them
.TE
.PP
Only the zone's own \fBallow\-transfer\fR is sent: the one of its view or of
\fBoptions\fR is not (the secondary has its own). A zone's list that has a
negated key (\fB!key "k"\fR) is sent as \fBallow\-transfer { none; };\fR,
because the flat form cannot say it. Never sent: \fBacl\-ceiling\fR, TSIG
secrets (only key names), \fBmasters\fR, \fBalso\-notify\fR,
\fBallow\-update\fR, \fBallow\-query\fR or any other server-level policy. The
ACLs of a \fIdatabase\fR zone are cut at the primary's \fBacl\-ceiling\fR before
they are sent: an address or prefix that is not entirely inside the ceiling and
a key that the ceiling does not name are left out, and a database zone's ACL
without a configured ceiling is sent as \fBnone\fR (the secondary must never get
a wider list than the primary applies). A text longer than 32,768 octets is not
sent (logged). The same record is sent in an IXFR (right after the opening SOA)
and in an AXFR, and a secondary that serves the zone to others sends the
settings in force on it. The tag definitions of the configuration
(\fBlocation\-tags\fR / \fBecs\-tags\fR of the zone, else \fBoptions\fR) that
the records of the zone use are sent as tag definitions (65403 / 65404) when the
zone data defines no tags of its own; the secondary then resolves tags as the
primary does and needs no \fBlocation\-tags\fR of its own for the zone.
.PP
\fBOn the secondary.\fR The text is parsed with the restricted parser
(\fBfile\fR, \fBtype\fR, \fBinclude\fR, unknown statements are errors; a text
that does not parse is dropped with a warning), and merged with the zone as the
secondary has it: \fBa statement written in the secondary's own \fBzone {}\fB >
the received statement > the secondary's \fBoptions\fB\fR. Statements the
secondary does not take from the primary (\fBmasters\fR, \fBtsig\-key\fR,
\fBalso\-notify\fR, ...) are parsed and ignored. A TSIG key name in the received
\fBallow\-transfer\fR is valid only if the secondary defines that key; otherwise
that ACL fails closed (\fBnone\fR, logged and shown by \fBzonestatus\fR) while
the rest of the settings apply. \fBprimary\-settings no;\fR in the zone ignores
the received settings altogether. The received settings are replaced by every
successful transfer (a transfer without a \fBSETTINGS\fR record clears them),
are kept when a refresh fails or the primary is down, follow a changed
\fBkaridns.conf\fR (the secondary's own statements are re-read), and live in
memory only, like the data; a catalog member zone and a database secondary use
them in the same way (for a database zone the settings of its database row take
the place of the \fBzone {}\fR block). \fBkarictl zonestatus\fR shows
\fBsettings=primary\fR while received settings are in force. A change of the
settings alone (no new serial) reaches the secondary with its next transfer.
.PP
.RS 4
\fBWarning:\fR Over an unsigned transfer a path attacker (or anybody who can
answer instead of the primary) can change the ACLs of the secondary: widen
\fBallow\-transfer\fR, add a trusted ECS resolver. Sign the transfer with a TSIG
key (\fBtsig\-key\fR in the secondary's zone, \fBallow\-transfer { key ...; }\fR
on the primary) whenever the settings are accepted, or write the statements that
matter in the secondary's own \fBzone {}\fR (they win) or set
\fBprimary\-settings no;\fR. Since 0.7 a secondary \fBzone {}\fR with
\fBprimary\-settings yes\fR (the default) and a primary whose transfer is not
signed is reported by \fBkaricheck conf\fR
(\fBconf.zone.primary\-settings\-unsigned\fR, with the place of the
\fBprimaries\fR statement) and logged at warning (\fBconfig\fR) when the server
reads the configuration. The key is the one the transfer uses: the \fBkey\fR of
the \fBprimaries\fR entry (or of its list), else the \fBkeys\fR of the
\fBserver\fR statement that covers the primary's address (the view's own
\fBserver\fR statements when it has any, else the top-level ones), else the
zone's \fBtsig\-key\fR; a primary for which none of them names a key is
reported.
.RE
.SS Forward and program zones: not applied upstream
The TCP connection a \fBtype forward;\fR zone opens to its forwarder (the
fallback after a truncated UDP answer) does not use \fBzone\-tcp\-*\fR /
\fBtcp\-mss\fR / \fBtcp\-window\fR; it keeps the OS defaults. The right values
for that connection depend on the forwarder and the path to it, not on the
zone's settings. A \fBtype program;\fR zone talks to its program over a pipe, so
there is no TCP connection to tune. In both cases the settings still apply to
the client's connection to KariDNS, like for any other zone.
.PP
Per-zone UDP \fIsocket\fR buffers are not possible: all zones share the same UDP
sockets. The server-wide \fBudp\-recvbuf\-size\fR / \fBudp\-sndbuf\-size\fR stay
the knobs for those. What can be set per zone on UDP is the EDNS payload size
(\fBzone\-udp\-bufsize\fR). Values above 1232 risk IP fragmentation on paths
with a smaller MTU (this is the reason for the DNS Flag Day 2020 default); lower
values push more answers to TCP.
.SH SEE ALSO
karidns(8), karicheck(8), karictl(8)
